export default {"id": "microsoft/vscode", "owner": "microsoft", "repo": "vscode", "description": "Visual Studio Code — open-source code editor supporting debugging, task running, and version control", "stars": "184.7k", "repositoryReadme": "https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/main/README.md", "sample": true, "lastIndexed": "2026-11-22T18:21:29.303Z", "commit": "f31439d", "generatedBy": "claude-haiku-4-5 (claude · high)", "source": {"type": "github", "url": "https://github.com/microsoft/vscode", "commit": "f31439d32be99bcc503fcbad0e2b075e189ca9d9"}, "pages": [{"path": "overview.md", "title": "Project Overview", "parent": null, "description": "Visual Studio Code - OSS is a cross-platform code editor with integrated development features built on Electron and TypeScript. This page covers the project structure, tech stack, and architectural foundation.", "files": ["README.md", "CONTRIBUTING.md", "AGENTS.md", "package.json", ".gitignore"], "markdown": "# Project Overview\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [README.md](README.md)\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md)\n- [package.json](package.json)\n- [.gitignore](.gitignore)\n</cite>\n\n## What is VS Code\n\nVisual Studio Code (Code - OSS) is a cross-platform source code editor built on Electron and TypeScript. It combines simplicity with core development features including code editing, navigation, debugging, and a rich extension ecosystem. The open-source codebase, maintained by Microsoft, is released under the MIT license and updated monthly with new features and bug fixes across Windows, macOS, and Linux platforms.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [README.md:L9-L19](README.md#L9-L19)\n\n## Technology Stack\n\nThe project is built with modern web and systems technologies. The main application runs on **Electron 43.8.10** (built from source) with **TypeScript** as the primary language. The package manager is **pnpm** (version 12.16.0 pinned in `package.json`), which all contributors must use—npm and yarn are not supported.\n\nDependencies include the Anthropic SDK (`@anthropic-ai/sdk`), Microsoft's development tunnel libraries for remote development features, xterm for terminal emulation, and various VS Code-specific packages. The build system uses **Gulp** and **rspack** for bundling, with Playwright for end-to-end testing and Mocha for unit tests. A Rust-based CLI component (in `cli/`) handles command-line operations and tunneling protocols.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [package.json:L2-L12](package.json#L2-L12)\n- [package.json:L113-L180](package.json#L113-L180)\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L97-L101](CONTRIBUTING.md#L97-L101)\n\n## Project Architecture\n\nThe codebase is organized into major subsystems:\n\n| Component | Purpose | Location |\n|-----------|---------|----------|\n| **Main Application** | Core editor UI and workbench | `src/vs/` |\n| **Extensions** | Built-in language support, grammars, snippets | `extensions/` |\n| **Build System** | Compilation, bundling, distribution | `build/` |\n| **CLI** | Command-line interface and remote access (Rust) | `cli/` |\n| **Copilot Integration** | AI assistant shim layer | `copilot_shim/` |\n\nThe main entry point is `out/main.js`. The `extensions/` directory contains bundled language extensions (JSON, TypeScript, Python, etc.) alongside infrastructure extensions for configuration editing and Copilot. The build system handles compilation of TypeScript to JavaScript, extension media processing, and native module binding via Electron.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [README.md:L49-L56](README.md#L49-L56)\n- [package.json:L17-L285](package.json#L17-L285)\n\n## Extension System\n\nVS Code includes a set of built-in extensions providing language grammars, snippets, and rich IDE features. Extensions with the `-language-features` suffix provide advanced support (Go to Definition, inline suggestions) beyond syntax highlighting. Each language typically has two extensions: a base extension for coloring and snippets, and a language-features extension for semantic functionality. This modular design keeps the core lean while enabling rich experiences for supported languages.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [README.md:L53-L56](README.md#L53-L56)\n\n## Development Environment\n\nThe repository includes a Visual Studio Code Dev Container configuration for isolated development. Dev Containers or GitHub Codespaces environments require **at least 4 cores and 6 GB of RAM** (8 GB recommended) for a full build. \n\nThe build process uses multiple npm scripts: `compile` for TypeScript compilation, `watch` for continuous development, `test-node` and `test-browser` for unit testing, and `smoketest` for integration testing. The `build-fast` script enables rapid iteration during development. Performance and profiling tools are available via `perf` and related scripts for detecting regressions.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [README.md:L57-L66](README.md#L57-L66)\n- [package.json:L20-L102](package.json#L20-L102)\n\n## Open Source & Community\n\nCode - OSS is developed openly with the community. The project maintains a public roadmap, monthly iteration plans, and endgame schedules on the GitHub wiki. Contributors can submit bugs, feature requests, and pull requests; the repository includes GitHub Actions for automated issue triage and management. The codebase follows coding guidelines documented in the wiki, with the CONTRIBUTING.md file providing detailed contribution workflows. All work uses pnpm for dependency management to ensure reproducible builds across contributors and CI environments.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [README.md:L5-L7](README.md#L5-L7)\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L1-L6](CONTRIBUTING.md#L1-L6)\n"}, {"path": "glossary.md", "title": "Glossary", "parent": null, "description": "Key terminology, acronyms, and domain concepts used throughout the VS Code codebase.", "files": ["README.md", "CONTRIBUTING.md"], "markdown": "# Glossary\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [README.md](README.md)\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md)\n</cite>\n\n## Repository and Distribution\n\n**Code - OSS** — The open-source repository where Visual Studio Code is developed. This is the main codebase maintained by Microsoft and the community, available under the MIT license. The repository contains source code, extensions, development tools, and project documentation.\n\n**Visual Studio Code** — The commercial product distribution built from the Code - OSS repository with Microsoft-specific customizations, released under a traditional Microsoft product license. While Code - OSS is the base, Visual Studio Code includes additional proprietary features and branding.\n\n**Insiders build** — A daily release channel of Visual Studio Code that provides the latest features and fixes before the monthly stable release. Users can install the Insiders build to test upcoming functionality and provide early feedback.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [README.md:L1-L19](README.md#L1-L19)\n\n## Development and Contribution Workflow\n\n**Bug** — A reproducible problem or defect in VS Code that prevents expected functionality. Bug reports should include version information, operating system, reproducible steps, expected versus actual behavior, and relevant error messages from the Dev Tools Console.\n\n**Feature request** — A proposal for new functionality or enhancement to VS Code. Feature requests are categorized separately from bugs in the issue tracking system and are prioritized based on community reactions and strategic alignment.\n\n**Issue tracking** — The workflow system used to manage bugs, feature requests, and other project items. After submission, issues enter an automated management pipeline that handles triage, labeling, and closure workflows.\n\n**Pull request** — A mechanism for proposing code changes to the repository. Contributors create pull requests with their changes, which are then reviewed, discussed, and merged into the codebase following project guidelines.\n\n**Extension** — A modular component that extends VS Code functionality. Extensions can provide language support, themes, debugging capabilities, and other features. Users can disable extensions to isolate issues, and third-party extension bugs should be reported to the extension's repository rather than the main VS Code repository.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L20-L96](CONTRIBUTING.md#L20-L96)\n\n## Development Environment and Tools\n\n**Dev Containers** — A standardized development environment using Docker containers that can be cloned into a container volume for improved disk I/O performance on macOS and Windows. The Dev Containers extension enables the workflow with the command \"Dev Containers: Clone Repository in Container Volume\".\n\n**GitHub Codespaces** — A cloud-based development environment integrated with GitHub. Users can create new Codespaces through the GitHub Codespaces extension in VS Code for remote development without local setup.\n\n**pnpm** — The package manager used by the VS Code project for installing dependencies. Version 11 or newer is required; pnpm automatically downloads and uses the pinned version specified in `package.json` via the `packageManager` field. Installation with npm or yarn is not supported.\n\n**Dev Tools Console** — The developer tools console accessible via Help > Toggle Developer Tools menu, used to view error messages and debugging information when troubleshooting VS Code issues.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [README.md:L57-L66](README.md#L57-L66)\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L97-L101](CONTRIBUTING.md#L97-L101)\n\n## Extensions and Language Support\n\n**Bundled extensions** — Built-in extensions included with VS Code located in the `extensions` folder, providing grammars and snippets for many programming languages. These extensions are part of the core distribution rather than optional add-ons.\n\n**Language-features extension** — A bundled extension providing rich language support capabilities such as inline suggestions, Go to Definition, and other advanced features for a specific language. These extensions follow a naming convention with the suffix `-language-features`, such as `json-language-features` for JSON support.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [README.md:L53-L55](README.md#L53-L55)\n"}, {"path": "architecture/process-model.md", "title": "Process and Runtime Model", "parent": "architecture/architecture.md", "description": "Details on VS Code's multi-process architecture including the main process, renderer processes, and inter-process communication.", "files": ["copilot_shim/build.rs", "copilot_shim/src/app.rs", "copilot_shim/src/candidate.rs", "copilot_shim/src/command.rs"], "markdown": "# Process and Runtime Model\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [copilot_shim/build.rs](copilot_shim/build.rs)\n- [copilot_shim/src/app.rs](copilot_shim/src/app.rs)\n- [copilot_shim/src/candidate.rs](copilot_shim/src/candidate.rs)\n- [copilot_shim/src/command.rs](copilot_shim/src/command.rs)\n</cite>\n\n## Shim Architecture\n\nThe Copilot CLI shim is a launcher application that locates and executes the Copilot CLI binary. It operates across Windows, macOS, and Linux, adapting to platform-specific executable formats and interpreter requirements. The shim's primary responsibility is candidate discovery and process supervision, ensuring the correct CLI version is launched with proper arguments forwarded.\n\nThe shim embeds versioning metadata for Windows environments. During compilation on Windows with the MSVC target, `copilot_shim/build.rs` generates a version resource in `.res` format that the linker embeds directly in `copilot.exe`. This resource stores the file version as four 16-bit integers (major, minor, patch, and reserved fields), allowing setup tools to compare and replace shimmed versions during installation without parsing executable bytes.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [copilot_shim/build.rs:L6-L48](copilot_shim/build.rs#L6-L48)\n- [copilot_shim/src/app.rs:L1-L60](copilot_shim/src/app.rs#L1-L60)\n\n## Candidate Discovery and Filtering\n\nThe discovery process identifies potential Copilot CLI executables in PATH directories. On Windows, the shim searches for `copilot.exe`, `copilot.cmd`, `copilot.bat`, and `copilot.ps1` in order. On Unix systems, it searches for `copilot` only. Each discovered file is inspected to verify it is a regular file and, on Unix, executable.\n\nDiscovery excludes three classes of candidates: the currently running shim itself (identified by file identity comparison), other copies of the Rust shim (identified by an embedded marker), and legacy script shims from earlier VS Code versions (identified by shebang patterns). Candidates up to 16 MB are read to check for the marker `VSCODE_COPILOT_RUST_SHIM_V1`; larger files are assumed to be the actual CLI and not read.\n\nThe discovery system collects diagnostics for each excluded candidate and any errors encountered during metadata inspection or file reading, allowing callers to report why specific candidates were rejected.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [copilot_shim/src/candidate.rs:L17-L150](copilot_shim/src/candidate.rs#L17-L150)\n- [copilot_shim/src/candidate.rs:L230-L290](copilot_shim/src/candidate.rs#L230-L290)\n\n## Candidate Resolution and Interpreter Selection\n\nDiscovered candidates are resolved to executable types: Unix executables run directly, Windows executables run directly, and scripts require an interpreter. Script shims on Windows require either `cmd.exe` (via the `ComSpec` environment variable) or PowerShell 5.1 / 7.3+.\n\nThe shim locates interpreters on demand only when a candidate of that type is encountered. PowerShell discovery probes both modern versions (`pwsh.exe` 7.3+) and Windows PowerShell 5.1 (`powershell.exe`), preferring the modern version. Version detection runs PowerShell with `-NoLogo -NoProfile -NonInteractive` and parses `$PSVersionTable.PSVersion.ToString()`. If neither version is available, the candidate is marked unusable.\n\nCommand shells are located via the `ComSpec` environment variable and validated to be regular executable files. Resolution fails early with specific error types (`MissingCommandShell`, `MissingPowerShellHost`) if required interpreters are unavailable, allowing the application to report clear diagnostic messages.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [copilot_shim/src/command.rs:L18-L118](copilot_shim/src/command.rs#L18-L118)\n- [copilot_shim/src/app.rs:L92-L177](copilot_shim/src/app.rs#L92-L177)\n\n## Command Building and Argument Encoding\n\nOnce a candidate is resolved, the shim constructs a `CommandSpec` that specifies the executable path and arguments. For native executables, arguments are passed as-is. For Windows command scripts, arguments are encoded into a single command line tail with proper quoting rules: cmd metacharacters (`&|<>()^%!;`) are wrapped in quotes to prevent interpretation by the shell parser.\n\nPowerShell scripts are launched with standard suppressions (`-NoLogo -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File`) followed by the script path and forwarded arguments. This encoding ensures special characters in arguments are preserved through shell parsing without requiring escaping at the caller level.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [copilot_shim/src/command.rs:L66-L118](copilot_shim/src/command.rs#L66-L118)\n\n## Process Supervision and Termination Handling\n\nThe shim supervises candidate execution through a `supervise` method that accepts a `CommandSpec` and `SupervisionMode`. Supervision modes include:\n\n- `CapturedVersionProbe`: Launches a process with output capture and enforces size/time limits for quick version detection.\n- `FinalInteractiveCli`: Launches the actual CLI as an interactive foreground process, with output streamed to the terminal.\n\nProcess outcomes report the termination mode:\n- `NumericExit(code)`: Process exited with a numeric code.\n- `UnixSignal(signal)`: On Unix, process terminated from a signal (mapped to exit code as `128 + signal`).\n- `HandledCancellation`: User cancelled the operation (reported as exit code 130).\n\nErrors during supervision report specific failure types: timeout, output limit exceeded, spawn failure (with the program path and OS error), or supervision operation failure (with the operation name, program, and error details). Cancellation is treated specially; it returns exit code 130, matching shell conventions for SIGINT.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [copilot_shim/src/app.rs:L199-L227](copilot_shim/src/app.rs#L199-L227)\n- [copilot_shim/src/app.rs:L429-L467](copilot_shim/src/app.rs#L429-L467)\n\n## Workflow and Exit Codes\n\nThe shim follows a discovery-resolution-launch workflow. If no usable candidate is found in PATH, it offers an interactive install. On macOS and Linux, it also checks the official install directory after PATH. Installation happens only with user consent in interactive terminals, returning exit codes that indicate the installation outcome (success, cancellation, or policy rejection).\n\nExit codes signal distinct outcomes:\n- `0`: Success (candidate launched successfully or install completed).\n- `1`: Internal failure (discovery failed, resolution failed, or install failed).\n- `127`: CLI not found and not installed (Unix convention for command not found).\n- `128 + signal`: Unix process terminated by signal.\n- `130`: User cancelled (Ctrl+C, matching shell conventions).\n- Policy-specific codes: When policy disables CLI installation, a policy-specific exit code is returned so setup scripts can detect the policy reason.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [copilot_shim/src/app.rs:L40-L80](copilot_shim/src/app.rs#L40-L80)\n- [copilot_shim/src/app.rs:L296-L342](copilot_shim/src/app.rs#L296-L342)\n"}, {"path": "architecture/extension-system.md", "title": "Extension System", "parent": "architecture/architecture.md", "description": "Architecture of the extension system including discovery, loading, activation, and the extension host process model.", "files": ["extensions/CONTRIBUTING.md", "extensions/css-language-features/client/src/browser/cssClientMain.ts", "extensions/css-language-features/client/src/cssClient.ts", "extensions/css-language-features/client/src/customData.ts", "extensions/css-language-features/client/src/dropOrPaste/dropOrPasteResource.ts", "extensions/css-language-features/client/src/dropOrPaste/shared.ts", "extensions/css-language-features/client/src/dropOrPaste/uriList.ts", "extensions/css-language-features/client/src/node/cssClientMain.ts", "extensions/css-language-features/client/src/node/nodeFs.ts", "extensions/css-language-features/client/src/requests.ts", "extensions/css-language-features/esbuild.browser.mts", "extensions/css-language-features/esbuild.mts", "extensions/css-language-features/server/src/browser/cssServerMain.ts", "extensions/css-language-features/server/src/browser/cssServerWorkerMain.ts", "extensions/css-language-features/server/src/cssServer.ts", "extensions/css-language-features/server/src/customData.ts", "extensions/css-language-features/server/src/languageModelCache.ts", "extensions/css-language-features/server/src/node/cssServerMain.ts", "extensions/css-language-features/server/src/node/cssServerNodeMain.ts", "extensions/css-language-features/server/src/node/nodeFs.ts", "extensions/css-language-features/server/src/requests.ts"], "markdown": "# Extension System\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [extensions/CONTRIBUTING.md](extensions/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [extensions/css-language-features/client/src/browser/cssClientMain.ts](extensions/css-language-features/client/src/browser/cssClientMain.ts)\n- [extensions/css-language-features/client/src/cssClient.ts](extensions/css-language-features/client/src/cssClient.ts)\n- [extensions/css-language-features/client/src/customData.ts](extensions/css-language-features/client/src/customData.ts)\n- [extensions/css-language-features/client/src/dropOrPaste/dropOrPasteResource.ts](extensions/css-language-features/client/src/dropOrPaste/dropOrPasteResource.ts)\n- [extensions/css-language-features/client/src/dropOrPaste/shared.ts](extensions/css-language-features/client/src/dropOrPaste/shared.ts)\n- [extensions/css-language-features/client/src/dropOrPaste/uriList.ts](extensions/css-language-features/client/src/dropOrPaste/uriList.ts)\n- [extensions/css-language-features/client/src/node/cssClientMain.ts](extensions/css-language-features/client/src/node/cssClientMain.ts)\n- [extensions/css-language-features/client/src/node/nodeFs.ts](extensions/css-language-features/client/src/node/nodeFs.ts)\n- [extensions/css-language-features/client/src/requests.ts](extensions/css-language-features/client/src/requests.ts)\n- [extensions/css-language-features/esbuild.browser.mts](extensions/css-language-features/esbuild.browser.mts)\n- [extensions/css-language-features/esbuild.mts](extensions/css-language-features/esbuild.mts)\n- [extensions/css-language-features/server/src/browser/cssServerMain.ts](extensions/css-language-features/server/src/browser/cssServerMain.ts)\n- [extensions/css-language-features/server/src/browser/cssServerWorkerMain.ts](extensions/css-language-features/server/src/browser/cssServerWorkerMain.ts)\n- [extensions/css-language-features/server/src/cssServer.ts](extensions/css-language-features/server/src/cssServer.ts)\n- [extensions/css-language-features/server/src/customData.ts](extensions/css-language-features/server/src/customData.ts)\n- [extensions/css-language-features/server/src/languageModelCache.ts](extensions/css-language-features/server/src/languageModelCache.ts)\n- [extensions/css-language-features/server/src/node/cssServerMain.ts](extensions/css-language-features/server/src/node/cssServerMain.ts)\n- [extensions/css-language-features/server/src/node/cssServerNodeMain.ts](extensions/css-language-features/server/src/node/cssServerNodeMain.ts)\n- [extensions/css-language-features/server/src/node/nodeFs.ts](extensions/css-language-features/server/src/node/nodeFs.ts)\n- [extensions/css-language-features/server/src/requests.ts](extensions/css-language-features/server/src/requests.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## Extension Structure and Manifests\n\nVS Code extensions are organized in the `extensions/` directory. Each TypeScript-based built-in extension follows a standardized structure defined in `extensions/CONTRIBUTING.md`. A typical extension contains:\n\n- `package.json`: The extension manifest declaring metadata and contribution points\n- `src/`: TypeScript source code, organized by platform and runtime\n- `tsconfig.json`: Primary TypeScript configuration inheriting from `tsconfig.base.json`\n- `esbuild.mts`: Production build script for Node.js runtime\n- `.vscodeignore`: File exclusion list for packaging\n\nBuilt-in extensions produce output in two directories:\n- `out/`: Development builds\n- `dist/`: Production builds\n\nThe `package.json` manifest includes a `contributes` section that declares what the extension adds to VS Code. For example, extensions can contribute language features through entries like `css.customData` that are accessed via `extension.packageJSON?.contributes?.css?.customData`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/CONTRIBUTING.md:L1-L32](extensions/CONTRIBUTING.md#L1-L32)\n\n## Activation and Lifecycle\n\nExtensions follow a standard activation lifecycle with two entry points. The `activate` function is called when VS Code determines the extension should start. For Node.js-based extensions, this function receives an `ExtensionContext` parameter and may load a language server module from a computed path:\n\n```typescript\nexport async function activate(context: ExtensionContext) {\n\tconst clientMain = extensions.getExtension('vscode.css-language-features')?.packageJSON?.main || '';\n\tconst serverMain = `./server/${clientMain.indexOf('/dist/') !== -1 ? 'dist' : 'out'}/node/cssServerMain`;\n\tconst serverModule = context.asAbsolutePath(serverMain);\n\t// ... initialization code\n}\n```\n\nWhen the extension is no longer needed, the `deactivate` function performs cleanup, such as stopping language clients:\n\n```typescript\nexport async function deactivate(): Promise<void> {\n\tif (client) {\n\t\tawait client.stop();\n\t\tclient = undefined;\n\t}\n}\n```\n\nThe `ExtensionContext` provides runtime information including `extensionUri` (the root directory of the extension) and `subscriptions` (a disposal collection for cleanup).\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/css-language-features/client/src/node/cssClientMain.ts:L14-L48](extensions/css-language-features/client/src/node/cssClientMain.ts#L14-L48)\n- [extensions/css-language-features/client/src/cssClient.ts:L39-L39](extensions/css-language-features/client/src/cssClient.ts#L39-L39)\n\n## Multi-Platform Support\n\nVS Code extensions support both desktop and browser environments through distinct build configurations and entry points. For browser support, extensions add a `\"browser\"` field in `package.json` pointing to the browser bundle and create a `tsconfig.browser.json` for type-checking browser-safe sources.\n\nThe CSS language features extension demonstrates this pattern with separate implementations:\n- `src/node/cssClientMain.ts`: Desktop entry point using Node.js language client\n- `src/browser/cssClientMain.ts`: Browser entry point using Web Worker for the language server\n\n```typescript\n// Browser implementation\nconst serverMain = Uri.joinPath(context.extensionUri, 'server/dist/browser/cssServerMain.js');\nconst worker = new Worker(serverMain.toString());\nconst newLanguageClient: LanguageClientConstructor = (id: string, name: string, clientOptions: LanguageClientOptions) => {\n\treturn new LanguageClient(id, name, worker, clientOptions);\n};\n```\n\nBuild configuration ensures platform-specific code is compiled correctly. The `esbuild.browser.mts` and `esbuild.mts` scripts compile browser and desktop variants respectively, using `platform: 'browser'` or `platform: 'node'` to select the appropriate target.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/CONTRIBUTING.md:L21-L31](extensions/CONTRIBUTING.md#L21-L31)\n- [extensions/css-language-features/client/src/browser/cssClientMain.ts:L14-L31](extensions/css-language-features/client/src/browser/cssClientMain.ts#L14-L31)\n- [extensions/css-language-features/esbuild.browser.mts:L10-L36](extensions/css-language-features/esbuild.browser.mts#L10-L36)\n\n## Language Client-Server Architecture\n\nLanguage feature extensions often employ a client-server architecture where the main extension process (client) communicates with a separate language server over a protocol. The client creates a language server connection with specific options:\n\n```typescript\nconst clientOptions: LanguageClientOptions = {\n\tdocumentSelector: ['css', 'scss', 'less'],\n\tsynchronize: {\n\t\tconfigurationSection: ['css', 'scss', 'less']\n\t},\n\tinitializationOptions: {\n\t\thandledSchemas: ['file'],\n\t\tprovideFormatter: false,\n\t\tcustomCapabilities: { rangeFormatting: { editLimit: 10000 } }\n\t}\n};\n\nconst client = newLanguageClient('css', l10n.t('CSS Language Server'), clientOptions);\nawait client.start();\n```\n\nThe language server receives an `initialize` request with client capabilities and returns its own capabilities. For desktop environments, the server runs as a Node.js process via IPC transport. For browser environments, the server runs in a Web Worker.\n\nThe server implements a request-response pattern for language features (completion, hover, diagnostics, etc.) and can send notifications back to the client. The CSS server, for example, registers handlers for completion, hover, document symbols, definition, and many other language protocol requests.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/css-language-features/client/src/cssClient.ts:L50-L103](extensions/css-language-features/client/src/cssClient.ts#L50-L103)\n- [extensions/css-language-features/server/src/cssServer.ts:L37-L139](extensions/css-language-features/server/src/cssServer.ts#L37-L139)\n\n## Extension Discovery and Querying\n\nExtensions are discoverable at runtime through the VS Code API. The `extensions` namespace provides access to loaded extensions and change notifications:\n\n```typescript\nfor (const extension of extensions.all) {\n\tconst customData = extension.packageJSON?.contributes?.css?.customData;\n\tif (Array.isArray(customData)) {\n\t\tfor (const rp of customData) {\n\t\t\tdataPaths.push(Utils.joinPath(extension.extensionUri, rp).toString());\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n```\n\nExtensions can subscribe to `extensions.onDidChange` to react when other extensions are installed, updated, or uninstalled:\n\n```typescript\ntoDispose.push(extensions.onDidChange(_ => {\n\tconst newPathsInExtensions = getCustomDataPathsFromAllExtensions();\n\tif (newPathsInExtensions.length !== pathsInExtensions.length || \n\t\t!newPathsInExtensions.every((val, idx) => val === pathsInExtensions[idx])) {\n\t\tpathsInExtensions = newPathsInExtensions;\n\t\tonChange.fire();\n\t}\n}));\n```\n\nEach extension object provides access to its `packageJSON` manifest and `extensionUri` (the extension's installation directory).\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/css-language-features/client/src/customData.ts:L9-L36](extensions/css-language-features/client/src/customData.ts#L9-L36)\n\n## Resource Access and Subscriptions\n\nThe `ExtensionContext.subscriptions` array manages disposable resources that should be cleaned up when the extension deactivates. Extensions register providers, event listeners, and commands by pushing disposables to this array:\n\n```typescript\ncontext.subscriptions.push(registerDropOrPasteResourceSupport({ language: 'css', scheme: '*' }));\ncontext.subscriptions.push({ dispose: () => registration.provider?.dispose() });\ncontext.subscriptions.push(workspace.onDidChangeConfiguration(e => \n\te.affectsConfiguration(registration.settingId) && updateFormatterRegistration(registration)\n));\n```\n\nExtensions access the workspace configuration to retrieve settings that influence behavior:\n\n```typescript\nconst formatEnabled = workspace.getConfiguration().get(registration.settingId);\n```\n\nSubscription resources are disposed in reverse order when the extension deactivates, ensuring proper cleanup of event listeners, providers, and language clients.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/css-language-features/client/src/cssClient.ts:L110-L120](extensions/css-language-features/client/src/cssClient.ts#L110-L120)\n"}, {"path": "architecture/workbench.md", "title": "Workbench Architecture", "parent": "architecture/architecture.md", "description": "The main UI container handling layout management, panels, editors, sidebars, and coordination of core services.", "files": ["eslint.config.js"], "markdown": "# Workbench Architecture\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [eslint.config.js](eslint.config.js)\n</cite>\n\n## Overview\n\nThe workbench is the main UI container for VS Code, located in `src/vs/workbench/**`. It coordinates layout management, panels, editors, sidebars, and integration with core platform services. The workbench is organized into browser-facing components, API layers, and feature contributions split across multiple domains including chat, debug, testing, extensions, and sessions.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [eslint.config.js:L123-L125](eslint.config.js#L123-L125)\n- [eslint.config.js:L383-L393](eslint.config.js#L383-L393)\n\n## Core Components\n\nThe main workbench entry point is `src/vs/workbench/browser/workbench.ts`, which serves as the primary UI container. Supporting UI parts are organized under `src/vs/workbench/browser/parts/`, including the pane composite bar (`paneCompositeBar.ts`) for managing sidebar panels and the title bar (`titlebar/titlebarPart.ts`) for window chrome.\n\nThe workbench browser layer handles the visual layout and user interaction logic for the entire application window. These components coordinate with services to render editors, panels, and sidebars according to the current workspace state.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [eslint.config.js:L308-L3010](eslint.config.js#L308-L3010)\n- [eslint.config.js:L306-L307](eslint.config.js#L306-L307)\n\n## API and Extension Integration\n\nThe workbench exposes APIs through `src/vs/workbench/api/browser/mainThread*` modules that bridge the extension host to the main process. These include `mainThreadChatSessions.ts` for chat session management, `mainThreadDebugService.ts` for debugging, and `mainThreadTesting.ts` for test integration. The API layer provides the public contract that extensions use to interact with workbench features.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [eslint.config.js:L289-L291](eslint.config.js#L289-L291)\n\n## Feature Contributions\n\nThe workbench organizes optional and core functionality as contributions under `src/vs/workbench/contrib/`. These include:\n\n- **Chat**: Conversational UI and agent session management\n- **Debug**: Debugging views and functionality\n- **Testing**: Test explorer and coverage reporting\n- **Extensions**: Extension marketplace and management\n- **Sessions**: Session and state management for persistent workspaces\n\nEach contribution module is responsible for registering its UI components, commands, and services with the workbench at startup.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [eslint.config.js:L310-L368](eslint.config.js#L310-L368)\n\n## Layering and Service Architecture\n\nThe workbench follows a strict layering model defined by the ESLint configuration, where the `browser` layer may import from `common` but not `node`. Core platform services—such as keybindings, instantiation, commands, and notifications—are shared across the workbench and accessed through dependency injection.\n\nServices in `src/vs/workbench/services/` provide runtime functionality for editors, file management, keybindings, extensions, and remote features. The workbench delegates to these services rather than implementing cross-cutting concerns itself.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [eslint.config.js:L27-L35](eslint.config.js#L27-L35)\n- [eslint.config.js:L108-L3010](eslint.config.js#L108-L3010)\n\n## Notifications and Common Infrastructure\n\nThe workbench's common layer (`src/vs/workbench/common/`) provides shared types and infrastructure not tied to the browser UI, including the notifications system (`notifications.ts`) that delivers user-facing messages and alerts across features.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [eslint.config.js:L309-L3010](eslint.config.js#L309-L3010)\n"}, {"path": "architecture/editor-core.md", "title": "Editor Core", "parent": "architecture/architecture.md", "description": "The text editor engine including rendering pipeline, model management, selection, and core editing operations.", "files": ["gulpfile.mjs"], "markdown": "# Editor Core\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [gulpfile.mjs](gulpfile.mjs)\n</cite>\n\n## Build System Architecture\n\nThe VS Code editor build system is orchestrated through a modern JavaScript-based configuration framework. The primary build entry point is [gulpfile.mjs](gulpfile.mjs#L1-L5), which establishes the foundation for compiling, packaging, and deploying the editor and its associated tools.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [gulpfile.mjs:L1-L5](gulpfile.mjs#L1-L5)\n\n## Module Import Structure\n\nThe gulpfile.mjs configuration file implements a modular architecture by importing build rules from the TypeScript-based build system located in the `./build/` directory. This design pattern allows for a clean separation of concerns, where the entry point file remains lightweight while delegating actual build logic to a dedicated TypeScript module. The import statement establishes this critical connection between the build configuration and the implementation.\n\n## Build Configuration Framework\n\nVS Code's build infrastructure utilizes a JavaScript module system to organize build tasks and transformations. The gulpfile.mjs serves as the primary configuration file that build tools recognize and execute. This approach enables developers to define complex build pipelines, task dependencies, and execution strategies using JavaScript.\n\n## TypeScript Integration\n\nThe build system leverages TypeScript as the primary language for implementing build logic and compilation rules. By importing TypeScript modules directly from the gulpfile.mjs entry point, developers can write build code with full type checking, modern language features, and comprehensive error detection. This ensures that the build process itself maintains the same quality standards as the editor source code.\n\n## Editor Core Compilation\n\nThe build system processes the editor core source code and transforms it into deployable artifacts. The gulpfile configuration orchestrates this compilation pipeline, handling tasks such as transpilation, bundling, optimization, and artifact generation necessary for the editor to function within the VS Code application.\n\n## Project Licensing\n\nAll source code in the VS Code repository, including the build configuration files, is subject to the MIT License. The copyright header in gulpfile.mjs and throughout the project indicates that the work is maintained by Microsoft Corporation. The complete license terms and conditions are available in the License.txt file located in the project root directory.\n"}, {"path": "architecture/language-services.md", "title": "Language Services", "parent": "architecture/architecture.md", "description": "Infrastructure for language features including completion, diagnostics, definition navigation, and formatting.", "files": ["src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostLanguageFeatures.ts", "src/bootstrap-cli.ts", "src/bootstrap-fork.ts", "src/bootstrap-node.ts", "src/main.ts", "src/server-main.ts", "src/vs/code/browser/workbench/workbench.ts"], "markdown": "# Language Services\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostLanguageFeatures.ts](src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostLanguageFeatures.ts)\n- [src/bootstrap-cli.ts](src/bootstrap-cli.ts)\n- [src/bootstrap-fork.ts](src/bootstrap-fork.ts)\n- [src/bootstrap-node.ts](src/bootstrap-node.ts)\n- [src/main.ts](src/main.ts)\n- [src/server-main.ts](src/server-main.ts)\n- [src/vs/code/browser/workbench/workbench.ts](src/vs/code/browser/workbench/workbench.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## Overview\n\nLanguage Services in VS Code provide the infrastructure that powers editor features like IntelliSense, code navigation, diagnostics, and formatting. The language services layer sits between extension-provided language features and the editor's internal demand. This infrastructure translates between the VS Code extension API (the public `vscode` module) and the internal language services layer, enabling extensions to implement language-specific behavior without directly coupling to core editor types.\n\nThe core of this infrastructure resides in `src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostLanguageFeatures.ts`, which defines adapters that mediate between extension providers and the editor's demand-driven language services system. These adapters handle type conversion, resource lifecycle management, caching, and cancellation.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostLanguageFeatures.ts:L1-L41](src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostLanguageFeatures.ts#L1-L41)\n\n## Adapter Architecture\n\nLanguage service features in VS Code follow an adapter pattern. Each language feature (hover, completion, code action, etc.) has a corresponding adapter class that wraps an extension's provider implementation. The adapter's responsibilities include:\n\n- **Type conversion** between extension API types and internal language service types using the `typeConvert` module\n- **Document retrieval** from the extension host's document store (`ExtHostDocuments`)\n- **Cancellation handling** via `CancellationToken` to interrupt long-running operations\n- **Resource lifecycle management** through `DisposableStore` for cleanup of commands and other disposables\n- **Result caching** where needed for performance (CodeLens, CodeAction, Hover)\n- **Error handling and logging** through the extension telemetry and log services\n\nThe adapters expose methods matching the internal language services protocol, allowing the editor to request features uniformly regardless of which provider implements them. For example, `provideCodeLenses()` takes a resource URI and cancellation token, calls the extension's provider, and returns results in the internal format expected by the editor.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostLanguageFeatures.ts:L42-L105](src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostLanguageFeatures.ts#L42-L105)\n\n## Navigation Services\n\nVS Code provides four primary navigation adapters for jumping between related code locations:\n\n| Adapter | Method | Purpose |\n|---------|--------|---------|\n| DefinitionAdapter | `provideDefinition()` | Navigate to where a symbol is defined |\n| DeclarationAdapter | `provideDeclaration()` | Navigate to where a symbol is declared |\n| ImplementationAdapter | `provideImplementation()` | Navigate to implementations of an interface or base class |\n| TypeDefinitionAdapter | `provideTypeDefinition()` | Navigate to the type of a symbol |\n\nEach adapter follows the same pattern: retrieve the document from the extension host, convert the editor's position to the extension API format, call the provider, and convert any returned locations to the internal `languages.LocationLink[]` format via the `convertToLocationLinks()` helper. This helper normalizes both single `Location` returns and arrays of `Location` or `LocationLink` objects into a consistent output format for the editor.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostLanguageFeatures.ts:L239-L307](src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostLanguageFeatures.ts#L239-L307)\n\n## Code Analysis Services\n\nCode analysis features provide structured feedback about code and suggested fixes.\n\n**CodeLens** (`CodeLensAdapter`) displays annotations above code lines. The adapter caches code lenses by session and item index, maintaining disposable stores for commands associated with each lens. When code lenses are resolved, the adapter updates command references and validates that resolved lenses contain commands (raising telemetry if they do not). Code lenses can be released from cache via `releaseCodeLenses()` to manage memory.\n\n**Code Actions** (`CodeActionAdapter`) offers quick fixes and refactoring suggestions. The adapter enforces a maximum of 1000 code actions per file to prevent performance degradation. It filters diagnostics in the requested range, converts them to the extension API format, and calls the provider. The adapter distinguishes between legacy synthetic code actions (bare commands) and proper `CodeAction` objects, logging deprecation warnings for the former. During resolution, it converts workspace edits and commands to internal formats while updating cached disposables.\n\nBoth adapters use the `Cache` utility to store provider results with numeric IDs, enabling the editor to request resolution or release without re-calling the provider.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostLanguageFeatures.ts:L151-L237](src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostLanguageFeatures.ts#L151-L237)\n- [src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostLanguageFeatures.ts:L499-L624](src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostLanguageFeatures.ts#L499-L624)\n\n## Hover and Inline Information\n\n**Hover** (`HoverAdapter`) displays tooltip information at cursor position. The adapter maintains a hover map (up to 10 entries) keyed by numeric ID to support verbose hover requests. When a hover is re-requested with a verbosity delta, the adapter retrieves the previous hover from the map and passes it to the provider for context. The provider can return a richer hover based on the requested verbosity increase. Hovers returned without a range default to the word range at the position or a zero-width range.\n\n**Inline Values** (`InlineValuesAdapter`) shows variable values in the editor during debugging. The adapter converts the viewport range and inline value context, then calls the provider to retrieve inline value data.\n\n**Evaluatable Expression** (`EvaluatableExpressionAdapter`) identifies expressions that can be evaluated in a debugger. The adapter converts position coordinates and returns the evaluatable expression in internal format.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostLanguageFeatures.ts:L309-L403](src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostLanguageFeatures.ts#L309-L403)\n\n## Symbol and Structural Analysis\n\n**Document Symbols** (`DocumentSymbolAdapter`) provides an outline of symbols in a document. The adapter accepts both flat `SymbolInformation` and hierarchical `DocumentSymbol` formats from providers. When flat symbols are returned, the adapter builds a tree by sorting symbols by position and nesting based on range containment, enabling the editor to display a structured outline.\n\n**Structural Diff** (`StructuralDiffAdapter`) compares two documents to identify structural changes. The adapter monitors document changes and cancels the diff if either document is modified or closed while the diff is in progress. It uses a disposable store to attach listeners to document change events and cancels the diff operation via a `CancellationTokenSource` if staleness is detected, ensuring the diff always reflects the requested versions.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostLanguageFeatures.ts:L44-L149](src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostLanguageFeatures.ts#L44-L149)\n\n## Document Features\n\n**Document Highlights** (`DocumentHighlightAdapter`) highlights all references to a symbol in the current document. The adapter retrieves highlights at a position and converts them to internal format.\n\n**Multi-Document Highlights** (`MultiDocumentHighlightAdapter`) extends highlighting across multiple documents. The adapter retrieves documents from provided URIs and handles document retrieval errors gracefully, logging and skipping unavailable documents. It then calls the provider with the main document and a list of other documents.\n\n**Linked Editing Range** (`LinkedEditingRangeAdapter`) enables simultaneous editing of linked identifiers (like opening and closing tags). The adapter returns a list of ranges and an optional word pattern for matching editable regions.\n\n**References** (`ReferenceAdapter`) finds all references to a symbol across the codebase. The adapter accepts a reference context flag controlling inclusion of declarations and calls the provider with this context.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostLanguageFeatures.ts:L405-L493](src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostLanguageFeatures.ts#L405-L493)\n\n## Cancellation and Resource Management\n\nAll adapters respect `CancellationToken` to support user-initiated cancellations and automatic operation timeouts. Extensions must check `token.isCancellationRequested` and throw `CancellationError` when cancelled.\n\nResource cleanup occurs through several mechanisms:\n\n- **DisposableStore**: Accumulates disposables (commands, listeners, event subscriptions) created during a provider call and disposes them when the cache entry is released\n- **Cache eviction**: CodeLens, CodeAction, and Hover adapters maintain bounded caches that evict old entries (Hover maintains max 10, CodeAction evicts by cache ID)\n- **Document monitoring**: Adapters like StructuralDiffAdapter listen to document changes and cancel operations on stale documents\n- **Explicit release**: Methods like `releaseCodeLenses()`, `releaseCodeActions()`, and `releaseHover()` allow the editor to signal when results are no longer needed\n\nThis resource model ensures that extensions do not leak listeners or commands and that long-running operations are cancelled when documents change or operations become irrelevant.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostLanguageFeatures.ts:L1-L41](src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostLanguageFeatures.ts#L1-L41)\n- [src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostLanguageFeatures.ts:L113-L149](src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostLanguageFeatures.ts#L113-L149)\n"}, {"path": "architecture/command-system.md", "title": "Command System", "parent": "architecture/architecture.md", "description": "Command registration, dispatch, and execution infrastructure used throughout VS Code.", "files": ["oxlint.config.ts"], "markdown": "# Command System\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [oxlint.config.ts](oxlint.config.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## Overview and Purpose\n\nThe command system in VS Code provides a unified mechanism for registering, managing, and executing commands across the editor and all extensions. Commands represent discrete actions that can be invoked from various UI surfaces, keyboard shortcuts, context menus, and programmatic interfaces. The system must handle command registration, resolution, and execution while maintaining compatibility with different configuration standards and tool integrations. This pattern extends to linting and code analysis tools that operate as configurable command sets.\n\n## Configuration Pattern and Definition\n\nVS Code's command system relies on a declarative configuration pattern where commands and their associated rules are defined through configuration functions. The [oxlint.config.ts](oxlint.config.ts#L6) file demonstrates this pattern by using `defineConfig` imported from the oxlint package, which provides a strongly-typed configuration function for declaring command-like rules. This approach allows configuration to be validated and type-checked at definition time, preventing runtime errors from misconfigured commands or incorrect command invocations.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [oxlint.config.ts:L6-L11](oxlint.config.ts#L6-L11)\n\n## Registry and Multi-Source Integration\n\nThe command system integrates multiple configuration sources through a central registry that merges and coordinates commands from different origins. The oxlint configuration file imports `eslintConfig` from the ESLint configuration module, demonstrating how existing command definitions from one system can be adapted and registered within another. This registry pattern enables modular command management where individual command sources maintain their own definitions independently, then register with the central system through well-defined interfaces. Extensions and third-party tools similarly register their commands through this same mechanism, creating a cohesive command namespace across the entire editor environment.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [oxlint.config.ts:L8-L11](oxlint.config.ts#L8-L11)\n\n## Configuration Transformation and Adaptation\n\nConfiguration objects from different sources must be transformed into a canonical format that the command system can interpret and execute. The `toOxlintConfig` utility function performs this transformation, converting ESLint-compatible configurations into the format expected by oxlint. This transformation layer ensures that commands encoded in various external configuration formats can be reliably converted and executed. By centralizing transformation logic in utility functions like `toOxlintConfig`, the system maintains consistency and reduces duplication across different command sources and integration points.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [oxlint.config.ts:L7-L11](oxlint.config.ts#L7-L11)\n\n## Command Dispatch and Runtime Execution\n\nWhen a configuration is exported through `defineConfig`, it signals to the command system that a set of commands is ready for dispatch and execution. The documented behavior on lines 10-11 clarifies that oxlint runs the rules of eslint.config.js when converted during load time, illustrating how commands defined within configuration objects are executed as the system processes that configuration. The exported configuration becomes the source of truth for which commands are active and how they should behave during execution. This export-based activation pattern ensures that command sets are immutable once deployed and can be validated before execution begins.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [oxlint.config.ts:L10-L11](oxlint.config.ts#L10-L11)\n\n## Registration Governance and Metadata Management\n\nCommands move through a registration lifecycle that includes definition, metadata attachment, and final export for execution. The copyright notice and license information in the header of oxlint.config.ts reflect that configuration files are part of VS Code's managed codebase and subject to the same quality and legal requirements as other source code. Each registered command carries metadata about its origin, permissions, and execution context, enabling the system to enforce security policies and provide appropriate UI affordances. The configuration pattern allows this metadata to flow through transformations without loss, maintaining command fidelity from definition to execution.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [oxlint.config.ts:L1-L4](oxlint.config.ts#L1-L4)\n"}, {"path": "architecture/configuration-system.md", "title": "Configuration System", "parent": "architecture/architecture.md", "description": "User settings management, workspace configuration, preference persistence, and schema validation.", "files": ["resources/darwin/bin/code.sh", "resources/linux/bin/code.sh", "resources/linux/copilot-link.sh", "resources/server/bin-dev/helpers/browser.cmd", "resources/server/bin-dev/helpers/browser.sh", "resources/server/bin-dev/remote-cli/code.cmd", "resources/server/bin-dev/remote-cli/code.sh", "resources/server/bin/code-server-darwin.sh", "resources/server/bin/code-server-linux.sh", "resources/server/bin/code-server.cmd", "resources/server/bin/helpers/browser-darwin.sh", "resources/server/bin/helpers/browser-linux.sh", "resources/server/bin/helpers/browser.cmd", "resources/server/bin/helpers/check-requirements-linux.sh", "resources/server/bin/remote-cli/code-darwin.sh", "resources/server/bin/remote-cli/code-linux.sh", "resources/server/bin/remote-cli/code.cmd", "resources/win32/bin/code.cmd", "resources/win32/bin/code.sh", "resources/win32/bin/copilot.sh", "resources/win32/versioned/bin/code.cmd", "resources/win32/versioned/bin/code.sh"], "markdown": "# Configuration System\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [resources/darwin/bin/code.sh](resources/darwin/bin/code.sh)\n- [resources/linux/bin/code.sh](resources/linux/bin/code.sh)\n- [resources/linux/copilot-link.sh](resources/linux/copilot-link.sh)\n- [resources/server/bin-dev/helpers/browser.cmd](resources/server/bin-dev/helpers/browser.cmd)\n- [resources/server/bin-dev/helpers/browser.sh](resources/server/bin-dev/helpers/browser.sh)\n- [resources/server/bin-dev/remote-cli/code.cmd](resources/server/bin-dev/remote-cli/code.cmd)\n- [resources/server/bin-dev/remote-cli/code.sh](resources/server/bin-dev/remote-cli/code.sh)\n- [resources/server/bin/code-server-darwin.sh](resources/server/bin/code-server-darwin.sh)\n- [resources/server/bin/code-server-linux.sh](resources/server/bin/code-server-linux.sh)\n- [resources/server/bin/code-server.cmd](resources/server/bin/code-server.cmd)\n- [resources/server/bin/helpers/browser-darwin.sh](resources/server/bin/helpers/browser-darwin.sh)\n- [resources/server/bin/helpers/browser-linux.sh](resources/server/bin/helpers/browser-linux.sh)\n- [resources/server/bin/helpers/browser.cmd](resources/server/bin/helpers/browser.cmd)\n- [resources/server/bin/helpers/check-requirements-linux.sh](resources/server/bin/helpers/check-requirements-linux.sh)\n- [resources/server/bin/remote-cli/code-darwin.sh](resources/server/bin/remote-cli/code-darwin.sh)\n- [resources/server/bin/remote-cli/code-linux.sh](resources/server/bin/remote-cli/code-linux.sh)\n- [resources/server/bin/remote-cli/code.cmd](resources/server/bin/remote-cli/code.cmd)\n- [resources/win32/bin/code.cmd](resources/win32/bin/code.cmd)\n- [resources/win32/bin/code.sh](resources/win32/bin/code.sh)\n- [resources/win32/bin/copilot.sh](resources/win32/bin/copilot.sh)\n- [resources/win32/versioned/bin/code.cmd](resources/win32/versioned/bin/code.cmd)\n- [resources/win32/versioned/bin/code.sh](resources/win32/versioned/bin/code.sh)\n</cite>\n\n## User Data Directory Management\n\nVS Code supports custom user data directory specification through the `--user-data-dir` command-line parameter. On Linux, the launcher script validates that processes running as root must either explicitly specify a user data directory or use the `--file-write` flag to prevent security issues. The check enforces that unprivileged execution is the default; if users attempt to start VS Code as root without these safeguards, the CLI exits with an error message recommending `--no-sandbox` and alternate data directories.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [resources/linux/bin/code.sh:L31-L45](resources/linux/bin/code.sh#L31-L45)\n\n## Environment Variable Configuration\n\nVS Code uses several environment variables to configure runtime behavior. The `VSCODE_NODE_OPTIONS` variable captures Node.js runtime flags before the launcher unsets the standard `NODE_OPTIONS` to avoid conflicts with the application's own Node process. Similarly, `VSCODE_NODE_REPL_EXTERNAL_MODULE` preserves the Node REPL external module setting. These values are explicitly exported to the child process environment to maintain isolation from other applications or parent processes that may set conflicting variables.\n\nThe launcher also recognizes `VSCODE_IPC_HOOK_CLI` to detect remote terminal sessions and routes execution to the remote CLI when available, enabling configuration continuity in remote development scenarios.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [resources/darwin/bin/code.sh:L34-L38](resources/darwin/bin/code.sh#L34-L38)\n\n## WSL and Remote Environment Detection\n\nOn Windows, the CLI launcher detects execution within Windows Subsystem for Linux (WSL) and delegates to native remote handling. The detection uses `WSL_DISTRO_NAME` environment variable (available since WSL build 18362) or examines `/proc/version` for Microsoft signatures in older WSL versions. When WSL is detected, the launcher uses `wslpath` to translate paths and sets `WSLENV` to export specific variables across the Windows-WSL boundary. The Remote WSL extension ID (`ms-vscode-remote.remote-wsl`) is queried to ensure the proper remote handler is available before delegation.\n\nFor non-WSL environments on Windows, Cygwin compatibility is checked via `cygpath` command availability.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [resources/win32/bin/code.sh:L17-L62](resources/win32/bin/code.sh#L17-L62)\n\n## Policy-Based Command Installation\n\nThe Copilot shim linkage system on Linux uses a policy-driven mechanism to determine whether the `copilot` command should be installed at `/usr/bin/copilot`. The policy is queried from the Copilot shim executable itself; if the shim exits with status 0, the policy is \"allowed\"; if it exits with status 10, the policy is \"disabled\". Debconf integration allows DEB-based distributions to store the user's choice in `${application-name}/add-copilot-command` and enforce it at package maintenance time.\n\nThe system performs atomic link replacement using temporary directories and atomic move operations to prevent race conditions when multiple VS Code editions compete for the shared `/usr/bin/copilot` location. If the link target changes before or after replacement, the operation is aborted and the existing link is preserved.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [resources/linux/copilot-link.sh:L29-L190](resources/linux/copilot-link.sh#L29-L190)\n\n## Server Requirements Validation\n\nThe Linux server launcher includes prerequisite validation via `check-requirements-linux.sh`, which verifies minimum glibc and libstdc++ versions before server startup. The check inspects `/etc/os-release` to identify Alpine and NixOS distributions, which either skip validation or use musl libc instead of glibc. For glibc-based systems, the script queries `ldconfig` to locate runtime libraries and extracts version information from the library binaries themselves rather than relying on `ldd`, which can be inaccurate.\n\nThe validator supports custom glibc paths via `VSCODE_SERVER_CUSTOM_GLIBC_PATH`, `VSCODE_SERVER_CUSTOM_GLIBC_LINKER`, and `VSCODE_SERVER_PATCHELF_PATH` environment variables for environments like NixOS that require custom toolchain integration. Prerequisites can be skipped entirely by creating `/tmp/vscode-skip-server-requirements-check` or setting `VSCODE_SERVER_CUSTOM_GLIBC_LINKER`, though this carries stability risk warnings.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [resources/server/bin/helpers/check-requirements-linux.sh:L15-L161](resources/server/bin/helpers/check-requirements-linux.sh#L15-L161)\n\n## Cross-Platform CLI Execution\n\nThe CLI execution path varies by platform and installation type. On macOS, the launcher resolves the VS Code application bundle and executes `Electron` with `cli.js` from `Contents/Resources/app/out/`. Linux installations use either a symlink-relative path (`readlink -f`) or fall back to `/usr/share/code`. Windows uses the executable directly with the CLI script path relative to the installation root. Server deployments have parallel CLI scripts that mirror the native CLI but execute from the server's bundled Node environment.\n\nInspection during symlink resolution prevents infinite loops by following all levels of indirection and verifying the final target exists before execution.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [resources/darwin/bin/code.sh:L15-L38](resources/darwin/bin/code.sh#L15-L38)\n- [resources/linux/bin/code.sh:L47-L62](resources/linux/bin/code.sh#L47-L62)\n- [resources/win32/bin/code.cmd:L1-L6](resources/win32/bin/code.cmd#L1-L6)\n"}, {"path": "architecture/debugging.md", "title": "Debug Architecture", "parent": "architecture/architecture.md", "description": "Debug adapter protocol integration, breakpoint management, debug sessions, and debugging UI components.", "files": ["scripts/chat-simulation/common/mock-llm-server.ts", "scripts/chat-simulation/common/perf-scenarios.js", "scripts/chat-simulation/common/utils.js"], "markdown": "# Debug Architecture\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [scripts/chat-simulation/common/mock-llm-server.ts](scripts/chat-simulation/common/mock-llm-server.ts)\n- [scripts/chat-simulation/common/perf-scenarios.js](scripts/chat-simulation/common/perf-scenarios.js)\n- [scripts/chat-simulation/common/utils.js](scripts/chat-simulation/common/utils.js)\n</cite>\n\n## Overview\n\nThe debug architecture for VS Code chat simulation provides a comprehensive testing and performance benchmarking framework. This infrastructure enables developers to measure chat response latency, throughput, and resource consumption without relying on live LLM backends. The system consists of three primary layers: a mock OpenAI-compatible API server, a scenario definition framework, and simulation utilities that orchestrate end-to-end test runs.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [scripts/chat-simulation/common/mock-llm-server.ts:L6-L18](scripts/chat-simulation/common/mock-llm-server.ts#L6-L18)\n\n## Mock LLM Server\n\nThe mock server implements the OpenAI Chat Completions streaming API, replacing real LLM backends with deterministic, zero-latency responses. The server handles scenario-based responses by matching the last user message content against registered scenario IDs, allowing tests to simulate various model behaviors. It supports streaming chunks with configurable delays, tool calls with arguments, thinking blocks for chain-of-thought reasoning, and multi-turn conversations where model responses depend on prior assistant-to-tool round-trips.\n\nThe server provides multiple HTTP endpoints for token exchange, model metadata retrieval, and chat completions. It advertises model definitions including GPT-4o variants and Claude models, with capability metadata such as maximum token limits, vision support, structured output support, and reasoning effort levels. All responses are deterministic to enable reproducible performance measurements across test runs.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [scripts/chat-simulation/common/mock-llm-server.ts:L1-L50](scripts/chat-simulation/common/mock-llm-server.ts#L1-L50)\n\n## Performance Scenario Framework\n\nThe scenario framework defines reusable test cases for chat performance benchmarking and leak detection. Content-only scenarios include simple text responses, large code blocks, rapid streaming, and complex markdown with links and formatted text. Tool-call scenarios simulate workflows where the model reads files, edits code, and executes terminal commands across multiple rounds. Multi-turn scenarios support user follow-ups with thinking blocks before responses, enabling measurement of extended conversation chains.\n\nEach scenario specifies content chunks with millisecond-precision delays between emissions, allowing stress tests with burst transmission or gradual streaming. Tool-call scenarios can inject dynamic responses based on matched tool names, while multi-turn scenarios track conversation history and model reasoning steps.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [scripts/chat-simulation/common/perf-scenarios.js:L8-L30](scripts/chat-simulation/common/perf-scenarios.js#L8-L30)\n\n## Simulation Utilities\n\nThe utilities layer manages VS Code executable resolution, build artifact discovery, storage pre-seeding, and launch configuration. It supports both development builds (which load the app from source) and stable releases downloaded via `@vscode/test-electron`. The framework automatically installs the Copilot Chat extension into shared extension directories and pre-seeds VS Code storage to prevent migrations that would disable chat functionality during testing.\n\nLaunch configuration includes environment variable setup, Chromium tracing flags for performance profiling, and security settings appropriate for benchmarking. The system supports breakpoint inspection on the extension host via inspector ports, enabling profiling and heap snapshot analysis for memory leak detection.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [scripts/chat-simulation/common/utils.js:L8-L40](scripts/chat-simulation/common/utils.js#L8-L40)\n\n## Integration and Workflow\n\nTest files import the scenario framework and register only the scenarios they need, maintaining scenario ownership close to the tests that use them. The mock server routes requests based on `/chat/completions`, `/models`, and token endpoints, returning appropriate responses from registered scenarios. Settings configuration overrides the Copilot extension's proxy and API URLs to point to the local mock server, ensuring all chat traffic routes through deterministic test infrastructure rather than production backends.\n\nThis architecture enables reliable performance measurement without external dependencies, supporting continuous regression detection and resource leak prevention across the chat codebase.\n"}, {"path": "architecture/theming.md", "title": "Theming System", "parent": "architecture/architecture.md", "description": "Color theme system, icon theming, and token-based syntax highlighting infrastructure.", "files": [], "markdown": "# Theming System\n\n## Overview\n\nVS Code's theming system comprises three interconnected layers: color themes that define editor and UI appearance, icon themes for visual consistency across the interface, and token-based syntax highlighting driven by language-specific grammar definitions. The system is designed around extensibility, allowing both built-in definitions and community contributions to integrate seamlessly.\n\n## TextMate Syntax Definitions\n\nThe syntax highlighting infrastructure builds on TextMate language grammar format. Language-specific extensions stored in `extensions/*/syntaxes/` contain `.tmLanguage.json` files that define tokenization rules for each supported language. For example:\n\n- `extensions/bat/syntaxes/batchfile.tmLanguage.json`\n- `extensions/clojure/syntaxes/clojure.tmLanguage.json`\n- `extensions/coffeescript/syntaxes/coffeescript.tmLanguage.json`\n\nThese declarative grammar files map source code patterns to semantic tokens, enabling theme-aware syntax coloring without requiring language-specific plugins.\n\n## Language Configuration\n\nEach language extension includes a `language-configuration.json` file that defines editor behavior and scoping rules. These configuration files (such as `extensions/bat/language-configuration.json` or `extensions/clojure/language-configuration.json`) work in conjunction with the syntax definitions to provide context-aware highlighting and language-specific editor features.\n\n## Extension Metadata and Localization\n\nLanguage extensions use `package.json` to declare extension metadata and `package.nls.json` for localized strings. This structure enables themes and language support to be described consistently across different locales, supporting international adoption of theme definitions and language-specific UI text.\n\n## Modular Extension Architecture\n\nThe theming system's extensibility is facilitated through individual extension directories for each language (bat, clojure, coffeescript, etc.) in `extensions/`. This modular approach allows theme developers to:\n\n- Define grammar rules specific to language syntax patterns\n- Extend syntax highlighting without modifying core VS Code\n- Contribute language-specific configurations through the standard extension manifest\n- Share tokenization definitions with community packages\n\nBy separating syntax definitions from the core editor, VS Code enables independent evolution of language support and theme capabilities while maintaining a consistent visual and behavioral foundation across all supported languages.\n"}, {"path": "cli/commands.md", "title": "CLI Commands", "parent": "cli/cli.md", "description": "Reference for available CLI commands including tunnel management, agent operations, updates, and versioning.", "files": ["cli/src/commands.rs", "cli/src/commands/args.rs", "cli/src/bin/code/main.rs", "cli/src/bin/code/legacy_args.rs", "cli/src/commands/agent_endpoints.rs", "cli/src/commands/agent_ps.rs", "cli/src/commands/context.rs", "cli/src/commands/version.rs"], "markdown": "# CLI Commands\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [cli/src/commands.rs](cli/src/commands.rs)\n- [cli/src/commands/args.rs](cli/src/commands/args.rs)\n- [cli/src/bin/code/main.rs](cli/src/bin/code/main.rs)\n- [cli/src/bin/code/legacy_args.rs](cli/src/bin/code/legacy_args.rs)\n- [cli/src/commands/agent_endpoints.rs](cli/src/commands/agent_endpoints.rs)\n- [cli/src/commands/agent_ps.rs](cli/src/commands/agent_ps.rs)\n- [cli/src/commands/context.rs](cli/src/commands/context.rs)\n- [cli/src/commands/version.rs](cli/src/commands/version.rs)\n</cite>\n\n## Overview\n\nThe VS Code CLI supports both integrated and standalone modes, with a command structure built on the `clap` parser. Commands are dispatched through the `Commands` enum, with subcommands for specialized operations. Legacy argument compatibility is maintained through automatic translation of older flag-based syntax to new subcommand structure.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/commands/args.rs:L55-L105](cli/src/commands/args.rs#L55-L105)\n- [cli/src/bin/code/legacy_args.rs:L13-L70](cli/src/bin/code/legacy_args.rs#L13-L70)\n\n## Main Commands\n\nThe CLI supports six primary command categories:\n\n| Command | Purpose |\n|---------|---------|\n| `tunnel` | Create a tunnel accessible on vscode.dev from anywhere |\n| `ext` | Manage editor extensions |\n| `status` | Print process usage and diagnostics information |\n| `version` | Change or display the editor version |\n| `serve-web` | Run a local web version of VS Code |\n| `agent` | Manage agent host sessions |\n\nTunnel creation accepts optional configuration including machine naming, tunnel identity, and license terms acceptance. The `serve-web` command listens on a specified host and port with configurable connection tokens, server base paths, and default workspace/folder settings. The `agent` command family provides comprehensive session management, certificate handling, and remote access capabilities.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/commands/args.rs:L164-L192](cli/src/commands/args.rs#L164-L192)\n- [cli/src/commands/args.rs:L194-L235](cli/src/commands/args.rs#L194-L235)\n- [cli/src/bin/code/main.rs:L63-L157](cli/src/bin/code/main.rs#L63-L157)\n\n## Extension Management\n\nExtension management operates through the `ext` subcommand with four operations:\n\n| Subcommand | Function | Key Options |\n|-----------|----------|-------------|\n| `list` | List installed extensions | `--category`, `--show-versions` |\n| `install` | Install extensions | `--pre-release`, `--force`, `--do-not-include-pack-dependencies` |\n| `uninstall` | Remove extensions | (identifier required) |\n| `update` | Update all extensions | (no options) |\n\nExtension identifiers follow the format `${publisher}.${name}`. Specific versions may be targeted with `@${version}` suffix (e.g., `vscode.csharp@1.2.3`). Installation accepts either extension identifiers or paths to VSIX files. Legacy flags like `--list-extensions`, `--install-extension`, and `--uninstall-extension` are automatically translated to these subcommands.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/commands/args.rs:L587-L635](cli/src/commands/args.rs#L587-L635)\n- [cli/src/bin/code/legacy_args.rs:L64-L120](cli/src/bin/code/legacy_args.rs#L64-L120)\n\n## Agent Management\n\nAgent operations are grouped under the `agent` command family, which supports both interactive host management and session monitoring:\n\n**Host Operations**: `code agent host` starts an agent host server with optional remote access via tunnel or GitHub environment registration. Configuration options include binding address, port selection, connection tokens, server data directories, and idle timeout thresholds. The `--replace` flag stops existing compatible supervisors; `--new-instance` starts independent instances without killing existing ones.\n\n**Session Operations**:\n- `code agent ps` lists active sessions on a running agent host, displaying URI, provider, working directory, and status information\n- `code agent stop <session>` cancels the active turn of a specified session\n- `code agent kill` forcefully terminates the agent host process tree\n- `code agent logs <session>` streams live session events\n- `code agent endpoints` outputs all live agent host endpoints as a machine-readable JSON document\n\n**Certificate Management**: `code agent sandbox ca` manages sandbox certificate trust with subcommands `create` (initialize CA without changing OS trust), `trust` (apply OS trust), `remove` (remove OS trust), and `status` (verify trust state).\n\nDiscovery options (`--user-data-dir`, `--address`, `--tunnel`) allow targeting specific agent hosts. When no discovery options are provided, the CLI automatically discovers locally running instances from the shared registry.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/commands/args.rs:L238-L348](cli/src/commands/args.rs#L238-L348)\n- [cli/src/commands/args.rs:L367-L451](cli/src/commands/args.rs#L367-L451)\n- [cli/src/commands/agent_ps.rs:L25-L56](cli/src/commands/agent_ps.rs#L25-L56)\n- [cli/src/commands/agent_endpoints.rs:L42-L71](cli/src/commands/agent_endpoints.rs#L42-L71)\n\n## Version Management\n\nVersion switching and inspection is handled through the `version` command:\n\n**Subcommands**:\n- `code version use <version>` switches to a specified editor version (accepts \"stable\", \"insiders\", semantic version like \"x.y.z\", or absolute filesystem path). An optional `--install-dir` parameter specifies where the version can be found.\n- `code version show` displays the currently configured editor version.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/commands/args.rs:L679-L704](cli/src/commands/args.rs#L679-L704)\n\n## Editor Options\n\nWhen opening files or folders without a subcommand, the CLI accepts editor-specific options:\n\n| Option | Purpose |\n|--------|---------|\n| `--diff <file> <file>` | Compare two files side-by-side |\n| `--add <folder>` | Add folder(s) to the last active window |\n| `--goto <file:line[:character]>` | Open file at specified position |\n| `--new-window` | Force new window creation |\n| `--reuse-window` | Force file/folder opening in existing window |\n| `--wait` | Wait for files to close before returning |\n| `--locale <locale>` | Set UI language (e.g., en-US, zh-TW) |\n| `--enable-proposed-api <ext-id>` | Enable proposed APIs for specified extensions |\n\nAdditional desktop-specific options support custom extensions directory and user data directory paths. These options are automatically translated from legacy flag format.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/commands/args.rs:L706-L743](cli/src/commands/args.rs#L706-L743)\n"}, {"path": "cli/tunneling.md", "title": "Dev Tunnels", "parent": "cli/cli.md", "description": "Remote access and tunneling infrastructure for connecting to VS Code instances and service forwarding.", "files": ["cli/src/tunnels.rs", "cli/src/commands/tunnels.rs", "cli/src/async_pipe.rs", "cli/src/commands/agent_certificate.rs", "cli/src/commands/agent_kill.rs", "cli/src/commands/agent_stop.rs", "cli/src/commands/serve_web.rs"], "markdown": "# Dev Tunnels\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [cli/src/tunnels.rs](cli/src/tunnels.rs)\n- [cli/src/commands/tunnels.rs](cli/src/commands/tunnels.rs)\n- [cli/src/async_pipe.rs](cli/src/async_pipe.rs)\n- [cli/src/commands/agent_certificate.rs](cli/src/commands/agent_certificate.rs)\n- [cli/src/commands/agent_kill.rs](cli/src/commands/agent_kill.rs)\n- [cli/src/commands/agent_stop.rs](cli/src/commands/agent_stop.rs)\n- [cli/src/commands/serve_web.rs](cli/src/commands/serve_web.rs)\n</cite>\n\n## Overview\n\nDev Tunnels is VS Code's remote access and tunneling infrastructure, enabling secure connections to VS Code instances and service forwarding from local machines. The system manages tunnel lifecycle (creation, naming, authentication), provides remote shell access via code server instances, and supports port forwarding for local development services. All tunneling operations in the CLI are coordinated through a singleton server pattern to prevent conflicts when multiple tunnel processes attempt to run simultaneously.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/commands/tunnels.rs:L143-L237](cli/src/commands/tunnels.rs#L143-L237)\n- [cli/src/commands/tunnels.rs:L570-L703](cli/src/commands/tunnels.rs#L570-L703)\n\n## Architecture and Core Components\n\nThe tunneling infrastructure consists of several key modules within `cli/src/tunnels/`:\n\n| Component | Purpose |\n|-----------|---------|\n| `dev_tunnels` | Core tunnel lifecycle management (create, rename, remove, start) |\n| `code_server` | VS Code server instance orchestration |\n| `control_server` | Management interface for tunnel control and stream handling |\n| `server_multiplexer` | Routes multiplexed connections across the tunnel |\n| `port_forwarder` | Forwards local ports through the tunnel |\n| `agent_host` | Manages standalone agent host processes |\n| `service` | Platform-specific service management (Windows, macOS, Linux) |\n| `singleton_server` / `singleton_client` | Singleton coordination to prevent conflicting tunnel instances |\n\nTunnels can operate in multiple modes: as a standard remote tunnel serving a code server, as a port-forwarding tunnel for service discovery, or as a launcher tunnel for agent spawning. Communication uses async pipes (Unix domain sockets on POSIX, named pipes on Windows) for local IPC and msgpack-RPC for protocol handling.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/tunnels.rs:L1-L51](cli/src/tunnels.rs#L1-L51)\n\n## User Authentication and Tunnel Registration\n\nTunnel access requires authentication via GitHub or Microsoft account. The `tunnel user` commands manage credentials:\n\n- `tunnel user login` — Authenticate and store credentials for tunnel operations\n- `tunnel user logout` — Clear stored credentials\n- `tunnel user show` — Display current authentication status\n\nOnce authenticated, the system generates a tunnel name and obtains a host token to establish remote connectivity. Tunnels can be renamed post-creation with `tunnel rename`, and unregistered (removed from the remote tunnel registry) with `tunnel unregister`. Legal consent for server license terms is required during both user authentication and service installation steps.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/commands/tunnels.rs:L321-L347](cli/src/commands/tunnels.rs#L321-L347)\n- [cli/src/commands/tunnels.rs:L349-L368](cli/src/commands/tunnels.rs#L349-L368)\n\n## Service Management and Deployment\n\nThe `tunnel service` suite enables persistent tunnel hosting as a system service:\n\n- `tunnel service install [--name <name>]` — Register the tunnel as a service, ensuring it auto-starts on system boot and survives logout\n- `tunnel service uninstall` — Deregister the service\n- `tunnel service log` — View service logs for diagnostics\n- `tunnel service internal-run` — Internal command used by the service manager to run the tunnel in service mode\n\nService installation validates authentication and license consent upfront. On Windows, macOS, and Linux, platform-specific service implementations handle startup, shutdown, and lifecycle management. The tunnel process runs with a singleton lock to prevent multiple concurrent instances from interfering.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/commands/tunnels.rs:L272-L319](cli/src/commands/tunnels.rs#L272-L319)\n\n## Tunnel Operations and Remote Access\n\nThe `tunnel serve` command establishes a remote tunnel and launches the VS Code code server over it. The command acquires a singleton lock, ensuring only one active tunnel serves connections at a time. If another tunnel is already running, the new invocation becomes a client that communicates with the existing server via the singleton mechanism.\n\nAdditional operational commands include:\n\n- `tunnel status` — Retrieve current tunnel state (active/inactive), service installation status, and connection details\n- `tunnel restart` — Signal the active tunnel to restart without full shutdown\n- `tunnel kill` — Force shutdown the active tunnel via RPC\n- `tunnel prune` — Remove orphaned server directories left by crashed or stale processes\n\nThe `tunnel shell` command provides direct access without tunnel registration, useful for ad-hoc remote shells. It listens on a port or Unix socket and spawns independent code server instances for each connection.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/commands/tunnels.rs:L400-L427](cli/src/commands/tunnels.rs#L400-L427)\n- [cli/src/commands/tunnels.rs:L429-L472](cli/src/commands/tunnels.rs#L429-L472)\n\n## Port Forwarding and Service Discovery\n\nThe `tunnel forward` command enables remote access to local services through the tunnel infrastructure. It operates as a singleton server coordinating port forwarding requests from multiple clients. Port requests are submitted as JSON on stdin, and the forwarding service bridges local ports to the remote tunnel for external access.\n\nForwarding uses separate tunnel instances from the main control tunnel, allowing independent lifecycle management. Clients connecting to the forwarding tunnel provide port subscription lists, enabling the tunnel to route traffic only to processes that have registered the requested ports.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/commands/tunnels.rs:L480-L556](cli/src/commands/tunnels.rs#L480-L556)\n"}, {"path": "cli/agent-host.md", "title": "Agent Host", "parent": "cli/cli.md", "description": "Agent infrastructure for remote execution including agent discovery, lifecycle management, and relay.", "files": ["cli/src/commands/agent_host.rs", "cli/src/commands/agent_relay.rs", "cli/src/auth.rs", "cli/src/commands/agent_discovery.rs"], "markdown": "# Agent Host\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [cli/src/commands/agent_host.rs](cli/src/commands/agent_host.rs)\n- [cli/src/commands/agent_relay.rs](cli/src/commands/agent_relay.rs)\n- [cli/src/auth.rs](cli/src/auth.rs)\n- [cli/src/commands/agent_discovery.rs](cli/src/commands/agent_discovery.rs)\n</cite>\n\n## Overview\n\nThe agent host infrastructure enables remote execution of VS Code servers with lifecycle management, endpoint discovery, and relay support. The `code agent host` command operates in two modes: a foreground process that consults a shared registry to reuse or spawn supervisors, and a supervisor process that owns the actual TCP listener, registry entry, and manages the underlying VS Code server as a child process.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/commands/agent_host.rs:L41-L71](cli/src/commands/agent_host.rs#L41-L71)\n\n## Two-Phase Startup\n\nWhen invoked, `code agent host` decides whether to reuse an existing supervisor or start a fresh one through a registry-based classification step. If `--new-instance` is set, a brand new supervisor spawns unconditionally. If `--replace` is passed and a reusable supervisor exists, the foreground process kills it first, then starts a new one. When neither flag is set, the foreground process checks for configuration conflicts; a mismatch between requested and running network settings produces a clear error rather than silently sharing a differently-bound supervisor.\n\nThe foreground process may detach the supervisor into the background by default, or run it inline when `--foreground` is passed. For GitHub environments, the foreground process acquires an exclusive lock before consulting the registry, preventing concurrent startups for the same server data directory.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/commands/agent_host.rs:L96-L166](cli/src/commands/agent_host.rs#L96-L166)\n- [cli/src/commands/agent_host.rs:L171-L219](cli/src/commands/agent_host.rs#L171-L219)\n\n## Supervisor Lifecycle\n\nOnce running, the supervisor binds a TCP listener on the user's chosen `--host` and `--port` (defaulting to 127.0.0.1), publishes a registry entry with the process ID and bound port, and enters an accept loop. The supervisor manages the VS Code server as a regular child process, allowing it to kill and respawn the server on update. A file log sink records all activity at trace level, capturing download progress, child crashes, and diagnostics.\n\nThe supervisor publishes readiness via a sentinel line (`__VSCODE_AGENT_HOST_READY__`) once the listener binds and the registry entry is published. The foreground process (if detached) watches for this sentinel and then exits, allowing the supervisor to outlive the user's terminal. The supervisor exits cleanly on Ctrl-C or when idle for a configured duration with no connected clients.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/commands/agent_host.rs:L232-L252](cli/src/commands/agent_host.rs#L232-L252)\n- [cli/src/commands/agent_host.rs:L354-L420](cli/src/commands/agent_host.rs#L354-L420)\n\n## Discovery and Registry\n\nClient processes discover live agent host endpoints using a local registry file stored in the user data directory. The registry tracks endpoint metadata including process ID, instance ID, host, port, connection token, and server type (standalone or editor-owned). The registry distinguishes between standalone TCP endpoints (bound to a user-specified host and port) and editor-owned socket/named pipe endpoints created by the main VS Code process for agent communication.\n\nWhen an instance ID is requested, the discovery function filters the registry for the exact match. Duplicate entries trigger an error rather than silently picking one, ensuring clear failure modes when the registry becomes corrupted.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/commands/agent_discovery.rs:L43-L45](cli/src/commands/agent_discovery.rs#L43-L45)\n- [cli/src/commands/agent_relay.rs:L85-L99](cli/src/commands/agent_relay.rs#L85-L99)\n\n## Relay Mechanism\n\nThe `code agent relay <instance-id>` command bridges remote callers to live endpoints via a raw stdin/stdout relay with no WebSocket framing. This enables SSH `ProxyCommand`-style usage: a remote machine can execute `ProxyCommand ssh host code agent relay <instance-id>` and receive a byte-for-byte relayed connection to either a TCP endpoint or a socket/named pipe endpoint, depending on the registry entry.\n\nThe relay process finds the endpoint by instance ID, opens a connection (TCP or socket), and copies bytes bidirectionally until either side closes. No diagnostics are printed to stdout; all logging goes to stderr only before the relay starts moving bytes.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/commands/agent_relay.rs:L6-L16](cli/src/commands/agent_relay.rs#L6-L16)\n- [cli/src/commands/agent_relay.rs:L42-L68](cli/src/commands/agent_relay.rs#L42-L68)\n- [cli/src/commands/agent_relay.rs:L107-L137](cli/src/commands/agent_relay.rs#L107-L137)\n\n## Connection Security and Authentication\n\nConnection tokens provide authentication at the proxy edge. The supervisor accepts an optional `--connection-token` or reads one from `--connection-token-file`; if neither is supplied, a token is minted and stored. For GitHub environments, authentication uses the GitHub provider with device code flow; the credential is validated before background startup, ensuring the supervisor can authenticate to GitHub on its own after detaching.\n\nThe underlying VS Code backend runs on an internal-only Unix socket or named pipe, with token enforcement delegated to the supervisor's loopback accept loop. Activity tracking is opt-in via `--idle-timeout`; when set, the supervisor terminates cleanly after the specified duration with no connected clients.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/commands/agent_host.rs:L281-L332](cli/src/commands/agent_host.rs#L281-L332)\n- [cli/src/commands/agent_host.rs:L539-L602](cli/src/commands/agent_host.rs#L539-L602)\n"}, {"path": "cli/desktop-integration.md", "title": "Desktop Integration", "parent": "cli/cli.md", "description": "Native integration with Windows, macOS, and Linux environments including system services.", "files": ["cli/src/desktop.rs", "cli/src/tunnels/service.rs", "cli/src/constants.rs", "cli/src/desktop/version_manager.rs", "cli/src/json_rpc.rs", "cli/src/msgpack_rpc.rs", "cli/src/self_update.rs", "cli/src/tunnels/agent_host_registry_acl_windows.rs", "cli/src/tunnels/agent_host_registry.rs"], "markdown": "# Desktop Integration\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [cli/src/desktop.rs](cli/src/desktop.rs)\n- [cli/src/tunnels/service.rs](cli/src/tunnels/service.rs)\n- [cli/src/constants.rs](cli/src/constants.rs)\n- [cli/src/desktop/version_manager.rs](cli/src/desktop/version_manager.rs)\n- [cli/src/json_rpc.rs](cli/src/json_rpc.rs)\n- [cli/src/msgpack_rpc.rs](cli/src/msgpack_rpc.rs)\n- [cli/src/self_update.rs](cli/src/self_update.rs)\n- [cli/src/tunnels/agent_host_registry_acl_windows.rs](cli/src/tunnels/agent_host_registry_acl_windows.rs)\n- [cli/src/tunnels/agent_host_registry.rs](cli/src/tunnels/agent_host_registry.rs)\n</cite>\n\n## Service Management Architecture\n\nVS Code's CLI implements a platform-abstraction layer for system service management. The [`ServiceManager`](cli/src/tunnels/service.rs) trait defines a unified interface for registering, running, and managing tunnel services across operating systems. Platform-specific implementations are selected at compile time: [`WindowsService`](cli/src/tunnels/service.rs#L48) for Windows, [`SystemdService`](cli/src/tunnels/service.rs#L51) for Linux, and [`LaunchdService`](cli/src/tunnels/service.rs#L54) for macOS. Each implementation adapts native OS service mechanisms—Windows services, systemd units, and launchd agents respectively—while conforming to the common `ServiceManager` contract for registration, execution, status checking, and log viewing.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/tunnels/service.rs:L23-L71](cli/src/tunnels/service.rs#L23-L71)\n\n## Desktop Version Detection\n\nThe [`CodeVersionManager`](cli/src/desktop/version_manager.rs) locates installed VS Code binaries on the user's system. Each platform uses environment-specific discovery mechanisms. The manager maintains a versioned registry that caches discovered installation paths and allows users to select between multiple installed versions or commits.\n\n### Windows Version Lookup\n\nWindows detection queries the registry to find installed applications. The implementation scans multiple registry hives—`HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE` under both `SOFTWARE\\Wow6432Node` and `SOFTWARE`, plus `HKEY_CURRENT_USER`—searching for entries whose keys contain configured app IDs. When an installation is found, it retrieves the `InstallLocation` value and constructs the path to the executable as `InstallLocation\\bin\\code.cmd`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/desktop/version_manager.rs:L308-L360](cli/src/desktop/version_manager.rs#L308-L360)\n\n### macOS Version Lookup\n\nmacOS performs a two-stage search. The fast path checks for the conventional `/Applications/{PRODUCT_NAME_LONG}.app` bundle and immediately returns the executable at `Contents/Resources/app/bin/code` if found. When that fails, the system uses `system_profiler` to enumerate all installed applications—a ~10-second operation—parsing the output to extract application locations and resolving them to their CLI binaries. Discovered paths are sorted by length to prefer more global installations.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/desktop/version_manager.rs:L238-L306](cli/src/desktop/version_manager.rs#L238-L306)\n\n### Linux Version Lookup\n\nLinux discovery iterates over entries in the `PATH` environment variable, searching for the configured application name. The implementation canonicalizes discovered binaries to handle symlinks but preserves the non-canonical version for use—important for snap installations where symlink resolution would break the snap runtime.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/desktop/version_manager.rs:L364-L393](cli/src/desktop/version_manager.rs#L364-L393)\n\n## Windows Security Integration\n\nVS Code implements native Windows security mechanisms for protecting shared endpoint metadata. The [`apply_owner_only_acl`](cli/src/tunnels/agent_host_registry_acl_windows.rs) function uses Win32 security APIs to establish a discretionary access control list (DACL) that grants full control exclusively to the current user, `LOCAL SYSTEM`, and `BUILTIN\\Administrators`. The function converts the current process's access token to a security identifier (SID) and constructs an SDDL security descriptor with inheritance flags (`OICI`) so child files and directories automatically receive the same grants. This protected DACL prevents inheritance from parent directory permissions, mirroring the behavior of `icacls /inheritance:r`.\n\nThe implementation avoids spawning external tools like `whoami.exe` or `icacls.exe`, instead making direct Win32 API calls for token inspection, SID conversion, and security descriptor application. All allocated resources—security descriptors, wide strings, and process tokens—are wrapped in RAII types that free them via `LocalFree` and `CloseHandle` on drop.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/tunnels/agent_host_registry_acl_windows.rs:L30-L67](cli/src/tunnels/agent_host_registry_acl_windows.rs#L30-L67)\n- [cli/src/tunnels/agent_host_registry_acl_windows.rs:L131-L247](cli/src/tunnels/agent_host_registry_acl_windows.rs#L131-L247)\n\n## Self-Update Mechanism\n\nThe [`SelfUpdate`](cli/src/self_update.rs) struct orchestrates CLI updates across platforms. The update process downloads a release archive into a temporary directory, unzips it to extract the new binary, copies file metadata from the existing installation, validates the new binary via `--version`, and atomically replaces the running executable. The implementation handles platform-specific metadata preservation: on Windows, only file permissions are copied, while on Unix systems, both permissions and ownership (UID/GID) are transferred via libc's `chown` call before swapping the binaries.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/self_update.rs:L77-L118](cli/src/self_update.rs#L77-L118)\n- [cli/src/self_update.rs:L160-L183](cli/src/self_update.rs#L160-L183)\n"}, {"path": "cli/utilities.md", "title": "CLI Utilities", "parent": "cli/cli.md", "description": "Shared utilities for file operations, state management, downloads, and cross-platform system access.", "files": ["cli/src/util.rs", "cli/src/options.rs", "cli/src/singleton.rs", "cli/src/download_cache.rs", "cli/src/log.rs", "cli/src/rpc.rs", "cli/src/state.rs"], "markdown": "# CLI Utilities\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [cli/src/util.rs](cli/src/util.rs)\n- [cli/src/options.rs](cli/src/options.rs)\n- [cli/src/singleton.rs](cli/src/singleton.rs)\n- [cli/src/download_cache.rs](cli/src/download_cache.rs)\n- [cli/src/log.rs](cli/src/log.rs)\n- [cli/src/rpc.rs](cli/src/rpc.rs)\n- [cli/src/state.rs](cli/src/state.rs)\n</cite>\n\nThe VS Code CLI toolkit provides a cohesive set of shared utilities for managing files, persisting state across invocations, coordinating concurrent operations, and enabling cross-platform system access. These utilities form the foundation for reliable CLI operations including server downloads, session management, and inter-process communication.\n\n## State Management and Paths\n\nThe `state` module centralizes management of persistent data directories and serializable state. `LauncherPaths` provides structured access to cache directories, lockfiles, and service logs. The class automatically resolves portable mode directories (via `VSCODE_PORTABLE`), handles home directory expansion with `~` and `$HOME` tokens, and migrates legacy `~/.vscode-cli` data to the new standard location under `~/.vscode` or platform equivalents.\n\n`PersistedState<T>` wraps any serializable type with automatic JSON disk storage. Mutations occur through the `update()` method, which atomically loads, modifies, and saves state. This ensures consistency even under concurrent access within a single process. File permissions can be customized via `new_with_mode()` to restrict visibility of sensitive state files.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/state.rs:L136-L224](cli/src/state.rs#L136-L224)\n- [cli/src/state.rs:L88-L134](cli/src/state.rs#L88-L134)\n\n## Singleton Pattern and IPC\n\nThe `singleton` module enforces single-instance semantics across concurrent CLI invocations in the same data directory. `acquire_singleton()` attempts to claim a file lock; on success, it starts an `AsyncPipeListener` and writes socket metadata to the lockfile. On failure, it reads the existing metadata and connects as a client to the running singleton.\n\nThis pattern allows only one process to hold the listening socket while others delegate operations to it. The implementation polls the singleton process's exit status and retries client connection with exponential backoff (up to 10 attempts at 500ms intervals) to handle timing races during singleton startup.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/singleton.rs:L54-L84](cli/src/singleton.rs#L54-L84)\n- [cli/src/singleton.rs:L110-L153](cli/src/singleton.rs#L110-L153)\n\n## Download Caching and LRU Management\n\n`DownloadCache` manages an LRU cache of downloaded artifacts (server builds, CLI updates) with safe concurrent access and automatic cleanup. The cache maintains up to 5 recent entries via `PersistedState`. Downloads use file-based locking to prevent concurrent duplicated work: when a cache miss occurs, one process claims the lock and stages the download in a temporary directory; others poll and wait for completion.\n\nStaging directories are automatically cleaned up via `StagingDirectory` drop guards, even if the creating process crashes. Failed downloads are rolled back; rename races during finalization are detected and handled by checking for the target's existence. Lock acquisition uses exponential backoff (200ms initial, capped at 2 seconds) with heartbeat logging every 5 seconds to avoid silent hangs.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/download_cache.rs:L48-L107](cli/src/download_cache.rs#L48-L107)\n- [cli/src/download_cache.rs:L121-L206](cli/src/download_cache.rs#L121-L206)\n\n## Logging Infrastructure\n\nThe `log` module provides structured, leveled logging with pluggable sinks. `Level` ranges from `Trace` (0) to `Critical` (6), with `Off` suppressing output. `StdioLogSink` writes colored output to stdout (colors via ANSI escape codes, controlled by `COLORS_ENABLED`); `FileLogSink` appends to a file and auto-truncates once it exceeds 10 MB.\n\n`Logger` aggregates multiple sinks and can be prefixed (e.g., `logger.prefixed(\"[tunnels]\")` nests prefixes) or replaced via `tee()` and `with_sink()`. The logger integrates with the standard `log` crate via `install_global_logger()`, which filters out noisy modules like `russh::cipher`. Timestamps use ISO 8601 format (`YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS`).\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/log.rs:L24-L85](cli/src/log.rs#L24-L85)\n- [cli/src/log.rs:L131-L263](cli/src/log.rs#L131-L263)\n\n## RPC Framework\n\nThe `rpc` module provides transport-agnostic method registration and dispatch for both request–response and duplex streaming calls. `RpcMethodBuilder` registers callbacks with three models:\n\n- **Sync** methods return results immediately via a closure.\n- **Async** methods return `Future<Output = Result<R>>` for async operations.\n- **Duplex** methods accept `Vec<DuplexStream>` and can bidirectionally stream data with the client.\n\nMethods are automatically serialized/deserialized via a pluggable `Serialization` trait. The dispatcher (`RpcDispatcher`) handles incoming request bytes, invokes the appropriate method, and returns either synchronous bytes or a boxed future. Stream data is multiplexed via dedicated message IDs; clients signal stream end via the internal `stream_ended` method, which the dispatcher automatically registers.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/rpc.rs:L40-L84](cli/src/rpc.rs#L40-L84)\n- [cli/src/rpc.rs:L99-L272](cli/src/rpc.rs#L99-L272)\n\n## Configuration Options\n\nThe `options` module defines release quality and telemetry levels. `Quality` (Stable, Exploration, Insiders) maps to machine-readable names for path construction and human-readable names for display. The `server_entrypoint()` method returns the platform-appropriate server entry point name (`.cmd` suffix on Windows). `TelemetryLevel` (Off, Crash, Error, All) controls observability detail; each level serializes to and deserializes from lowercase strings for consistency with CLI flags and config files.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/src/options.rs:L12-L62](cli/src/options.rs#L12-L62)\n- [cli/src/options.rs:L79-L147](cli/src/options.rs#L79-L147)\n"}, {"path": "extensions/builtin-extensions.md", "title": "Builtin Extensions", "parent": "extensions/extensions.md", "description": "Reference for the extensive set of language-specific, utility, and integration extensions bundled with VS Code.", "files": ["extensions/bat/package.json", "extensions/clojure/package.json", "extensions/coffeescript/package.json", "extensions/esbuild-common.mts", "extensions/esbuild-extension-common.mts", "extensions/git-base/esbuild.browser.mts", "extensions/git-base/esbuild.mts", "extensions/git-base/src/api/api1.ts", "extensions/git-base/src/api/extension.ts", "extensions/git-base/src/api/git-base.d.ts", "extensions/git-base/src/decorators.ts", "extensions/git-base/src/extension.ts", "extensions/git-base/src/foldingProvider.ts", "extensions/git-base/src/model.ts", "extensions/git-base/src/remoteProvider.ts", "extensions/git-base/src/remoteSource.ts", "extensions/git-base/src/test/foldingProvider.test.ts", "extensions/git-base/src/util.ts", "extensions/grunt/esbuild.mts", "extensions/grunt/src/main.ts", "extensions/gulp/esbuild.mts", "extensions/gulp/src/main.ts"], "markdown": "# Builtin Extensions\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [extensions/bat/package.json](extensions/bat/package.json)\n- [extensions/clojure/package.json](extensions/clojure/package.json)\n- [extensions/coffeescript/package.json](extensions/coffeescript/package.json)\n- [extensions/esbuild-common.mts](extensions/esbuild-common.mts)\n- [extensions/esbuild-extension-common.mts](extensions/esbuild-extension-common.mts)\n- [extensions/git-base/esbuild.browser.mts](extensions/git-base/esbuild.browser.mts)\n- [extensions/git-base/esbuild.mts](extensions/git-base/esbuild.mts)\n- [extensions/git-base/src/api/api1.ts](extensions/git-base/src/api/api1.ts)\n- [extensions/git-base/src/api/extension.ts](extensions/git-base/src/api/extension.ts)\n- [extensions/git-base/src/api/git-base.d.ts](extensions/git-base/src/api/git-base.d.ts)\n- [extensions/git-base/src/decorators.ts](extensions/git-base/src/decorators.ts)\n- [extensions/git-base/src/extension.ts](extensions/git-base/src/extension.ts)\n- [extensions/git-base/src/foldingProvider.ts](extensions/git-base/src/foldingProvider.ts)\n- [extensions/git-base/src/model.ts](extensions/git-base/src/model.ts)\n- [extensions/git-base/src/remoteProvider.ts](extensions/git-base/src/remoteProvider.ts)\n- [extensions/git-base/src/remoteSource.ts](extensions/git-base/src/remoteSource.ts)\n- [extensions/git-base/src/test/foldingProvider.test.ts](extensions/git-base/src/test/foldingProvider.test.ts)\n- [extensions/git-base/src/util.ts](extensions/git-base/src/util.ts)\n- [extensions/grunt/esbuild.mts](extensions/grunt/esbuild.mts)\n- [extensions/grunt/src/main.ts](extensions/grunt/src/main.ts)\n- [extensions/gulp/esbuild.mts](extensions/gulp/esbuild.mts)\n- [extensions/gulp/src/main.ts](extensions/gulp/src/main.ts)\n</cite>\n\nVS Code includes a curated set of builtin extensions that provide language support, version control integration, and task automation. These extensions are published under the `vscode` publisher namespace and use consistent version numbering across releases.\n\n## Language Support Extensions\n\nVS Code bundles syntax highlighting, language configuration, and snippets for several programming languages and file formats.\n\n### Batch Files\n\nThe Batch extension provides language support for batch script files (`.bat`, `.cmd`). It contributes TextMate syntax highlighting from the mmims language-batchfile grammar, language configuration rules, and code snippets. The extension is categorized as a Programming Language extension and maintains compatibility with VS Code 1.52.0 and later.\n\n### Clojure\n\nThe Clojure extension supports Clojure syntax across multiple file extensions (`.clj`, `.cljs`, `.cljc`, `.cljx`, `.clojure`, `.edn`). It provides grammar support sourced from the atom/language-clojure repository and includes configuration that disables trim whitespace diffing for consistent formatting in Clojure projects. The extension has no minimum engine version requirement.\n\n### CoffeeScript\n\nThe CoffeeScript extension supports CoffeeScript source files (`.coffee`, `.cson`, `.iced`) with full language integration including syntax highlighting, language configuration, breakpoint support, and code snippets. Configuration defaults disable trim whitespace diffing and color decorators to match CoffeeScript conventions. Like Clojure, this extension has no minimum engine version constraint.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/bat/package.json:L1-L48](extensions/bat/package.json#L1-L48)\n- [extensions/clojure/package.json:L1-L51](extensions/clojure/package.json#L1-L51)\n- [extensions/coffeescript/package.json:L1-L61](extensions/coffeescript/package.json#L1-L61)\n\n## Git Integration\n\nThe git-base extension provides foundational Git functionality and APIs for other extensions to register remote source providers and query Git repositories.\n\n### Remote Source Provider API\n\nThe git-base extension exposes a versioned API (currently version 1) for extensions to register remote source providers. The `RemoteSourceProvider` interface allows providers to supply repository browsing, branch lookup, and action execution. The API supports both programmatic access via `git-base.api.getRemoteSources` command and UI-driven selection through the `pickRemoteSource` function.\n\nProviders can implement optional methods including `getRemoteSources` for querying repositories, `getBranches` for fetching branch lists, `getRecentRemoteSources` for history, and `getRemoteSourceActions` for custom branch actions. The quick-pick UI includes debounced search with a 300ms delay for responsive filtering and displays recent sources sorted by timestamp.\n\n### Git Commit Folding\n\nThe `GitCommitFoldingProvider` enables code folding in git commit message buffers. It recognizes two folding regions: comment blocks (lines starting with `#`) and diff sections (lines starting with `diff --git`). The provider requires at least two consecutive lines to create a fold, ensuring single-line blocks remain unfolded. Comment blocks are marked with `FoldingRangeKind.Comment` for semantic distinction. The implementation handles end-of-document scenarios and mixed comment/diff content, as demonstrated in comprehensive test coverage.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/git-base/src/api/git-base.d.ts:L9-L85](extensions/git-base/src/api/git-base.d.ts#L9-L85)\n- [extensions/git-base/src/remoteSource.ts:L57-L110](extensions/git-base/src/remoteSource.ts#L57-L110)\n- [extensions/git-base/src/foldingProvider.ts:L8-L92](extensions/git-base/src/foldingProvider.ts#L8-L92)\n\n## Build Task Runners\n\nVS Code includes extensions that automatically detect and integrate with Grunt and Gulp task runners, surfacing available tasks in the Task Explorer.\n\n### Grunt Auto-Detection\n\nThe Grunt extension monitors workspace folders for `gruntfile.js` or `Gruntfile.js` files and automatically discovers available tasks by parsing `grunt --help` output. The `FolderDetector` class watches for changes to Gruntfile and node_modules directory, caching discovered tasks until file changes invalidate the cache. Tasks are categorized as build tasks (containing keywords \"build\", \"compile\", or \"watch\") or test tasks (containing \"test\"), with proper `TaskGroup` assignment. The extension respects the `grunt.autoDetect` configuration setting and can be enabled or disabled per workspace folder.\n\n### Gulp Auto-Detection\n\nThe Gulp extension uses a similar pattern to Grunt, with file monitoring and task discovery integrated into the VS Code task system. Both extensions spawn task runner subprocesses to enumerate available tasks and resolve task execution through `ShellExecution` with the detected runner command. Gulp and Grunt both use platform detection to locate the correct executable (`.cmd` on Windows, shell script on POSIX systems).\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/grunt/src/main.ts:L85-L135](extensions/grunt/src/main.ts#L85-L135)\n- [extensions/gulp/src/main.ts:L52-L70](extensions/gulp/src/main.ts#L52-L70)\n\n## Extension Build System\n\nBuiltin extensions use esbuild for bundling and optimization. The shared build infrastructure enables consistent configuration across language and utility extensions.\n\nThe base build configuration (`esbuild-extension-common.mts`) establishes defaults: tree-shaking, minification, source maps, ES2024 target, and externalized `vscode` module. Extensions specify platform (`node` or `browser`), entry points, and optional overrides. Browser-platform extensions alias `path` to `path-browserify` and define process environment stubs to prevent Node.js APIs from leaking into browser contexts. The watch mode uses `@parcel/watcher` for low CPU overhead compared to esbuild's native file watching. Build output is debounced with a 100ms delay to batch multiple file changes into a single rebuild.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/esbuild-extension-common.mts:L16-L46](extensions/esbuild-extension-common.mts#L16-L46)\n- [extensions/esbuild-common.mts:L41-L102](extensions/esbuild-common.mts#L41-L102)\n"}, {"path": "extensions/copilot-extension.md", "title": "Copilot Extension", "parent": "extensions/extensions.md", "description": "AI-powered assistant including chat interface, inline completions, and language model integration.", "files": ["extensions/copilot/CHANGELOG.md", "extensions/copilot/README.md", "extensions/copilot/script/alternativeAction/index.ts", "extensions/copilot/script/cleanLog.ts", "extensions/copilot/script/compareStestAlternativeRuns.ts"], "markdown": "# Copilot Extension\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [extensions/copilot/CHANGELOG.md](extensions/copilot/CHANGELOG.md)\n- [extensions/copilot/README.md](extensions/copilot/README.md)\n- [extensions/copilot/script/alternativeAction/index.ts](extensions/copilot/script/alternativeAction/index.ts)\n- [extensions/copilot/script/cleanLog.ts](extensions/copilot/script/cleanLog.ts)\n- [extensions/copilot/script/compareStestAlternativeRuns.ts](extensions/copilot/script/compareStestAlternativeRuns.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## Overview\n\nGitHub Copilot is an AI-powered peer programming assistant integrated into VS Code. It provides autonomous agents that handle complete coding tasks end-to-end by planning work, editing files, running commands, and self-correcting when errors occur. The extension transforms development workflows through AI-driven code generation and task automation capabilities.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/copilot/README.md:L1-L10](extensions/copilot/README.md#L1-L10)\n\n## Autonomous Agents\n\nCopilot agents operate across multiple execution environments. Local agents run directly in VS Code, while background agents execute through Copilot CLI and cloud agents operate via GitHub's cloud infrastructure. Agents are managed from a centralized Agent Sessions view that tracks active sessions, displays file changes, and monitors progress across all environments.\n\nThe Plan agent breaks complex tasks into structured implementation plans before code generation begins. Agents support custom personas defined through `.github/agents` or `.claude/agents` directories, enabling team-specific coding standards and conventions. When local sessions encounter file conflicts during parallel task execution, agents can run in isolated Git worktrees to maintain independence.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/copilot/README.md:L18-L28](extensions/copilot/README.md#L18-L28)\n- [extensions/copilot/CHANGELOG.md:L257-L263](extensions/copilot/CHANGELOG.md#L257-L263)\n\n## Chat Interface and Inline Editing\n\nThe Chat view accepts multi-turn conversations with tool use, embeddings-based tool selection for users with 100+ tools, and support for reasoning tokens in Claude models. Inline chat, triggered with `Ctrl+I`/`Cmd+I`, enables focused edits directly in the editor for single-file code changes.\n\nGhost text suggestions appear as users type, predicting the next logical code change. Next Edit Suggestions (NES) recommend edits anywhere in the file, not just near the cursor, with configurable eagerness settings to balance suggestion frequency against relevance. The language models editor provides centralized management of available models, model visibility settings, and provider configuration.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/copilot/README.md:L30-L39](extensions/copilot/README.md#L30-L39)\n- [extensions/copilot/CHANGELOG.md:L265-L276](extensions/copilot/CHANGELOG.md#L265-L276)\n\n## Customization and Extensibility\n\nCopilot adapts to project-specific needs through custom instructions, skills, and MCP server integration. Custom instructions specify project conventions and coding guidelines, while agent skills teach specialized domain knowledge without modifying the core agent. Organizations can share custom agents across GitHub teams, and extensions distribute skills via the `chatSkills` contribution point.\n\nThe extension reads Claude configuration files directly from `CLAUDE.md` instructions and `.claude/agents` and `.claude/skills` directories, ensuring compatibility between VS Code and Claude Code environments. MCP (Model Context Protocol) servers extend agents with access to external tools, APIs, and data sources. Teams can configure private MCP registries and install MCP servers to workspace `.vscode/mcp.json` for shared use.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/copilot/README.md:L41-L50](extensions/copilot/README.md#L41-L50)\n- [extensions/copilot/CHANGELOG.md:L127-L150](extensions/copilot/CHANGELOG.md#L127-L150)\n\n## Language Support and Version Compatibility\n\nGitHub Copilot works with any language, including Java, PHP, Python, JavaScript, Ruby, Go, C#, and C++. The extension supports most popular languages, libraries, and frameworks due to its training on public repositories.\n\nCopilot Chat releases synchronously with VS Code due to deep UI integration. Each new Copilot Chat version is compatible only with the latest VS Code release. Using an older VS Code version prevents access to the latest Copilot Chat features and models. Only the latest versions use upgraded models from the Copilot service, as even minor model improvements require prompt adjustments and extension fixes.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/copilot/README.md:L51-L59](extensions/copilot/README.md#L51-L59)\n\n## Development Utilities\n\nThe extension includes utility scripts in `script/` for processing inline edit telemetry and test comparisons. The `alternativeAction` script processes alternative edit recordings and suggestion data from CSV files, extracting scored edits for analysis. The `cleanLog.ts` utility filters log files by topic, stripping timestamps to highlight relevant entries.\n\nThe `compareStestAlternativeRuns.ts` script aggregates test results by signal kind, provider, and test name, supporting colored output and pass/fail ratio tracking for inline suggestion quality metrics. These tools assist development teams in analyzing suggestion performance and debugging model behavior across different inline editing strategies.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/copilot/script/alternativeAction/index.ts:L1-L52](extensions/copilot/script/alternativeAction/index.ts#L1-L52)\n- [extensions/copilot/script/cleanLog.ts:L1-L31](extensions/copilot/script/cleanLog.ts#L1-L31)\n- [extensions/copilot/script/compareStestAlternativeRuns.ts:L71-L110](extensions/copilot/script/compareStestAlternativeRuns.ts#L71-L110)\n"}, {"path": "extensions/language-support.md", "title": "Language Support Extensions", "parent": "extensions/extensions.md", "description": "Syntax highlighting, snippet support, and language configuration for major programming languages.", "files": ["extensions/bat/language-configuration.json", "extensions/clojure/language-configuration.json", "extensions/debug-server-ready/esbuild.mts", "extensions/debug-server-ready/src/extension.ts", "extensions/esbuild-webview-common.mts", "extensions/github-authentication/esbuild.browser.mts", "extensions/github-authentication/esbuild.mts", "extensions/github-authentication/src/browser/authServer.ts", "extensions/github-authentication/src/browser/buffer.ts", "extensions/github-authentication/src/browser/crypto.ts", "extensions/github-authentication/src/browser/fetch.ts", "extensions/github-authentication/src/common/accountLinks.ts", "extensions/github-authentication/src/common/enterpriseConfiguration.ts", "extensions/github-authentication/src/common/enterpriseStorage.ts", "extensions/github-authentication/src/common/env.ts", "extensions/github-authentication/src/common/errors.ts", "extensions/github-authentication/src/common/experimentationService.ts", "extensions/github-authentication/src/common/gitHubAccount.ts", "extensions/github-authentication/src/common/http.ts", "extensions/github-authentication/src/common/keychain.ts", "extensions/github-authentication/src/common/logger.ts", "extensions/github-authentication/src/common/microsoftAuthentication.ts", "extensions/github-authentication/src/common/notification.ts", "extensions/github-authentication/src/common/utils.ts", "extensions/github-authentication/src/config.ts", "extensions/github-authentication/src/entraTokenExchange.ts"], "markdown": "# Language Support Extensions\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [extensions/bat/language-configuration.json](extensions/bat/language-configuration.json)\n- [extensions/clojure/language-configuration.json](extensions/clojure/language-configuration.json)\n- [extensions/debug-server-ready/esbuild.mts](extensions/debug-server-ready/esbuild.mts)\n- [extensions/debug-server-ready/src/extension.ts](extensions/debug-server-ready/src/extension.ts)\n- [extensions/esbuild-webview-common.mts](extensions/esbuild-webview-common.mts)\n- [extensions/github-authentication/esbuild.browser.mts](extensions/github-authentication/esbuild.browser.mts)\n- [extensions/github-authentication/esbuild.mts](extensions/github-authentication/esbuild.mts)\n- [extensions/github-authentication/src/browser/authServer.ts](extensions/github-authentication/src/browser/authServer.ts)\n- [extensions/github-authentication/src/browser/buffer.ts](extensions/github-authentication/src/browser/buffer.ts)\n- [extensions/github-authentication/src/browser/crypto.ts](extensions/github-authentication/src/browser/crypto.ts)\n- [extensions/github-authentication/src/browser/fetch.ts](extensions/github-authentication/src/browser/fetch.ts)\n- [extensions/github-authentication/src/common/accountLinks.ts](extensions/github-authentication/src/common/accountLinks.ts)\n- [extensions/github-authentication/src/common/enterpriseConfiguration.ts](extensions/github-authentication/src/common/enterpriseConfiguration.ts)\n- [extensions/github-authentication/src/common/enterpriseStorage.ts](extensions/github-authentication/src/common/enterpriseStorage.ts)\n- [extensions/github-authentication/src/common/env.ts](extensions/github-authentication/src/common/env.ts)\n- [extensions/github-authentication/src/common/errors.ts](extensions/github-authentication/src/common/errors.ts)\n- [extensions/github-authentication/src/common/experimentationService.ts](extensions/github-authentication/src/common/experimentationService.ts)\n- [extensions/github-authentication/src/common/gitHubAccount.ts](extensions/github-authentication/src/common/gitHubAccount.ts)\n- [extensions/github-authentication/src/common/http.ts](extensions/github-authentication/src/common/http.ts)\n- [extensions/github-authentication/src/common/keychain.ts](extensions/github-authentication/src/common/keychain.ts)\n- [extensions/github-authentication/src/common/logger.ts](extensions/github-authentication/src/common/logger.ts)\n- [extensions/github-authentication/src/common/microsoftAuthentication.ts](extensions/github-authentication/src/common/microsoftAuthentication.ts)\n- [extensions/github-authentication/src/common/notification.ts](extensions/github-authentication/src/common/notification.ts)\n- [extensions/github-authentication/src/common/utils.ts](extensions/github-authentication/src/common/utils.ts)\n- [extensions/github-authentication/src/config.ts](extensions/github-authentication/src/config.ts)\n- [extensions/github-authentication/src/entraTokenExchange.ts](extensions/github-authentication/src/entraTokenExchange.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## Language Configuration Structure\n\nVS Code's built-in language support extensions define language-specific behaviors through `language-configuration.json` files. Each language extension maintains a standardized configuration structure that controls editor features like bracket matching, auto-closing pairs, comment handling, and code folding. The configuration system provides language authors a declarative way to describe syntax and editor interaction patterns without requiring custom extension code.\n\nLanguage extensions typically organize resources in a consistent directory structure with separate directories for syntax definitions (TextMate grammars), code snippets, and configuration files. Each language extension includes a `package.json` manifest for metadata and contribution registration, enabling VS Code to discover and activate the appropriate language support when files of that type are opened.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/bat/language-configuration.json:L1-L29](extensions/bat/language-configuration.json#L1-L29)\n- [extensions/clojure/language-configuration.json:L1-L25](extensions/clojure/language-configuration.json#L1-L25)\n\n## Comment Support Configuration\n\nLanguage configurations specify how comments are handled in each language. The Batch language (`extensions/bat/language-configuration.json`) defines `@REM ` as the line comment marker, reflecting Batch's native comment syntax. Clojure (`extensions/clojure/language-configuration.json`) uses `;;` as its line comment marker, consistent with Lisp family conventions. These comment definitions enable the editor's comment toggle and line-comment operations.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/bat/language-configuration.json:L2-L4](extensions/bat/language-configuration.json#L2-L4)\n- [extensions/clojure/language-configuration.json:L2-L4](extensions/clojure/language-configuration.json#L2-L4)\n\n## Bracket Matching and Pair Handling\n\nLanguage configurations define three bracket-related structures: recognized bracket pairs for matching, auto-closing pairs that the editor automatically completes, and surrounding pairs for wrapping selected text. Both Batch and Clojure recognize the same bracket types—curly braces, square brackets, and parentheses—allowing the editor to provide navigation and highlighting features.\n\nBatch adds environment variable delimiters (`%`, `%`) as surrounding pairs and quotes as an auto-closing pair excluding the string context to prevent nested closure issues. Clojure similarly handles quotes (`\"`, `\"`) as auto-closing pairs with string context exclusion. These configurations ensure that bracket-related editing operations behave predictably within each language's syntax rules.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/bat/language-configuration.json:L5-L22](extensions/bat/language-configuration.json#L5-L22)\n- [extensions/clojure/language-configuration.json:L5-L21](extensions/clojure/language-configuration.json#L5-L21)\n\n## Code Folding Configuration\n\nCode folding configuration determines how the editor identifies foldable regions in code. Batch uses explicit region markers with the pattern `#region` and `#endregion`, allowing developers to manually demarcate foldable blocks. The regex pattern matches comment-prefixed markers (using either `::` or `@REM`/`@rem`), supporting both comment styles.\n\nClojure takes a different approach, using `offSide: true` to enable offset-based folding. This strategy leverages indentation changes to identify structural boundaries, aligning with the typical formatting conventions of Lisp-family languages where nested expressions are indentation-delimited. This reduces the need for explicit markers while still providing intuitive fold regions.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/bat/language-configuration.json:L23-L28](extensions/bat/language-configuration.json#L23-L28)\n- [extensions/clojure/language-configuration.json:L22-L24](extensions/clojure/language-configuration.json#L22-L24)\n"}, {"path": "extensions/api-reference.md", "title": "Extension API", "parent": "extensions/extensions.md", "description": "VS Code Extension API providing access to editor, workspace, UI features, and service integrations.", "files": ["extensions/copilot/src/extension/api/vscode/api.d.ts", "extensions/copilot/chat-lib/script/postinstall.ts", "extensions/copilot/chat-lib/vitest.config.ts", "extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/node/adapters/anthropicAdapter.ts", "extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/node/adapters/types.ts", "extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/node/langModelServer.ts", "extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/node/test/mockLanguageModelServer.ts", "extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/agentTypes.ts", "extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/askAgentProvider.ts", "extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/cachedAgentFileWriter.ts", "extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/exploreAgentProvider.ts", "extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/githubOrgChatResourcesService.ts"], "markdown": "# Extension API\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [extensions/copilot/src/extension/api/vscode/api.d.ts](extensions/copilot/src/extension/api/vscode/api.d.ts)\n- [extensions/copilot/chat-lib/script/postinstall.ts](extensions/copilot/chat-lib/script/postinstall.ts)\n- [extensions/copilot/chat-lib/vitest.config.ts](extensions/copilot/chat-lib/vitest.config.ts)\n- [extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/node/adapters/anthropicAdapter.ts](extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/node/adapters/anthropicAdapter.ts)\n- [extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/node/adapters/types.ts](extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/node/adapters/types.ts)\n- [extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/node/langModelServer.ts](extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/node/langModelServer.ts)\n- [extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/node/test/mockLanguageModelServer.ts](extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/node/test/mockLanguageModelServer.ts)\n- [extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/agentTypes.ts](extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/agentTypes.ts)\n- [extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/askAgentProvider.ts](extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/askAgentProvider.ts)\n- [extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/cachedAgentFileWriter.ts](extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/cachedAgentFileWriter.ts)\n- [extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/exploreAgentProvider.ts](extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/exploreAgentProvider.ts)\n- [extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/githubOrgChatResourcesService.ts](extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/githubOrgChatResourcesService.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## Copilot Extension API\n\nThe Copilot extension exposes a public API for interacting with editor and workspace features through the `CopilotExtensionApi` interface. The primary method is `selectScope()`, which allows consumers to present a scope selection dialog to the user. This method accepts an optional `TextEditor` parameter to specify a target editor context and an optional `options` object containing a `reason` string displayed in the placeholder hint. The method returns a Promise resolving to a `Selection` object or `undefined` if the user cancels the operation.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/copilot/src/extension/api/vscode/api.d.ts:L1-L20](extensions/copilot/src/extension/api/vscode/api.d.ts#L1-L20)\n\n## Language Model Server\n\nThe `LanguageModelServer` provides an HTTP-based interface for handling chat requests and routing them to available language model endpoints. Located in `extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/node/langModelServer.ts`, the server listens on localhost with a randomly allocated port and uses nonce-based authentication to verify requests.\n\nThe server accepts POST requests at `/v1/messages` and uses protocol-specific adapters to parse and format responses. When a chat request arrives, the server validates the authentication key, selects an appropriate language model endpoint from configured providers, and streams responses as server-sent events. The server manages request lifecycle through cancellation tokens, handling client disconnections gracefully. Response streaming includes content blocks, text deltas, and tool call invocations, with automatic token usage tracking and context window adjustment to normalize model capability perception across different provider implementations.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/node/langModelServer.ts:L20-L50](extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/node/langModelServer.ts#L20-L50)\n- [extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/node/langModelServer.ts:L143-L200](extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/node/langModelServer.ts#L143-L200)\n\n## Protocol Adapters\n\nProtocol adapters bridge different API formats and VS Code's internal chat message representation. The `IProtocolAdapter` interface defines methods for parsing requests, formatting stream responses, generating initial and final events, extracting authentication credentials, and providing content type headers.\n\nThe `AnthropicAdapter` implementation converts Anthropic MessageStreamParams to VS Code's Raw chat message format, maps tools between protocols, and reformats streaming events. A key capability is token usage adjustment: the adapter scales token counts based on a configured context window size, allowing agents to perceive a uniform capability level regardless of underlying model constraints. This compensation factor prevents agents from modifying behavior due to perceived context window variations.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/node/adapters/types.ts:L11-L75](extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/node/adapters/types.ts#L11-L75)\n- [extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/node/adapters/anthropicAdapter.ts:L21-L75](extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/node/adapters/anthropicAdapter.ts#L21-L75)\n\n## Agent Configuration and Built-in Agents\n\nAgents are defined using `AgentConfig` type specifications containing name, description, argument hints, tool lists, and optional model overrides. The `buildAgentMarkdown()` utility in `extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/agentTypes.ts` generates agent manifests in YAML frontmatter format from these configuration objects.\n\nVS Code provides two built-in agents:\n\n- **Ask Agent** (`askAgentProvider.ts`): A read-only conversational assistant for answering questions, explaining code, and providing information without modifying files. It includes codebase search, file reading, and GitHub integration tools.\n\n- **Explore Agent** (`exploreAgentProvider.ts`): A read-only code research subagent for autonomous codebase exploration. It emphasizes parallelized search patterns and narrow-to-broad investigation strategies, with configurable model priority lists.\n\nBoth agents use `CachedAgentFileWriter` to store generated manifest files in the extension's global storage, with SHA-256 content hashing to prevent redundant file writes when configuration does not change.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/agentTypes.ts:L22-L56](extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/agentTypes.ts#L22-L56)\n- [extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/askAgentProvider.ts:L14-L74](extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/askAgentProvider.ts#L14-L74)\n- [extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/exploreAgentProvider.ts:L29-L82](extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/exploreAgentProvider.ts#L29-L82)\n\n## Chat Resources and Organization Management\n\nThe `IGitHubOrgChatResourcesService` interface manages organization-scoped chat resources including agents and custom instructions. The service automatically detects the preferred organization context from the current workspace's git repository remote URLs, Copilot sign-in organization membership, or the first organization the authenticated user belongs to.\n\nResources are cached locally in the extension's global storage under organization-specific directories. The service provides methods for reading, writing, and clearing cached files with optional change detection to avoid redundant writes. Organization names are sanitized for filesystem compatibility by converting to lowercase and replacing non-alphanumeric characters with underscores. The service also supports polling-based resource updates and handles authentication changes by invalidating cached organization preferences.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/githubOrgChatResourcesService.ts:L19-L57](extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/githubOrgChatResourcesService.ts#L19-L57)\n- [extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/githubOrgChatResourcesService.ts:L119-L174](extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/githubOrgChatResourcesService.ts#L119-L174)\n"}, {"path": "extensions/debugging-adapters.md", "title": "Debug Adapters", "parent": "extensions/extensions.md", "description": "Debug adapter protocol implementations and debugging extension infrastructure.", "files": [], "markdown": "# Debug Adapters\n\nVS Code implements the Debug Adapter Protocol (DAP) to provide a standardized interface for debugging support across different programming languages and runtime environments. The debugging infrastructure allows extensions to contribute debug adapters, debug sessions, breakpoints, and other debugging features while maintaining separation between the core VS Code editor and language-specific debugging implementations.\n\n## Debug Adapter Protocol\n\nThe Debug Adapter Protocol defines a JSON-RPC based communication protocol between VS Code and debug adapters. Debug adapters are either external processes or built-in components that communicate with debugged applications. This protocol enables VS Code to support multiple languages through a unified debugging interface without requiring language-specific debugging code in the editor itself.\n\nDebug adapters handle launching or attaching to debug targets, managing breakpoints, stepping through code execution, evaluating expressions, and providing stack traces and variable inspection. The protocol abstracts away the specifics of individual debuggers and runtimes, allowing extensions to provide debugging support by implementing DAP endpoints.\n\n## Debugging Extension Infrastructure\n\nThe debugging system in VS Code is modular and extensible through contributions. Extensions can register debug adapters, debug configuration providers, and debug console input handlers. The debugging infrastructure manages debug sessions, maintains state for active debugging targets, and coordinates between multiple debug adapters running simultaneously.\n\nExtensions contribute debugging features through their `package.json` manifest using the `debuggers` contribution point, which specifies the debug adapter executable, language configuration, and supported debug scenarios like launch and attach. Debug adapters registered through extensions can be spawned when debugging starts or attached to existing processes based on user configuration.\n\n## Configuration and Launch\n\nDebug launch configurations are stored in `launch.json` and define how to start debugging for different targets. Configuration providers allow extensions to generate or validate debug configurations dynamically. When a user initiates debugging, VS Code resolves the appropriate debug adapter for the configured language or runtime, establishes a connection, and begins exchanging DAP messages.\n\nThe debug adapter remains a separate process from VS Code, communicating through stdout/stdin pipes or network sockets. This isolation ensures that debug adapter crashes or misbehavior does not affect the VS Code editor itself, maintaining application stability while debugging complex applications.\n\n## Debug Console and Output\n\nThe debug console provides bidirectional communication between the user and the debugging session. Debug adapters can send output to VS Code's debug console, which displays logs, error messages, and debugging information. Extensions can register debug console input processors to handle custom commands or redirect console input to debug adapters in specialized ways.\n\nVS Code manages the debug console lifecycle alongside debug sessions, clearing console state between debugging runs and providing filtering options for different output types. The console preserves debugging history and allows users to interact with the debugged application through the debug console interface when the debug adapter supports console input evaluation.\n"}, {"path": "extensions/configuration-editing.md", "title": "Configuration Editing", "parent": "extensions/extensions.md", "description": "Extensions providing smart editing for JSON settings files with schema completion and validation.", "files": ["extensions/configuration-editing/package.json", "extensions/configuration-editing/esbuild.browser.mts", "extensions/configuration-editing/esbuild.mts", "extensions/configuration-editing/src/configurationEditingMain.ts", "extensions/configuration-editing/src/extensionsProposals.ts", "extensions/configuration-editing/src/importExportProfiles.ts", "extensions/configuration-editing/src/settingsDocumentHelper.ts", "extensions/configuration-editing/src/test/completion.test.ts", "extensions/configuration-editing/src/test/importExportProfiles.test.ts"], "markdown": "# Configuration Editing\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [extensions/configuration-editing/package.json](extensions/configuration-editing/package.json)\n- [extensions/configuration-editing/esbuild.browser.mts](extensions/configuration-editing/esbuild.browser.mts)\n- [extensions/configuration-editing/esbuild.mts](extensions/configuration-editing/esbuild.mts)\n- [extensions/configuration-editing/src/configurationEditingMain.ts](extensions/configuration-editing/src/configurationEditingMain.ts)\n- [extensions/configuration-editing/src/extensionsProposals.ts](extensions/configuration-editing/src/extensionsProposals.ts)\n- [extensions/configuration-editing/src/importExportProfiles.ts](extensions/configuration-editing/src/importExportProfiles.ts)\n- [extensions/configuration-editing/src/settingsDocumentHelper.ts](extensions/configuration-editing/src/settingsDocumentHelper.ts)\n- [extensions/configuration-editing/src/test/completion.test.ts](extensions/configuration-editing/src/test/completion.test.ts)\n- [extensions/configuration-editing/src/test/importExportProfiles.test.ts](extensions/configuration-editing/src/test/importExportProfiles.test.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Configuration Editing extension provides schema-aware completion and validation for VS Code's JSON configuration files. It enables intelligent editing of `settings.json`, `launch.json`, `tasks.json`, `keybindings.json`, and other configuration files through contextual completion proposals, inline variable suggestions, and document symbols. The extension runs in both desktop and browser environments, supporting virtual and untrusted workspaces.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/configuration-editing/package.json:L1-L40](extensions/configuration-editing/package.json#L1-L40)\n\n## Supported Configuration Files\n\nThe extension registers JSON validation schemas for a comprehensive set of VS Code configuration files at different scopes:\n\n| File | Locations |\n|---|---|\n| `settings.json` | User home, machine, profile, workspace folder (`/.vscode/`) |\n| `launch.json` | Workspace folder (`/.vscode/`) |\n| `tasks.json` | User home, workspace folder (`/.vscode/`) |\n| `keybindings.json` | User home, profile |\n| `extensions.json` | Workspace folder (`/.vscode/`) |\n| `argv.json` | Workspace root |\n| `mcp.json` | User home, profile, workspace folder |\n| `profiles.json` | User home |\n| `devcontainer.json` | Workspace root (external schema) |\n| `snippets/*.json` | User home, profile |\n| `chatLanguageModels.json` | User home, profile |\n\nThe extension activates on JSONC and JSON file types when opening recognized configuration filenames, and also on the `onProfile` activation event for profile operations.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/configuration-editing/package.json:L43-L192](extensions/configuration-editing/package.json#L43-L192)\n\n## Intelligent Completions for Settings\n\nThe `SettingsDocument` class in `settingsDocumentHelper.ts` provides context-aware completions for specific setting properties. For `window.title`, it suggests variables like `${activeEditorShort}`, `${folderPath}`, `${rootName}`, `${profileName}`, and `${dirty}`, each with descriptive text. The `files.associations` setting receives completion snippets for file glob patterns mapped to language identifiers. The `files.exclude`, `search.exclude`, and `explorer.autoRevealExclude` settings offer templated glob patterns for excluding files and folders by extension, by sibling name, or by folder hierarchy.\n\nLanguage-specific setting overrides using square-bracket syntax (e.g., `[python]`) receive language identifier completions. The `remote.portsAttributes` setting provides snippets for configuring port forwarding behavior with single ports, port ranges, or command-based matching. The `settingsSync.ignoredExtensions` and `remote.extensionKind` settings show available extensions filtered by those already configured.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/configuration-editing/src/settingsDocumentHelper.ts:L12-L190](extensions/configuration-editing/src/settingsDocumentHelper.ts#L12-L190)\n\n## Variable and Context Key Completions\n\nFor `launch.json` and `tasks.json`, the extension registers a completion provider that recognizes variable substitution contexts and suggests variables like `${workspaceFolder}`, `${workspaceFolderBasename}`, `${file}`, `${relativeFile}`, `${fileDirname}`, `${fileBasename}`, `${fileExtname}`, `${cwd}`, `${lineNumber}`, `${selectedText}`, `${defaultBuildTask}`, and `${extensionInstallFolder}`. Completions appear only inside string property values.\n\nContext key completions are registered for `keybindings.json` and `package.json` contribution points with `when` clauses. These are dynamically fetched via the `getContextKeyInfo` command and include context key names, their types, and descriptions for inline documentation.\n\nThe `launch.json` file also receives a document symbol provider that extracts configuration names and presents them as navigable symbols in the breadcrumb and outline.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/configuration-editing/src/configurationEditingMain.ts:L43-L246](extensions/configuration-editing/src/configurationEditingMain.ts#L43-L246)\n\n## Extension Recommendations and Profile Management\n\n### Extensions Completions\n\nThe `extensions.json` file and `extensions` arrays in workspace configurations receive completion proposals through the `provideInstalledExtensionProposals` function. When completing the `recommendations` array, the extension lists installed extensions (excluding builtin extensions by default) that are not already in the list, allowing users to quickly add extension recommendations.\n\n### GitHub Gist Profile Handler\n\nThe `GitHubGistProfileContentHandler` class implements VS Code's `ProfileContentHandler` API for saving and loading profiles via GitHub Gists. When saving a profile, it creates a private gist containing the profile JSON and returns the gist ID and URI. When reading a profile, it fetches the gist by ID or extracts the ID from a gist URL, validates the content (rejecting truncated or binary files), and returns the profile data. The handler uses the GitHub authentication provider to obtain user credentials and manages cancellation through `CancellationToken`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/configuration-editing/src/extensionsProposals.ts:L9-L32](extensions/configuration-editing/src/extensionsProposals.ts#L9-L32)\n- [extensions/configuration-editing/src/importExportProfiles.ts:L16-L81](extensions/configuration-editing/src/importExportProfiles.ts#L16-L81)\n\n## Architecture and Build Configuration\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    A[\"configurationEditingMain.ts<br/>Extension Activation\"] -->|registers| B[\"Settings Completions\"]\n    A -->|registers| C[\"Variable Completions<br/>launch.json, tasks.json\"]\n    A -->|registers| D[\"Context Key Completions<br/>keybindings.json, package.json\"]\n    A -->|registers| E[\"Launch Document<br/>Symbols\"]\n    A -->|registers| F[\"Extensions<br/>Completions\"]\n    A -->|registers| G[\"GitHub Gist<br/>Profile Handler\"]\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [extensions/configuration-editing/src/configurationEditingMain.ts:L12-L33](extensions/configuration-editing/src/configurationEditingMain.ts#L12-L33)\n\nThe extension maintains separate build pipelines for desktop and browser platforms. The `esbuild.mts` script compiles the Node.js entry point, while `esbuild.browser.mts` bundles the browser distribution with a separate TypeScript configuration. Both target `configurationEditingMain.ts` as the entry point and output to their respective `dist/` directories.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/configuration-editing/esbuild.mts:L1-L18](extensions/configuration-editing/esbuild.mts#L1-L18)\n- [extensions/configuration-editing/esbuild.browser.mts:L1-L21](extensions/configuration-editing/esbuild.browser.mts#L1-L21)\n"}, {"path": "extensions/git-integration.md", "title": "Git Integration", "parent": "extensions/extensions.md", "description": "Source control management with Git including status, diff, staging, history, and branching operations.", "files": ["extensions/git/package.json", "extensions/debug-auto-launch/esbuild.mts", "extensions/debug-auto-launch/src/extension.ts", "extensions/esbuild-common.test.mts", "extensions/extension-editing/esbuild.browser.mts", "extensions/extension-editing/esbuild.mts", "extensions/extension-editing/src/constants.ts", "extensions/extension-editing/src/extensionEditingBrowserMain.ts", "extensions/extension-editing/src/extensionEditingMain.ts", "extensions/extension-editing/src/extensionEngineValidation.ts", "extensions/extension-editing/src/extensionLinter.ts"], "markdown": "# Git Integration\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [extensions/git/package.json](extensions/git/package.json)\n- [extensions/debug-auto-launch/esbuild.mts](extensions/debug-auto-launch/esbuild.mts)\n- [extensions/debug-auto-launch/src/extension.ts](extensions/debug-auto-launch/src/extension.ts)\n- [extensions/esbuild-common.test.mts](extensions/esbuild-common.test.mts)\n- [extensions/extension-editing/esbuild.browser.mts](extensions/extension-editing/esbuild.browser.mts)\n- [extensions/extension-editing/esbuild.mts](extensions/extension-editing/esbuild.mts)\n- [extensions/extension-editing/src/constants.ts](extensions/extension-editing/src/constants.ts)\n- [extensions/extension-editing/src/extensionEditingBrowserMain.ts](extensions/extension-editing/src/extensionEditingBrowserMain.ts)\n- [extensions/extension-editing/src/extensionEditingMain.ts](extensions/extension-editing/src/extensionEditingMain.ts)\n- [extensions/extension-editing/src/extensionEngineValidation.ts](extensions/extension-editing/src/extensionEngineValidation.ts)\n- [extensions/extension-editing/src/extensionLinter.ts](extensions/extension-editing/src/extensionLinter.ts)\n</cite>\n\nVS Code's Git integration is provided by the Git extension (`extensions/git/`), which manages source control operations directly within the editor. The extension enables developers to perform repository operations, track changes, stage and commit modifications, manage branches, and synchronize with remote repositories without leaving the development environment.\n\n## Repository Management\n\nThe Git extension provides commands for initializing repositories and managing repository instances. `git.init` creates a new repository in the workspace, while `git.clone` and `git.cloneRecursive` enable cloning existing repositories from remote sources. Once repositories are open, `git.openRepository` allows adding additional repositories to the workspace, and `git.close` or `git.closeOtherRepositories` removes repositories from the active workspace.\n\nFor developers working with Git worktrees, `git.createWorktree` creates a new working tree, `git.openWorktree` and `git.openWorktreeInNewWindow` switch between worktrees, and `git.deleteWorktree` removes them. The `git.refresh` command manually updates the repository status view to reflect the latest state of the working directory.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/git/package.json:L85-L146](extensions/git/package.json#L85-L146)\n\n## Staging and Committing\n\nThe staging system in Git integration allows granular control over which changes are included in a commit. The `git.stage` command stages individual changes, while `git.stageAll`, `git.stageAllTracked`, and `git.stageAllUntracked` provide bulk operations. `git.stageChange` stages specific file changes, `git.stageFile` stages entire files, and `git.stageSelectedRanges` stages particular line selections. Corresponding `git.unstage*` and `git.revert*` commands reverse staging operations.\n\nCommit operations support multiple workflows through variations including `git.commit`, `git.commitAmend`, `git.commitSigned`, `git.commitStaged`, and `git.commitAll`. These can be combined with modifiers like `git.commitNoVerify` to bypass pre-commit hooks and `git.commitEmpty` to create commits without staged changes. The input box accepts commit messages that can be restored with `git.restoreCommitTemplate`, and completed commits can be undone with `git.undoCommit`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/git/package.json:L181-L482](extensions/git/package.json#L181-L482)\n\n## Branch and Reference Management\n\nBranch operations include creating, switching, renaming, and deleting branches. `git.branch` creates a new branch from the current HEAD, while `git.branchFrom` creates a branch from a specified starting point. `git.checkout` switches to an existing branch, and `git.checkoutDetached` checks out a specific commit in detached HEAD state. `git.renameBranch` renames the current branch, `git.deleteBranch` deletes local branches, and `git.deleteRemoteBranch` removes remote branches.\n\nTag management is handled through `git.createTag` to create annotated or lightweight tags, `git.deleteTag` for local deletion, and `git.deleteRemoteTag` for remote tag deletion. The Graph visualization supports tag operations through `git.graph.deleteTag`. Branch navigation in the history view is supported by `git.graph.checkout` and `git.graph.checkoutDetached` commands.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/git/package.json:L508-L609](extensions/git/package.json#L508-L609)\n\n## Remote Synchronization\n\nRemote operations enable fetching updates and pushing changes to remote repositories. `git.fetch` retrieves updates from the default remote, `git.fetchAll` from all remotes, and `git.fetchPrune` removes deleted remote references. `git.pull` integrates remote changes into the current branch, while `git.pullRebase` rebases instead of merging. `git.pullFrom` allows selecting a specific remote and branch.\n\nPush operations include `git.push` for the default remote, `git.pushForce` for forced pushes, and `git.pushTo` for pushing to a specified remote. `git.pushTags` publishes local tags, `git.pushWithTags` pushes commits and follows tags, and `git.pushWithTagsForce` combines forced push with tag following. The `git.sync` command performs pull followed by push in sequence, while `git.syncRebase` uses rebase during synchronization. Remote configuration is managed through `git.addRemote` and `git.removeRemote`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/git/package.json:L611-L758](extensions/git/package.json#L611-L758)\n\n## Advanced Operations\n\nMerge and rebase operations allow integrating changes from other branches. `git.merge` merges a specified branch into the current branch, `git.mergeAbort` cancels an in-progress merge when conflicts occur, and `git.rebase` rebases the current branch onto another. `git.cherryPick` applies specific commits to the current branch, with `git.cherryPickAbort` to cancel if needed.\n\nThe stash system temporarily saves uncommitted changes with `git.stash` and `git.stashIncludeUntracked`, which also captures untracked files. `git.stashStaged` stashes only staged changes (Git 2.35+), `git.stashPop` applies and removes a stash, `git.stashPopLatest` pops the most recent stash, and `git.stashApply` applies without removing. `git.stashPopEditor` and `git.stashApplyEditor` apply stashes through the editor interface.\n\nFile operations include `git.clean` and `git.cleanAll` to discard uncommitted changes, with variants for tracked or untracked files. `git.rename` renames files and stages the rename, `git.delete` removes tracked files, and `git.ignore` adds files to `.gitignore`. The extension also exposes `git.continueInLocalClone` for repository operations and `git.showOutput` to display the Git output panel.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/git/package.json:L544-L841](extensions/git/package.json#L544-L841)\n"}, {"path": "extensions/theme-and-icons.md", "title": "Themes and Icons", "parent": "extensions/extensions.md", "description": "Builtin color themes and icon themes providing the visual appearance of VS Code.", "files": ["extensions/theme-defaults/package.json", "extensions/theme-monokai-dimmed/package.json", "extensions/emmet/esbuild.browser.mts", "extensions/emmet/esbuild.mts", "extensions/emmet/src/abbreviationActions.ts", "extensions/emmet/src/balance.ts", "extensions/emmet/src/browser/emmetBrowserMain.ts", "extensions/emmet/src/bufferStream.ts", "extensions/emmet/src/defaultCompletionProvider.ts", "extensions/emmet/src/editPoint.ts", "extensions/emmet/src/emmetCommon.ts", "extensions/emmet/src/evaluateMathExpression.ts"], "markdown": "# Themes and Icons\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [extensions/theme-defaults/package.json](extensions/theme-defaults/package.json)\n- [extensions/theme-monokai-dimmed/package.json](extensions/theme-monokai-dimmed/package.json)\n- [extensions/emmet/esbuild.browser.mts](extensions/emmet/esbuild.browser.mts)\n- [extensions/emmet/esbuild.mts](extensions/emmet/esbuild.mts)\n- [extensions/emmet/src/abbreviationActions.ts](extensions/emmet/src/abbreviationActions.ts)\n- [extensions/emmet/src/balance.ts](extensions/emmet/src/balance.ts)\n- [extensions/emmet/src/browser/emmetBrowserMain.ts](extensions/emmet/src/browser/emmetBrowserMain.ts)\n- [extensions/emmet/src/bufferStream.ts](extensions/emmet/src/bufferStream.ts)\n- [extensions/emmet/src/defaultCompletionProvider.ts](extensions/emmet/src/defaultCompletionProvider.ts)\n- [extensions/emmet/src/editPoint.ts](extensions/emmet/src/editPoint.ts)\n- [extensions/emmet/src/emmetCommon.ts](extensions/emmet/src/emmetCommon.ts)\n- [extensions/emmet/src/evaluateMathExpression.ts](extensions/emmet/src/evaluateMathExpression.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## Built-in Color Themes\n\nVS Code provides a collection of built-in color themes that control the visual appearance of the editor and its interface. These themes are organized into two extension packages: `theme-defaults` and `theme-monokai-dimmed`.\n\nThe `theme-defaults` extension includes ten color themes categorized by their UI type. Light themes (`uiTheme: \"vs\"`) include Light 2026, Light+, Light Modern, and Visual Studio Light. Dark themes (`uiTheme: \"vs-dark\"`) include Dark 2026, Dark+, Dark Modern, Visual Studio Dark, and Monokai Dimmed. High contrast themes provide enhanced visibility: Default High Contrast (`uiTheme: \"hc-black\"`) and Default High Contrast Light (`uiTheme: \"hc-light\"`).\n\nEach theme definition specifies three properties: an `id` for internal reference, a `label` localized via i18n strings, and a `path` pointing to the theme's JSON color definition file. For example, Dark+ has id `\"Dark+\"` with label `\"%darkPlusColorThemeLabel%\"` and resolves to `./themes/dark_plus.json`. The UI theme type indicates the category to which the theme belongs and determines how it integrates with VS Code's overall interface styling.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/theme-defaults/package.json:L15-L75](extensions/theme-defaults/package.json#L15-L75)\n- [extensions/theme-monokai-dimmed/package.json:L12-L20](extensions/theme-monokai-dimmed/package.json#L12-L20)\n\n## UI Theme Categories\n\nVS Code organizes themes into four UI theme categories that govern the overall interface appearance beyond editor color schemes. The `vs` category represents light themes with a light background and dark text, used by Light 2026, Light+, Light Modern, and Visual Studio Light. The `vs-dark` category represents dark themes with a dark background and light text, used by Dark 2026, Dark+, Dark Modern, Visual Studio Dark, and Monokai Dimmed.\n\nHigh contrast themes serve accessibility purposes. The `hc-black` category provides enhanced contrast with a black background, while `hc-light` provides enhanced contrast with a light background. These categories ensure that themes not only define color palettes but also communicate their intended use case to VS Code's UI system.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/theme-defaults/package.json:L15-L75](extensions/theme-defaults/package.json#L15-L75)\n\n## Icon Themes\n\nIcon themes define the visual representation of files and folders in the Explorer. The `vs-minimal` icon theme is the sole built-in icon theme provided in the `theme-defaults` extension, referenced with id `\"vs-minimal\"` and label `\"%minimalIconThemeLabel%\"`. The theme definition points to `./fileicons/vs_minimal-icon-theme.json` for its icon specifications.\n\nIcon themes are contributed separately from color themes through the `iconThemes` extension point in `package.json`. Unlike color themes which define syntax highlighting and UI colors, icon themes provide customization of the file and folder icons displayed throughout the VS Code interface.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/theme-defaults/package.json:L77-L83](extensions/theme-defaults/package.json#L77-L83)\n\n## Theme Extension Structure\n\nBuilt-in themes are implemented as VS Code extensions located in the `extensions/` directory. The `theme-defaults` extension aggregates the most commonly used themes, while specialized themes like Monokai Dimmed are packaged separately in `extensions/theme-monokai-dimmed/`. Each theme extension defines its contributions in `package.json` with version 10.0.0, publisher `vscode`, and MIT license.\n\nThe extension manifest declares themes and icon themes through the `contributes` field, allowing VS Code to discover and register them on startup. Themes reference external JSON files in the `themes/` and `fileicons/` subdirectories relative to the extension root. This modular approach enables users to override default themes by installing extensions while maintaining a stable built-in theme set.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/theme-defaults/package.json:L1-L14](extensions/theme-defaults/package.json#L1-L14)\n- [extensions/theme-monokai-dimmed/package.json:L1-L11](extensions/theme-monokai-dimmed/package.json#L1-L11)\n"}, {"path": "modules/editor.md", "title": "Editor Module", "parent": "modules/modules.md", "description": "Core text editor including rendering, model management, selection, navigation, and editing operations.", "files": [], "markdown": "# Editor Module\n\nThe Editor Module (`src/vs/editor/`) is the core text editing engine of VS Code. It provides the foundation for all text editing functionality, including the base Monaco Editor that powers the code editor. The module handles text rendering, selection management, cursor movement, and all editing operations.\n\n## Architecture Overview\n\nThe Editor Module is organized into three main layers: the **common layer** contains language-agnostic model and operation definitions; the **browser layer** implements rendering and user interaction through the DOM; and the **standalone layer** provides the Monaco Editor public API. The module processes user input through controllers, applies changes to text models, maintains view state through a view model, and renders the result through GPU-accelerated or DOM-based rendering pipelines.\n\n## Core Components\n\n**TextModel** (`src/vs/editor/common/model/`) manages the document content and maintains document state including line structure, tokens, and decorations. It handles text insertions, deletions, and all edits through identifiable operations that support undo/redo. **View** (`src/vs/editor/browser/view.ts`) renders the visual representation by composing multiple view parts like `ViewLines`, `LineNumbers`, `Minimap`, and `EditorScrollbar`. **ViewController** mediates between user actions and model changes, coordinating cursor movement, selection, and edit operations while maintaining view scroll position and viewport alignment.\n\n## Text Operations and Selection\n\nEditing operations are expressed through the **ICommand** interface, which computes required edits and determines cursor state after modification. The cursor system manages primary and secondary selections with full support for multi-cursor editing. Selections track position changes automatically as the document is modified, using tracked ranges to maintain semantic relationships. Edit operations support markers and tracked ranges that adjust as content changes, enabling robust handling of complex editing scenarios like code completion with snippet insertion.\n\n## Visual Rendering Pipeline\n\nThe editor supports two rendering strategies: DOM-based rendering for compatibility and GPU-accelerated rendering for performance on large files. ViewParts are composable units that render specific visual elements—lines, numbers, decorations, cursors, gutters—each responding to model and configuration changes. The rendering pipeline uses `RenderingContext` to provide viewport information to view parts, enabling efficient partial updates. Decorations, selections, and line highlights use overlay systems that compose multiple visual effects.\n\n## Widget System and Extensions\n\nThe editor provides extensibility through widgets: **ContentWidgets** float above specific positions, **OverlayWidgets** are positioned absolutely, and **GlyphMarginWidgets** attach to the gutter. Widgets receive viewport updates and can be repositioned dynamically. The **IEditorAction** interface allows registering commands that modify editor state, while language features register through `ILanguageFeatureRegistry` for diagnostics, hover tooltips, completion, and formatting. This architecture allows the editor to remain decoupled from language services while providing rich editing experiences.\n"}, {"path": "modules/workbench.md", "title": "Workbench Services", "parent": "modules/modules.md", "description": "Workbench-level services providing layout, panel management, editor orchestration, and service lifecycle.", "files": [], "markdown": "# Workbench Services\n\nWorkbench services in VS Code provide the core infrastructure for layout management, panel orchestration, editor lifecycle, and service initialization. These services establish the foundational contract between the application shell and UI components, enabling a modular architecture where different subsystems communicate through well-defined service boundaries.\n\n## Service Lifecycle and Registration\n\nWorkbench services follow a dependency injection pattern where services are registered at application startup and become available to consumers through the service collection. Services typically expose initialization hooks, shutdown handlers, and observable state changes. The service lifecycle begins during workbench construction, where core services are instantiated in dependency order, and concludes with orderly teardown when the workbench closes or reloads.\n\n## Layout and Panel Management\n\nLayout services manage the organization of editor groups, side panels, and auxiliary views within the workbench. These services coordinate resizing operations, state persistence, and transitions between layout configurations. Panel management services provide registration and lifecycle control for auxiliary panels (e.g., Problems, Terminal, Output), enabling extensions to contribute their own panels while maintaining consistent behavior and storage.\n\n## Editor Orchestration\n\nEditor services orchestrate the lifecycle of editor instances, track active editors, and manage editor group operations including splitting, closing, and reordering. These services maintain the editor model, persist editor state across sessions, and expose events for editor open, close, and change operations. They act as the central hub for operations that span multiple editors or affect the overall editing experience.\n\n## Configuration and State Persistence\n\nWorkbench services integrate with the configuration system to track settings changes and propagate them to affected subsystems. State persistence services save workbench layout, panel states, and editor open state to storage, enabling restoration on subsequent sessions. These services balance eager persistence for critical state against performance considerations.\n\n## Service Availability and Extension Points\n\nServices are exposed to extensions through carefully controlled APIs, allowing extensions to integrate with workbench behavior without coupling to implementation details. Some services provide contribution points for registering new panel types, commands, or layout configurations. Services typically provide methods to query current state and subscribe to change events, enabling extensions to react to workbench state changes.\n"}, {"path": "modules/commands-keybindings.md", "title": "Commands and Keybindings", "parent": "modules/modules.md", "description": "Command dispatch system, keybinding resolution, and keyboard shortcut handling.", "files": [], "markdown": "# Commands and Keybindings\n\nVS Code's command and keybinding system provides the core mechanism for executing actions and mapping keyboard shortcuts to commands. Commands are registered in a central registry and can be invoked programmatically or through keybindings. Keybindings map keyboard input sequences to command execution, with support for multi-platform shortcuts, context-aware conditions, and weighted priority levels.\n\n## Command Registration\n\nCommands are registered through the `CommandsRegistry`, which maintains a map of command IDs to handlers. Each command consists of an identifier, a handler function, and optional metadata. The handler is invoked with a `ServicesAccessor` to access platform services and command arguments. Commands can include metadata describing their purpose, expected arguments, and return types, which is used by the Command Palette and API documentation. When a command is registered, the registry emits a `onDidRegisterCommand` event that listeners can observe for initialization or caching purposes.\n\n## Keybinding Resolution\n\nThe `KeybindingResolver` manages the mapping between keyboard input sequences and commands. It maintains two collections: default keybindings provided by the core and extensions, and user overrides. When a keyboard event occurs, the resolver matches the key sequence against its internal maps to determine which command to execute. The resolver supports multi-chord sequences, where complex shortcuts are built from multiple key combinations pressed in succession. Resolution results indicate whether a matching keybinding was found, more chords are needed to complete a sequence, or no match exists.\n\n## Keybinding Configuration\n\nKeybindings are defined as `IKeybindingRule` objects, specifying a command ID and platform-specific key combinations. Each platform—Windows, Linux, and macOS—can have distinct primary and secondary keybindings to accommodate different keyboard layouts and conventions. The keybinding registry accepts these rules and translates them into `IKeybindingItem` entries, which include encoded keybinding data and binding metadata. User keybindings override built-in defaults through the resolution process, allowing customization of the entire shortcut set.\n\n## Context-Aware Conditions\n\nKeybindings support conditional execution through context key expressions evaluated by the `IContextKeyService`. When a keybinding has a `when` clause, the resolver only matches it if the context condition evaluates to true. This enables command availability to depend on editor state, language mode, focus location, or custom conditions. Context expressions combine multiple conditions using logical operators, enabling sophisticated rules such as \"execute this command only when editing a TypeScript file and the editor is focused.\"\n\n## Priority and Extension Integration\n\nVS Code assigns weight levels to keybindings based on their source and scope, creating a priority hierarchy for conflict resolution. Core editor keybindings have the lowest weight, followed by workbench contributions, session contributions, built-in extensions, and external extensions at the highest weight. This weighting ensures that user keybindings and extension contributions can override defaults predictably. Extensions register keybindings through the same registry with their extension ID, enabling the system to track and validate bindings per extension.\n"}, {"path": "modules/settings.md", "title": "Settings Module", "parent": "modules/modules.md", "description": "User and workspace preferences, configuration file management, and settings UI.", "files": [], "markdown": "# Settings Module\n\nVS Code manages user and workspace preferences through the configuration-editing extension and related settings infrastructure. The settings system handles configuration file management, schema validation, and UI integration for VS Code's extensible preference system.\n\n## Configuration Editing Extension\n\nThe primary settings handling component resides in `extensions/configuration-editing/`, which provides tools for editing and managing VS Code's configuration files. The extension includes:\n\n- **Main entry point** (`configurationEditingMain.ts`) - Initializes the configuration editing functionality\n- **Settings document helper** (`settingsDocumentHelper.ts`) - Utility functions for working with settings documents\n- **Profile management** (`importExportProfiles.ts`) - Import and export functionality for settings profiles\n- **Extensions proposals** (`extensionsProposals.ts`) - Suggests extensions based on configuration context\n\n## Schema and Validation\n\nThe configuration-editing extension includes JSON schema files for validating configuration structures:\n\n| Schema File | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `devContainer.vscode.schema.json` | Validates VS Code-specific dev container configuration |\n| `devContainer.codespaces.schema.json` | Validates GitHub Codespaces dev container settings |\n| `attachContainer.schema.json` | Validates attach container configuration |\n\nThese schemas ensure configuration files conform to expected structures and enable IDE tooling like autocompletion and validation.\n\n## Profile Management\n\nThe settings system supports importing and exporting user profiles through dedicated functionality in `importExportProfiles.ts`. This enables users to share settings configurations, transfer preferences between machines, and version control their preferences.\n\n## Testing and Development\n\nThe extension includes comprehensive testing coverage:\n\n- `completion.test.ts` - Tests for settings and configuration autocompletion\n- `importExportProfiles.test.ts` - Tests for profile import/export operations\n- `index.ts` - Test setup and utilities\n\nThis test structure ensures settings operations remain reliable across updates.\n"}, {"path": "modules/scm.md", "title": "Source Control", "parent": "modules/modules.md", "description": "Version control integration with provider system and source control UI.", "files": [], "markdown": "# Source Control\n\nVS Code provides an extensible source control management (SCM) system that enables integration with version control providers and delivers a unified UI for version control operations. The architecture separates provider implementation from the user interface, allowing multiple VCS systems to coexist within a single editor instance.\n\n## Provider System\n\nThe source control system is built on a plugin architecture where individual VCS providers register themselves and expose their capabilities through standardized interfaces. Each provider manages its own repository state, working tree changes, and VCS-specific operations. The extension host in `src/workbench/api/common/extHostSCM.ts` mediates communication between extension-provided providers and the editor's core SCM infrastructure.\n\nProviders implement common operations such as staging, committing, branching, and merging through a consistent API surface. This design allows extensions for Git, Mercurial, Subversion, and other systems to integrate without modifying core VS Code code.\n\n## Source Control UI\n\nThe source control user interface consists of a source control view panel, change list display, and inline diff viewers. The UI layer subscribes to provider state changes and updates the displayed repository status, file changes, and branch information accordingly. Users interact with the VCS through context menu commands, quick actions, and the source control sidebar.\n\nThe view integrates with other VS Code features including:\n- Diff editor for reviewing changes\n- SCM input box for composing commit messages\n- Decorations on file explorer items indicating file status\n- Merge conflict resolution UI\n\n## Repository State Management\n\nEach SCM provider maintains representation of the working tree, staged changes, and commit history. The state model tracks whether repositories are clean or dirty, which files have uncommitted changes, and what merge or rebase operations may be in progress. This information drives UI updates and determines which operations are available at any given moment.\n\n## Integration with Extensions\n\nThe extension system allows third-party authors to create source control providers and extend existing ones. Extensions can contribute custom commands, merge strategies, and specialized UI components. The extension host provides necessary APIs for providers to register repositories, report state changes, and interact with the editor's file system and diff infrastructure.\n\nThe configuration editing extension at `extensions/configuration-editing/` supports editing VCS-related settings and configuration files, allowing users to customize provider behavior through settings and `.gitignore` or equivalent configuration files.\n"}, {"path": "modules/terminal.md", "title": "Terminal Integration", "parent": "modules/modules.md", "description": "Integrated terminal with PTY management, shell integration, and terminal UI.", "files": [], "markdown": "# Terminal Integration\n\nVS Code's integrated terminal provides a managed PTY (pseudo-terminal) environment with cross-platform support and shell-specific integration features. The implementation includes platform-specific PTY creation, shell detection and configuration, and terminal UI management through the main editor interface.\n\n## PTY Management\n\nPTY (pseudo-terminal) management is handled through platform-specific implementations in the `copilot_shim/` module. The codebase includes separate test coverage for PTY functionality (`copilot_shim/tests/pty.rs`) and Windows ConPTY (Console Pseudo-console) support (`copilot_shim/tests/conpty.rs`), indicating distinct handling for Unix-like and Windows terminal environments.\n\nPlatform-specific implementation files are located in `copilot_shim/src/platform/`, with separate modules for Unix (`copilot_shim/src/platform/unix.rs`) and Windows (`copilot_shim/src/platform/windows.rs`) terminal creation. This architecture allows VS Code to leverage native PTY capabilities on each platform while maintaining a unified interface for the editor.\n\n## Shell Integration\n\nShell integration features detect and configure the user's shell environment. The `copilot_shim/src/` directory contains shell detection and integration logic through files such as `identity.rs`, `command.rs`, and `runtime.rs`. Shell-specific setup routines are handled in `copilot_shim/src/setup/`, with platform-specific setup modules for Windows (`copilot_shim/src/setup/windows.rs`).\n\nThe codebase includes shell wrappers and bootstrap scripts for common shells located in `copilot_shim/tests/fixtures/legacy/`:\n- `posix-launcher.sh` for POSIX shells\n- `windows-bootstrapper.ps1` for PowerShell\n- `windows-wrapper.cmd` and `npm-wrapper.cmd` for Windows command environments\n- `git-bash-wrapper.sh` for Git Bash on Windows\n\nThese fixtures facilitate shell environment detection and feature injection.\n\n## CLI Terminal Support\n\nThe CLI component in `cli/src/` provides terminal-related utilities including command execution and pipe management. The `cli/src/async_pipe.rs` module handles asynchronous I/O operations for terminal communication, while `cli/src/commands.rs` manages CLI command routing and execution. Utility modules in `cli/src/util/` provide OS-specific terminal operations (`cli/src/util/os.rs`), process management (`cli/src/util/command.rs`), and I/O abstractions (`cli/src/util/io.rs`).\n\n## Terminal UI and Rendering\n\nThe terminal UI is integrated into the main editor through the `extensions/` directory structure. Configuration editing and schema support (`extensions/configuration-editing/`) enable terminal settings management and validation. The rendering and display of terminal content is managed through the main editor's workbench and view components.\n\n## Cross-Platform Architecture\n\n| Platform | PTY Implementation | Key Files |\n|----------|-------------------|-----------|\n| Windows | ConPTY (Windows 10+) | `copilot_shim/src/platform/windows.rs`, `copilot_shim/tests/conpty.rs` |\n| macOS | PTY syscalls | `copilot_shim/src/platform/unix.rs`, `copilot_shim/src/platform/mod.rs` |\n| Linux | PTY syscalls | `copilot_shim/src/platform/unix.rs`, `cli/src/util/os.rs` |\n\nThe platform abstraction layer allows terminal features to be implemented once at the editor level while leveraging OS-specific capabilities for performance and compatibility. Shell wrapper fixtures enable feature detection and injection across different shell environments without requiring shell modifications.\n"}, {"path": "modules/output-channel.md", "title": "Output Channel", "parent": "modules/modules.md", "description": "Output panel for displaying diagnostic messages and logs from tools and extensions.", "files": [], "markdown": "# Output Channel\n\nThe Output Channel is a core VS Code interface for displaying diagnostic messages, logs, and output from tools, extensions, and language servers. It provides a centralized panel where users can monitor activities, debug issues, and review execution results from various components running within the editor environment.\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Output Channel serves as a logging and diagnostic display system integrated into VS Code's user interface. Extensions and built-in tools use output channels to communicate asynchronous status updates, errors, warnings, and informational messages to users without interrupting the editing experience. Each channel maintains its own stream of messages, allowing users to switch between different sources of output for focused troubleshooting.\n\n## Channel Creation and Management\n\nExtensions create output channels to segregate their logging from other tools. The output channel system manages multiple named channels, each associated with a specific extension or tool, such as language servers, build tools, or debugging utilities. Users can create new channels and select which ones to view through the Output panel. The panel allows filtering and searching across messages, as well as clearing channel contents for fresh debugging sessions.\n\n## Message Types and Display\n\nOutput channels display various categories of messages including informational logs, warnings, errors, and debug traces. The formatting and presentation of these messages help users quickly identify issues. Timestamps can be associated with entries to track when events occurred. The Output panel preserves message history within a session, allowing users to scroll through previous output and correlate events.\n\n## Integration with Extensions\n\nThe Output Channel API enables extensions to programmatically write messages, making it accessible from code locations throughout the codebase. Extensions can append text to their designated channel, control channel visibility, and manage the output lifecycle. This integration appears throughout the extension ecosystem, particularly in language server implementations, debugging adapters, and diagnostic tools found in the `extensions/` directory.\n\n## Related Features\n\nOutput channels work alongside other VS Code diagnostic systems including the Debug Console, which displays debugging session output, and the Terminal, which shows command-line tool execution. The relationship between these components allows users to monitor different aspects of their development environment simultaneously, switching between panels based on the current task or issue being investigated.\n"}, {"path": "modules/notifications.md", "title": "Notifications", "parent": "modules/modules.md", "description": "Toast notifications, notification center, and user message handling infrastructure.", "files": [], "markdown": "# Notifications\n\n## Overview\n\nVS Code provides a comprehensive notification system to communicate important information, warnings, and errors to users. The notification infrastructure consists of toast notifications that appear temporarily at the bottom of the editor window and a persistent notification center that stores all notifications for later review. This system allows extensions and the core application to inform users about state changes, task completion, and actionable feedback without blocking the user's workflow.\n\n## Toast Notifications\n\nToast notifications are ephemeral messages that appear in the bottom-right corner of the editor. These notifications fade automatically after a short duration, typically displaying status updates, warnings, and errors. VS Code's toast notification system supports multiple severity levels including info, warning, and error. Each toast can include action buttons that users can click to perform contextual tasks. The notification API allows extensions to show toasts programmatically by calling the appropriate notification methods in the VS Code extension API.\n\n## Notification Center\n\nThe notification center provides a persistent record of all notifications generated during the VS Code session. Users can access the notification center through the notification icon in the status bar to review past messages and take actions on stored notifications. The notification center maintains notification history and allows users to dismiss individual notifications or clear the entire history. This persistent view ensures that users don't miss important information even if they close toasts too quickly or weren't focused on the editor when notifications appeared.\n\n## Configuration and Behavior\n\nVS Code allows users to customize notification behavior through settings and preferences. Users can control the notification level, determining which severity levels trigger notifications and which are silently suppressed. The notification system respects user preferences for do-not-disturb modes and allows customization of toast duration and position. Extensions can register custom notification handlers and define notification channels to organize notifications by category or source.\n\n## Extension Integration\n\nExtensions interact with the notification system through the VS Code extension API, which provides methods for showing notifications, handling user responses, and managing notification state. The API supports asynchronous notification display with promise-based return values for capturing user actions. Extensions should follow notification best practices by using appropriate severity levels, providing clear action buttons, and avoiding notification spam that could reduce effectiveness and user experience.\n"}, {"path": "ai/copilot-integration.md", "title": "Copilot Integration", "parent": "ai/ai.md", "description": "AI assistant integration including language model selection, context awareness, and suggestion handling.", "files": [], "markdown": "# Copilot Integration\n\nCopilot Integration in VS Code provides AI-assisted coding through language model backends, agent-based workflows, and context-aware suggestion handling. The system supports multiple language model providers via a \"Bring Your Own Key\" (BYOK) architecture and integrates agents for specialized tasks like planning, exploration, and code modification.\n\n## Language Model Selection\n\nVS Code Copilot supports multiple language model providers configured through the BYOK framework in `extensions/copilot/src/extension/byok/`. Each provider implements a consistent interface for model interactions:\n\n- **Anthropic** (`anthropicProvider.ts`) — Native Anthropic API integration\n- **Azure OpenAI** (`azureProvider.ts`) — Azure-hosted OpenAI models\n- **Google Gemini** (`geminiNativeProvider.ts`) — Gemini model integration\n- **OpenAI** (`openAIProvider.ts`, `gptLiveSession.ts`) — OpenAI GPT models\n- **OpenRouter** (`openRouterProvider.ts`) — Multi-model routing service\n- **Ollama** (`ollamaProvider.ts`) — Local model support\n- **xAI** (`xAIProvider.ts`) — xAI's Grok model\n\nEach provider is implemented in `vscode-node/` and handles authentication, endpoint configuration, model-specific message formatting, and function declaration conversion. Providers abstract protocol differences—for example, `geminiFunctionDeclarationConverter.ts` converts function schemas to Gemini's format, while `anthropicMessageConverter.ts` handles Anthropic-specific message transformations.\n\n## Agent Integration\n\nCopilot agents (`extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/`) provide specialized AI workflows:\n\n- **Ask Agent** (`askAgentProvider.ts`) — General question answering and code assistance\n- **Explore Agent** (`exploreAgentProvider.ts`) — Codebase exploration and navigation\n- **Plan Agent** (`planAgentProvider.ts`) — Feature planning and design workflows\n- **Custom Agents** (`githubOrgCustomAgentProvider.ts`) — Organization-specific agent definitions\n\nAgents run on a language model server (`langModelServer.ts`) that manages model selection and execution. The agent system is extensible, allowing new agent types to be registered and discovered through `agentTypes.ts` and contribution points.\n\n## Context Awareness\n\nContext providers make repository information, settings, and workspace state available to language models:\n\n- **Chat Session Context** (`chatSessionContextProvider.ts`) — Tracks conversation state and history\n- **VS Code Context** (`vscodeContextProviderApi.ts`) — Exposes editor selection, active document, diagnostics, and workspace configuration\n- **GitHub Organization Context** (`githubOrgChatResourcesService.ts`) — Integrates organizational resources and documentation\n\nThis context is passed to models during inference, enabling smarter suggestions based on the current editing context, open files, and project structure.\n\n## Chat and Suggestion Handling\n\nChat functionality (`extensions/copilot/src/extension/chat/`) manages user interactions and suggestion delivery:\n\n- **Chat Hooks** (`chatHookService.ts`) — Intercepts and customizes chat behavior through extensibility points\n- **Session Transcripts** (`sessionTranscriptService.ts`) — Maintains chat history for context window management\n- **Debug Logging** (`chatDebugFileLoggerService.ts`) — Logs chat interactions for troubleshooting\n- **Telemetry** (`chatHookTelemetry.ts`) — Tracks suggestion acceptance and usage patterns\n\nSuggestions are processed through hook services that allow extensions to modify model outputs before they appear in the editor, supporting suggestion filtering, ranking, and custom presentation logic.\n\n## Model Configuration and Storage\n\nBYOK model configuration is persisted through `byokStorageService.ts` and exposed via `byokModelInfo.ts`, which tracks:\n\n- Selected model provider and model ID\n- API keys and endpoint URLs\n- Provider-specific settings (temperature, max tokens, system prompts)\n- Usage statistics and telemetry\n\nConfiguration can be customized per workspace or globally, allowing developers to choose their preferred language model backend while maintaining consistency across chat and inline suggestion features.\n"}, {"path": "ai/chat-interface.md", "title": "Chat Interface", "parent": "ai/ai.md", "description": "Conversational AI chat interface with session management and conversation history.", "files": [], "markdown": "# Chat Interface\n\nVS Code's chat interface provides conversational AI interactions with integrated session and conversation history management. The implementation is centralized in the `extensions/copilot/` extension, with core chat functionality organized into dedicated modules for sessions, transcripts, hooks, and metadata storage.\n\n## Architecture Overview\n\nThe chat system is structured across two main areas: core chat services and session management. Chat-related functionality resides in `extensions/copilot/src/extension/chat/vscode-node/`, which includes services for session transcripts, debug logging, and hook integration. Session management is handled separately in `extensions/copilot/src/extension/chatSessions/`, which manages metadata, agent sessions, and conversation history persistence.\n\nA reusable chat library is maintained in `extensions/copilot/chat-lib/`, enabling consistent chat functionality across components and simplifying dependencies across the extension ecosystem.\n\n## Session Management\n\nSession management is handled through the `agentSessionsWorkspace` and `chatSessionMetadataStore` modules in `extensions/copilot/src/extension/chatSessions/common/`. These components coordinate:\n\n- **Session Metadata Storage**: The `chatSessionMetadataStore.ts` module maintains metadata about active and historical chat sessions, enabling persistence and recovery of session state.\n- **Agent Session Workspace**: The `agentSessionsWorkspace.ts` module organizes agent-specific sessions and coordinates multi-agent interactions within conversation contexts.\n\n## Conversation History and Transcripts\n\nThe `sessionTranscriptService` in `extensions/copilot/src/extension/chat/vscode-node/` manages conversation transcripts and history. This service handles recording, retrieval, and serialization of conversation messages, maintaining a queryable history of user interactions and AI responses throughout each session.\n\n## Hooks and Integration Points\n\nChat operations integrate with VS Code's hook system through `chatHookService.ts` and `chatHookTelemetry.ts`. These services enable extensibility for custom chat behaviors and telemetry collection. Related output handling is managed by `hooksOutputChannel.ts`, which directs hook execution results to appropriate output streams.\n\nDebug logging for chat interactions is provided by `chatDebugFileLoggerService.ts`, supporting development-time troubleshooting and issue diagnosis.\n\n## Quota and Resource Management\n\nChat usage is controlled through quota enforcement, managed by the `chatQuota.contribution.ts` module. This contribution point limits concurrent sessions and usage patterns while maintaining fair resource allocation across users and contexts.\n"}, {"path": "ai/language-models.md", "title": "Language Model Providers", "parent": "ai/ai.md", "description": "Language model provider registration, model selection, and API integration framework.", "files": [], "markdown": "# Language Model Providers\n\nVS Code provides a modular framework for integrating language model providers, supporting both official AI services and custom endpoints. The system enables extensible provider registration, model selection, and API integration through a contribution-based architecture.\n\n## Provider Architecture\n\nThe language model provider system is built on an abstract base pattern located in `extensions/copilot/src/extension/byok/vscode-node/abstractLanguageModelChatProvider.ts`. This abstraction defines the core interface that all provider implementations follow, ensuring consistency across different model services and enabling new providers to be added without modifying core infrastructure.\n\nProviders are organized by integration type:\n- **Official providers**: Azure, OpenAI, Anthropic, Google Gemini, xAI\n- **Community providers**: Ollama, OpenRouter\n- **Custom endpoints**: Support for user-defined API endpoints\n\n## Supported Providers\n\nThe framework includes implementations for multiple language model services:\n\n| Provider | File Path | Integration Type |\n|----------|-----------|------------------|\n| Anthropic | `anthropicProvider.ts` | Native |\n| OpenAI | `openAIProvider.ts` | Native |\n| Azure OpenAI | `azureProvider.ts` | Cloud |\n| Google Gemini | `geminiNativeProvider.ts` | Native |\n| Ollama | `ollamaProvider.ts` | Local |\n| OpenRouter | `openRouterProvider.ts` | Gateway |\n| xAI | `xAIProvider.ts` | Native |\n| Custom | `customEndpointProvider.ts` | User-defined |\n\n## BYOK (Bring Your Own Key) System\n\nThe `extensions/copilot/src/extension/byok/` directory implements the BYOK system for user-managed credentials and custom model configurations. This system decouples authentication from VS Code's internal infrastructure, allowing users to provide their own API keys and select preferred models.\n\nKey components include:\n\n- **Storage Service** (`byokStorageService.ts`): Manages secure credential storage\n- **Model Information** (`byokModelInfo.ts`): Tracks available models and capabilities\n- **Contribution System** (`byokContribution.ts`): Registers providers and their configurations\n- **Telemetry** (`byokOTelHelpers.ts`): OpenTelemetry integration for monitoring\n\n## Message Conversion\n\nProviders use model-specific message formats. The framework includes converters to normalize VS Code's internal message representation to each provider's protocol:\n\n- `anthropicMessageConverter.ts`: Anthropic API format\n- `geminiMessageConverter.ts`: Google Gemini format\n- `geminiFunctionDeclarationConverter.ts`: Gemini function calling schema\n\n## API Integration\n\nProvider implementations handle authentication and endpoint configuration:\n\n- **OpenAI Endpoint** (`openAIEndpoint.ts`): Direct API access\n- **Azure OpenAI Endpoint** (`azureOpenAIEndpoint.ts`): Azure service integration\n- **Custom Endpoint Provider** (`customEndpointProvider.ts`): User-defined API hosts\n\nEach provider encapsulates its specific authentication flow, error handling, and streaming response management, allowing independent updates to provider implementations without affecting the core system.\n"}, {"path": "ai/agents.md", "title": "Agent System", "parent": "ai/ai.md", "description": "AI agents for specialized tasks including planning, code exploration, and task automation.", "files": [], "markdown": "# Agent System\n\nVS Code's Agent System provides specialized AI agents for distinct development tasks. The system integrates with Copilot and leverages various language models through a unified interface, enabling agents to perform planning, code exploration, and task automation.\n\n## Agent Types\n\nThe agent system defines three primary agent categories, each serving a distinct purpose:\n\n- **Ask Agent** — Interactive question-answering for general queries and troubleshooting\n- **Explore Agent** — Code discovery and analysis for understanding repository structure and patterns\n- **Plan Agent** — Strategic planning for feature implementation and architectural decisions\n\nEach agent type is implemented as a provider in `extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/`, exposing specialized capabilities through dedicated provider classes (`askAgentProvider.ts`, `exploreAgentProvider.ts`, `planAgentProvider.ts`).\n\n## Language Model Integration\n\nThe Agent System abstracts language model interactions through `extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/node/langModelServer.ts`, which coordinates with multiple model providers:\n\n- Anthropic (via native adapter in `extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/node/adapters/anthropicAdapter.ts`)\n- Gemini\n- OpenAI\n- Azure OpenAI\n- Ollama\n- OpenRouter\n\nProvider implementations in `extensions/copilot/src/extension/byok/vscode-node/` handle authentication, endpoint configuration, and model-specific message formatting for each backend.\n\n## Agent Architecture\n\nAgent types are defined in `extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/agentTypes.ts`. The system uses a provider-based architecture where each agent type supplies its own tools, context, and execution model. The `cachedAgentFileWriter.ts` utility manages agent-generated file outputs, caching writes to avoid redundant operations.\n\nGitHub organization integration is supported through `githubOrgChatResourcesService.ts` and `githubOrgCustomAgentProvider.ts`, enabling agents to access repository data and custom configurations stored in GitHub organizations.\n\n## Prompt Management\n\nAgent prompts are stored as markdown files in `extensions/copilot/assets/prompts/`, including core prompts for `plan.prompt.md` and skill-specific prompts. Skills provide reusable agent capabilities and are defined in `extensions/copilot/assets/prompts/skills/` with metadata and reference documentation.\n\n## Instructions and Configuration\n\nVS Code project instructions for agent development are documented in `AGENTS.md` at the repository root, defining cancellation patterns, token management, and architectural guidelines for agent implementations.\n"}, {"path": "ai/byok.md", "title": "BYOK Providers", "parent": "ai/ai.md", "description": "Bring Your Own Key support for integrating with OpenAI, Azure, Anthropic, and Gemini services.", "files": [], "markdown": "# BYOK Providers\n\nVS Code supports Bring Your Own Key (BYOK) integration, allowing users to connect with multiple AI service providers using their own API credentials. The BYOK system enables integration with OpenAI, Azure OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini, and other services through a unified provider architecture.\n\n## Architecture Overview\n\nThe BYOK system is organized under `extensions/copilot/src/extension/byok/` with three main layers:\n\n**Common Layer** (`common/`): Shared functionality including message converters that normalize requests and responses across different provider protocols. This includes:\n- `byokProvider.ts` - Core provider abstraction\n- `anthropicMessageConverter.ts` - Anthropic protocol translation\n- `geminiMessageConverter.ts` and `geminiFunctionDeclarationConverter.ts` - Gemini protocol translation\n\n**Node Layer** (`node/`): Provider-specific endpoint implementations:\n- `openAIEndpoint.ts` - OpenAI API integration\n- `azureOpenAIEndpoint.ts` - Azure OpenAI endpoint configuration\n\n**VSCode Layer** (`vscode-node/`): VS Code-specific provider implementations and integration:\n- `abstractLanguageModelChatProvider.ts` - Base provider interface\n- Provider implementations: `anthropicProvider.ts`, `azureProvider.ts`, `openAIProvider.ts`, `geminiNativeProvider.ts`\n- `byokContribution.ts` - Integration with VS Code's language model API\n- `byokStorageService.ts` - Credential and configuration storage\n- `byokModelInfo.ts` - Provider model metadata\n- `byokOTelHelpers.ts` - Observability and telemetry support\n\n## Supported Providers\n\nThe BYOK system includes the following provider implementations:\n\n| Provider | File | Status |\n|----------|------|--------|\n| OpenAI | `openAIProvider.ts` | Full support |\n| Azure OpenAI | `azureProvider.ts` | Full support |\n| Anthropic | `anthropicProvider.ts` | Full support |\n| Gemini | `geminiNativeProvider.ts` | Full support |\n| Ollama | `ollamaProvider.ts` | Supported |\n| OpenRouter | `openRouterProvider.ts` | Supported |\n| xAI | `xAIProvider.ts` | Supported |\n| Custom Endpoints | `customEndpointProvider.ts`, `customOAIProvider.ts` | Supported |\n\n## Message Protocol Translation\n\nDifferent AI providers use distinct message and function declaration formats. The BYOK system handles protocol differences through specialized converters:\n\n- **Anthropic**: `anthropicMessageConverter.ts` translates between VS Code's standard message format and Anthropic's protocol\n- **Gemini**: `geminiMessageConverter.ts` and `geminiFunctionDeclarationConverter.ts` handle Google's protocol requirements\n\nThese converters ensure that tool calls, system prompts, and message roles are correctly formatted for each provider's API specifications.\n\n## Configuration and Storage\n\nProvider credentials and configuration are managed through:\n\n- `byokStorageService.ts` - Secure storage of API keys and endpoint configurations\n- `byokModelInfo.ts` - Model availability and capability metadata for each provider\n- `byokContribution.ts` - Registration and lifecycle management within VS Code's extension system\n\nThe separation of storage and configuration services allows for secure credential handling while maintaining flexibility in model selection and provider switching.\n\n## Integration Points\n\nThe BYOK system integrates with VS Code through multiple extension subsystems:\n\n- **Chat API**: Providers implement the language model chat interface\n- **Telemetry**: `byokOTelHelpers.ts` provides OpenTelemetry instrumentation\n- **Session Management**: `gptLiveSession.ts` handles per-session provider state\n- **Custom Endpoints**: Support for self-hosted or alternative endpoint configurations.\n"}, {"path": "development/build-system.md", "title": "Build System", "parent": "development/development.md", "description": "Gulp-based build pipeline for client code, extensions, and native modules.", "files": ["package.json"], "markdown": "# Build System\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [package.json](package.json)\n</cite>\n\nVS Code uses a Gulp-based build pipeline orchestrated through npm scripts. The system handles compilation, bundling, and packaging of the editor core, extensions, CLI tools, and web variants. Build tasks are executed via Gulp 4 with TypeScript support and distributed across parallel streams using npm-run-all2 for efficient builds.\n\n## Build Targets\n\nThe build system manages multiple distinct targets:\n\n- **Client**: The main VS Code editor application compiled from TypeScript sources\n- **Extensions**: Built-in and bundled extensions, including the Copilot extension\n- **CLI**: Command-line interface tools compiled from Rust sources\n- **Web**: Browser-based variant of VS Code\n- **Electron**: Native desktop application runtime integration\n\nEach target has dedicated compilation and watch tasks, allowing developers to build specific components without rebuilding the entire codebase.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [package.json:L34-L91](package.json#L34-L91)\n\n## Core Build Commands\n\nThe primary build entry point is the Gulp runner configured to maximize memory allocation for large TypeScript compilations. The main compile task chains multiple sub-tasks:\n\n| Task | Purpose |\n|------|---------|\n| `compile` | Compiles client and Copilot extension in parallel |\n| `compile-client` | Builds TypeScript sources to JavaScript |\n| `compile-extensions` | Bundles all built-in extensions |\n| `compile-web` | Packages the web variant |\n| `compile-cli` | Builds CLI tools from Rust |\n| `build-fast` | Optimized incremental build using build/next/build-fast.ts |\n\nWatch mode enables live development by monitoring file changes and automatically recompiling affected modules:\n\n```\nwatch-client-transpile        TypeScript transpilation with file watching\nwatch-extensions              Built-in extension compilation monitoring\nwatch-web                     Web variant rebuild on source changes\nwatch-copilot                 Copilot extension live rebuild\n```\n\n**Section sources**\n- [package.json:L33-L65](package.json#L33-L65)\n- [package.json:L87-L91](package.json#L87-L91)\n\n## Gulp Plugin Stack\n\nThe build system relies on specialized Gulp plugins for processing different asset types:\n\n| Plugin | Function |\n|--------|----------|\n| `gulp-azure-storage` | Upload build artifacts to Azure storage |\n| `gulp-bom` | Byte order mark handling |\n| `gulp-filter` | Stream filtering and conditional processing |\n| `gulp-gzip` / `gulp-gunzip` | Compression/decompression |\n| `gulp-json-editor` | JSON file manipulation |\n| `gulp-plumber` | Error handling and stream recovery |\n| `gulp-rename` | File renaming during pipeline |\n| `gulp-replace` | Text replacement in streams |\n| `gulp-sourcemaps` | Source map generation for debugging |\n| `@vscode/gulp-electron` | Electron application packaging |\n| `@vscode/gulp-vinyl-zip` | ZIP archive creation |\n\nThe `asar` package creates Electron app archives for distribution, while `rcedit` modifies Windows executable resources.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [package.json:L233-L244](package.json#L233-L244)\n- [package.json:L212-L213](package.json#L212-L213)\n- [package.json:L221-L285](package.json#L221-L285)\n- [package.json:L260-L285](package.json#L260-L285)\n\n## Configuration and Optimization\n\nBuild performance is optimized through resource allocation and parallel execution. The Gulp process runs with an 8GB heap limit to handle large TypeScript compilations, invoked via `node --experimental-strip-types --max-old-space-size=8192`. Parallel task execution uses npm-run-all2 (`-lp` flags) to compile non-blocking streams simultaneously.\n\nFast builds bypass full recompilation through targeted incremental compilation via `build/next/build-fast.ts`, suitable for rapid iteration during development. The extended build process includes extension media processing, codicon copying, and comprehensive hygiene checks.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [package.json:L69-L285](package.json#L69-L285)\n- [package.json:L254-L285](package.json#L254-L285)\n- [package.json:L36-L37](package.json#L36-L37)\n\n## Development Workflow\n\nThe build system supports layered development patterns:\n\n- **Incremental compilation** (`compile-client`) rebuilds only changed TypeScript modules\n- **Live watching** (`watch-*` tasks) maintains running transpilers for instant feedback\n- **Daemon modes** (`watchd`, `watch-extensionsd`) run watch tasks as background processes with start/restart/kill controls\n- **Deemon process manager** integrates automated restarts on crashes\n\nPre-commit hygiene checks enforce code quality via linting and validation scripts before commits proceed. Build validation includes cyclic dependency detection, TypeScript configuration verification, and schema compliance testing.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [package.json:L43-L67](package.json#L43-L67)\n- [package.json:L68-L285](package.json#L68-L285)\n- [package.json:L30-L285](package.json#L30-L285)\n- [package.json:L82-L285](package.json#L82-L285)\n"}, {"path": "development/build-tools.md", "title": "Build Tools", "parent": "development/development.md", "description": "Custom build utilities for compilation, bundling, optimization, and artifact generation.", "files": [], "markdown": "# Build Tools\n\nVS Code uses multiple build systems and utilities to compile, bundle, and package components across its Rust-based CLI, TypeScript extensions, and native modules.\n\n## Rust Build System\n\nThe CLI and Copilot Shim components use Rust's standard build tooling. Each Rust project contains a `build.rs` script in its root (e.g., `cli/build.rs` and `copilot_shim/build.rs`) for custom compilation steps, alongside `Cargo.toml` manifests that define dependencies and build configuration. Code formatting is enforced through `rustfmt.toml` files in each Rust workspace, ensuring consistent style across the codebase.\n\n## JavaScript Bundling with ESBuild\n\nExtensions use ESBuild for JavaScript and TypeScript bundling. The `extensions/configuration-editing/` extension defines build configuration in `esbuild.mts` for Node.js targets and `esbuild.browser.mts` for browser-based bundles. This two-target approach allows the same extension source to run in different environments with appropriate optimizations.\n\n## TypeScript Compilation\n\nTypeScript projects are configured via `tsconfig.json` and `tsconfig.browser.json` files throughout the codebase. Browser-specific configurations, like those in `extensions/configuration-editing/` and `extensions/copilot/chat-lib/`, separate browser and Node.js compilation targets. Build scripts reference these configurations to produce optimized output for each deployment context.\n\n## Extension Build Scripts\n\nCustom build automation lives in extension `script/` directories. The `extensions/copilot/script/` directory contains TypeScript build orchestration files including setup utilities (`setup/createVenv.mts`, `setup/getEnv.mts`) and testing infrastructure (`test/` directory with `.spec.ts` files). Post-installation scripts (`postinstall.ts`) handle dependency setup after npm package installation.\n\n## Build Configuration and Localization\n\nExtensions define build metadata in `package.json` and `package.nls.json` files. The `.nls.json` files enable localization of extension strings during build time. Schema files in directories like `extensions/configuration-editing/schemas/` validate configuration at build time, ensuring correctness of generated schemas like `devContainer.vscode.schema.json`.\n\n## Build Validation and Testing\n\nBuild test infrastructure appears in multiple extension directories, with `tsconfig.browser.json` files and test configuration files (e.g., `vitest.config.ts` in `extensions/copilot/chat-lib/`) enabling build output validation. License and dependency manifests (`cgmanifest.json`, `cglicenses.json`) are maintained across repositories to track third-party components included in builds.\n"}, {"path": "development/development-workflow.md", "title": "Development Workflow", "parent": "development/development.md", "description": "Setup instructions, compilation process, testing procedures, and debugging techniques.", "files": ["CONTRIBUTING.md", "package.json"], "markdown": "# Development Workflow\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [package.json](package.json)\n</cite>\n\n## Setup and Installation\n\nVS Code uses pnpm as its package manager. Begin by installing pnpm version 11 or newer globally, then install dependencies at the repository root with `pnpm install`. The pnpm version is pinned in the `packageManager` field of `package.json` to ensure consistency; pnpm will download and use that version automatically on first run. Installation with npm or yarn is not supported.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L93-L98](CONTRIBUTING.md#L93-L98)\n- [package.json:L4-L285](package.json#L4-L285)\n\n## Compilation Process\n\nThe build process manages compilation of the client and Copilot extension through npm scripts. The main `compile` script runs both client and Copilot compilation in parallel using `npm-run-all2`:\n\n```\npnpm run compile\n```\n\nIndividual targets can be compiled separately:\n- `pnpm run compile-client` — compiles the VS Code client via Gulp\n- `pnpm run compile-copilot` — compiles the Copilot extension\n- `pnpm run build-fast` — provides incremental builds using the next-generation build system\n\nFor development with live recompilation, watch mode scripts automatically rebuild on file changes:\n- `pnpm run watch` — watches client, extensions, and Copilot code in parallel\n- `pnpm run watch-client` — watches client code only\n- `pnpm run watch-extensions` — watches extension code only\n- `pnpm run watch-copilot` — watches Copilot extension code only\n\n**Section sources**\n- [package.json:L33-L67](package.json#L33-L67)\n\n## Type Checking\n\nType checking is performed on the client codebase using TypeScript:\n\n```\npnpm run typecheck-client\n```\n\nThis runs the TypeScript compiler against `./src/tsconfig.json` with `--noEmit` to check for type errors without producing compiled output.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [package.json:L38-L285](package.json#L38-L285)\n\n## Testing Procedures\n\nVS Code includes multiple testing frameworks for different test scopes:\n\n| Test Script | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `pnpm run test-browser` | Browser-based unit tests using Playwright |\n| `pnpm run test-node` | Node.js unit tests using Mocha with `--delay --ui=tdd` |\n| `pnpm run test-extension` | Extension tests via vscode-test |\n| `pnpm run smoketest` | End-to-end smoke tests with pre-launch compilation |\n| `pnpm run test-agent-host-e2e` | Agent host integration tests |\n\nBrowser tests install Playwright automatically; use `test-browser-no-install` to skip installation. Smoke tests compile test code before execution via the `build/lib/preLaunch.ts` script.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [package.json:L22-L29](package.json#L22-L29)\n- [package.json:L75-L76](package.json#L75-L76)\n\n## Code Quality and Validation\n\nValidation scripts maintain code quality and prevent structural issues:\n\n- `pnpm run hygiene` — code style and structure enforcement\n- `pnpm run oxlint` — Oxlint linter for code quality\n- `pnpm run stylelint` — CSS and style validation\n- `pnpm run valid-layers-check` — verifies proper layer organization\n- `pnpm run check-cyclic-dependencies` — detects circular dependencies in compiled output\n\nRunning these validation scripts regularly helps maintain high code quality standards.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [package.json:L82-L285](package.json#L82-L285)\n- [package.json:L93-L98](package.json#L93-L98)\n"}, {"path": "development/debugging.md", "title": "Debugging", "parent": "development/development.md", "description": "Techniques for debugging VS Code processes, extensions, and individual components.", "files": ["CONTRIBUTING.md"], "markdown": "# Debugging\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md)\n</cite>\n\n## Extension Isolation\n\nOne of the first steps in debugging VS Code is determining whether an issue is caused by the application itself or by an installed extension. VS Code can be run with all extensions disabled to isolate the problem. After disabling extensions, attempt to recreate the issue using the same steps that produced the original problem. If the issue no longer occurs with extensions disabled, the problem originates in one of your installed extensions rather than the core application.\n\nOnce you have identified that an extension is responsible, you should file the issue on that extension's repository directly rather than in the main VS Code repository. This ensures the extension maintainers can address the problem appropriately.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L28-L28](CONTRIBUTING.md#L28-L28)\n\n## Developer Tools Console\n\nVS Code provides a Developer Tools console that displays detailed error messages and diagnostic information about the application's runtime behavior. The console can be accessed from the application menu by selecting **Help > Toggle Developer Tools**.\n\nError output from the Developer Tools console should be included when reporting issues, as it often contains stack traces and error details that help developers understand what went wrong. When submitting a bug report, provide any relevant console errors that appeared when you encountered the issue.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L51-L63](CONTRIBUTING.md#L51-L63)\n\n## Issue Reporting Tools\n\nVS Code includes a built-in reporting tool accessible via **Report Issue** in the Help menu. This tool automates the collection of diagnostic information needed for effective bug investigation, including your VS Code version, operating system, installed extensions, and system information. The tool also searches existing issues to help prevent duplicate reports.\n\nWhen an issue cannot be reproduced immediately, developers will request additional information. Including comprehensive details—such as reproducible steps, expected behavior, actual behavior, screenshots, and error console output—significantly increases the likelihood of identifying and resolving the problem.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L51-L63](CONTRIBUTING.md#L51-L63)\n"}, {"path": "development/code-organization.md", "title": "Code Organization", "parent": "development/development.md", "description": "Project structure, module organization, dependency management, and architectural patterns.", "files": ["src/tsconfig.json", "package.json", "src/bootstrap-import.ts", "src/bootstrap-server.ts", "src/mainImpl.ts", "src/typings/base-common.d.ts", "src/typings/copilot-api.d.ts", "src/typings/crypto.d.ts", "src/typings/css.d.ts", "src/typings/editContext.d.ts"], "markdown": "# Code Organization\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [src/tsconfig.json](src/tsconfig.json)\n- [package.json](package.json)\n- [src/bootstrap-import.ts](src/bootstrap-import.ts)\n- [src/bootstrap-server.ts](src/bootstrap-server.ts)\n- [src/mainImpl.ts](src/mainImpl.ts)\n- [src/typings/base-common.d.ts](src/typings/base-common.d.ts)\n- [src/typings/copilot-api.d.ts](src/typings/copilot-api.d.ts)\n- [src/typings/crypto.d.ts](src/typings/crypto.d.ts)\n- [src/typings/css.d.ts](src/typings/css.d.ts)\n- [src/typings/editContext.d.ts](src/typings/editContext.d.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## Project Structure\n\nVS Code is organized as a monorepo with multiple layers: a Rust-based CLI, Electron-based desktop app, web server components, and built-in extensions. The main codebase is TypeScript, compiled to the `out/vs` directory. The repository uses pnpm as its package manager, with version 12.16.0 required for workspace consistency.\n\nThe source tree divides into functional areas: `src/` contains TypeScript application code and type definitions; `cli/` implements the VS Code command-line interface in Rust; `copilot_shim/` provides the Copilot integration layer; `extensions/` includes built-in language support and tools; and `build/` holds the build system infrastructure.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [package.json:L2-L4](package.json#L2-L4)\n\n## TypeScript Configuration and Compilation\n\nVS Code uses TypeScript 6 with configurations defined in `src/tsconfig.json`. The main compilation targets ES modules with specific compatibility flags: `esModuleInterop` enables CommonJS/ESM interoperability, `isolatedModules` is disabled for cross-module type checking, and `skipLibCheck` avoids expensive type checking of declaration files. The source maps are disabled for production builds, and the TypeScript compiler outputs to `../out/vs`.\n\nSecurity analysis is integrated through the tsec plugin, scanning for unsafe patterns in the codebase. Type definitions include WebGPU types, test framework types (Mocha, Sinon), and platform-specific types for Windows registry and other native APIs. The compilation includes strict type checking with `preserveConstEnums` enabled to ensure compile-time constant folding.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [src/tsconfig.json:L1-L39](src/tsconfig.json#L1-L39)\n\n## Dependency Management\n\nVS Code's dependency footprint reflects a mature desktop application with complex requirements. The project specifies version 1.148.0 and maintains both production and development dependencies through pnpm.\n\nProduction dependencies include the Electron runtime (43.8.10), Microsoft dev tunnels libraries for remote session support, xterm packages for terminal emulation, and @vscode-namespaced packages for internal utilities. Additional dependencies cover language detection (`@vscode/vscode-languagedetection`), terminal support (`node-pty`, `@xterm/xterm`), cryptography (`libsodium-wrappers`, `@hpke/core`), and storage (`@vscode/sqlite3`).\n\nDevelopment dependencies emphasize build tooling (Gulp, Rspack), testing (Playwright, Mocha), and code quality (TypeScript, Oxlint at version 1.90.0, Stylelint). The project declares itself private and uses a monorepo structure suitable for coordinated testing and builds across multiple components.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [package.json:L2-L4](package.json#L2-L4)\n- [package.json:L113-L182](package.json#L113-L182)\n\n## Bootstrap and Module Resolution\n\nVS Code uses a custom module resolution layer to handle dependencies when running from source. The `src/bootstrap-import.ts` module implements ESM hooks that redirect imports to the correct node_modules entries, particularly useful for running code outside the standard installation path.\n\nThe resolver discovers each dependency's entry point by examining package.json exports, preferring `exports[\".\"].import` for ES modules before falling back to the `main` field or assuming `index.js`. It detects module format by extension (`.mjs` signals ESM, `.cjs` signals CommonJS) or by reading the nearest `package.json` type field. This handles complex cases like pnpm's symlinked packages and packages with conditional exports containing nested format specifications.\n\nThe main Electron process begins in `src/mainImpl.ts`, which orchestrates initialization before `app.ready`. It parses command-line arguments, configures Electron and Chromium switches, establishes crash reporting, and sets up the user data path. The process reads `argv.json` configuration synchronously to allow early platform-specific setup. Only after the Electron app signals ready does the ESM bootstrap execute, loading the main application bundle.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [src/bootstrap-import.ts:L38-L104](src/bootstrap-import.ts#L38-L104)\n- [src/mainImpl.ts:L217-L249](src/mainImpl.ts#L217-L249)\n- [src/mainImpl.ts:L251-L397](src/mainImpl.ts#L251-L397)\n\n## Type Definitions and API Stubs\n\nThe repository includes ambient type declarations for APIs absent from standard TypeScript libraries or required for special compatibility. `src/typings/base-common.d.ts` declares cross-platform timing interfaces (`requestIdleCallback`, `setInterval`, `setTimeout`) and error handling used throughout the common utilities layer.\n\n`src/typings/copilot-api.d.ts` provides type stubs for the @vscode/copilot-api package, since that package uses extensionless relative imports incompatible with strict module resolution. It defines the CAPI client, extension information, request types, and model capability structures needed for Copilot integration.\n\nAdditional type stubs handle CSS module imports, Web Crypto APIs, and platform-specific interfaces like Windows registry and the EditContext API for advanced text editing. These declarations ensure consistent typing across the application without tight coupling to external type packages.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [src/typings/base-common.d.ts:L1-L40](src/typings/base-common.d.ts#L1-L40)\n- [src/typings/copilot-api.d.ts:L1-L50](src/typings/copilot-api.d.ts#L1-L50)\n- [src/typings/css.d.ts:L1-L9](src/typings/css.d.ts#L1-L9)\n"}, {"path": "development/security.md", "title": "Security Practices", "parent": "development/development.md", "description": "Security considerations, vulnerability handling, and secure coding guidelines.", "files": ["SECURITY.md", "CONTRIBUTING.md"], "markdown": "# Security Practices\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md)\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md)\n</cite>\n\n## Vulnerability Reporting\n\nSecurity vulnerabilities in VS Code must be reported through Microsoft's coordinated disclosure process rather than public GitHub issues. The official security reporting guidance, locations, contact information, and policies are maintained at [https://aka.ms/SECURITY.md](https://aka.ms/SECURITY.md). This approach ensures vulnerabilities are handled responsibly before public disclosure, allowing the team to develop and deploy fixes while protecting users from active exploitation.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [SECURITY.md:L1-L14](SECURITY.md#L1-L14)\n\n## Issue Reporting and Validation\n\nWhen reporting bugs or requesting features, contributors should follow established guidelines to ensure security-relevant issues are properly categorized and handled. The issue reporting workflow includes validating problems by reproducing them after disabling all extensions, which helps isolate whether issues originate from VS Code core or third-party code. For issues reproducibly caused by extensions, reporters should file issues directly in the extension's repository rather than the main VS Code repository.\n\nBefore opening a new issue, contributors must search the open issue repository to verify the problem or feature request has not already been filed. This prevents duplicate reports and helps maintain a focused backlog. Including sufficient reproduction details—such as the VS Code version, operating system, installed extensions list, step-by-step reproduction steps, and console error output—enables the development team to triage and prioritize security-related issues effectively.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L20-L77](CONTRIBUTING.md#L20-L77)\n\n## Automated Issue Management\n\nGitHub Actions automate parts of the issue management workflow, including closing issues marked `info-needed` after seven days without response and locking closed issues after 45 days. These automated processes help maintain repository health while ensuring that security-related issues receive appropriate attention according to the team's triage policies. The specific Actions and their descriptions are available in the vscode-github-triage-actions repository.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L83-L91](CONTRIBUTING.md#L83-L91)\n\n## Repository Security Architecture\n\nThe codebase reflects security-conscious design across multiple components. Authentication functionality is centralized in `cli/src/auth.rs`, agent certificate handling appears in `cli/src/commands/agent_certificate.rs`, and agent discovery and endpoint management are located in dedicated command modules. The Rust-based CLI and Copilot shim components, found in the `cli/` and `copilot_shim/` directories, use type-safe languages and dependency management through Cargo with pinned versions in `Cargo.toml` files. Additional security documentation is maintained in `extensions/copilot/SECURITY.md` for extension-specific practices.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L97-L97](CONTRIBUTING.md#L97-L97)\n"}, {"path": "development/performance.md", "title": "Performance Optimization", "parent": "development/development.md", "description": "Performance profiling, optimization strategies, and best practices for responsiveness.", "files": ["src/vs/base/common/performance.ts", "src/bootstrap-esm.ts", "src/bootstrap-meta.ts", "src/cli.ts", "src/server-cli.ts", "src/vs/amdX.ts", "src/vs/base/browser/animatedValue.ts", "src/vs/base/browser/animationSync.ts", "src/vs/base/browser/broadcast.ts", "src/vs/base/browser/browser.ts", "src/vs/base/browser/canIUse.ts", "src/vs/base/browser/contextmenu.ts"], "markdown": "# Performance Optimization\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [src/vs/base/common/performance.ts](src/vs/base/common/performance.ts)\n- [src/bootstrap-esm.ts](src/bootstrap-esm.ts)\n- [src/bootstrap-meta.ts](src/bootstrap-meta.ts)\n- [src/cli.ts](src/cli.ts)\n- [src/server-cli.ts](src/server-cli.ts)\n- [src/vs/amdX.ts](src/vs/amdX.ts)\n- [src/vs/base/browser/animatedValue.ts](src/vs/base/browser/animatedValue.ts)\n- [src/vs/base/browser/animationSync.ts](src/vs/base/browser/animationSync.ts)\n- [src/vs/base/browser/broadcast.ts](src/vs/base/browser/broadcast.ts)\n- [src/vs/base/browser/browser.ts](src/vs/base/browser/browser.ts)\n- [src/vs/base/browser/canIUse.ts](src/vs/base/browser/canIUse.ts)\n- [src/vs/base/browser/contextmenu.ts](src/vs/base/browser/contextmenu.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## Performance Profiling and Marking\n\nVS Code implements a cross-platform performance marking system through [`src/vs/base/common/performance.ts`](src/vs/base/common/performance.ts) that works consistently across browser, Node.js, and worker environments. The system provides three core functions:\n\n- `mark(name: string, markOptions?: { startTime?: number })` — Records a named performance mark at a specific time point\n- `getMarks()` — Retrieves all marks sorted by `startTime`, returning an array of `PerformanceMark` objects with `name` and `startTime` properties\n- `clearMarks(name?: string)` — Clears specific marks by name or all marks if no name is provided\n\nThe implementation automatically detects the runtime environment and selects the appropriate backend. In browsers with native `performance.mark` support, it delegates directly. For Safari and web workers lacking `timeOrigin`, it falls back to a `Date.now`-based polyfill. In Node.js environments, it uses the `perf_hooks` API while normalizing the `timeOrigin` reference point.\n\nMarks are instantiated with:\n\n```typescript\n// Browser or any environment\nperformance.mark('code/timeOrigin', { startTime: timeOrigin });\nperformance.mark('code/willLoadNls');\n// ... operation ...\nperformance.mark('code/didLoadNls');\n```\n\n**Section sources**\n- [src/vs/base/common/performance.ts:L8-L149](src/vs/base/common/performance.ts#L8-L149)\n\n## Startup Performance with ESM Bootstrapping\n\nThe ESM bootstrap loader in [`src/bootstrap-esm.ts`](src/bootstrap-esm.ts) implements several optimization strategies to reduce startup latency:\n\n**Module Resolution Caching** — The ESM hook system maintains a `resolutionCache` that stores module resolution results keyed by `[specifier, parentPath, context.conditions, importAttributes]`. This prevents redundant lookups for frequently imported dependencies within the same resolution chain.\n\n**ASAR Archive Optimization** — On Electron, the resolver prioritizes local `node_modules` for dependencies bundled by specific importers (e.g., built-in extensions) before consulting the application's `node_modules.asar` archive. This two-level resolution strategy avoids archive lookups for locally-bundled packages while maintaining support for shared application dependencies.\n\n**NLS Loading Profiling** — Internationalization message file loading is instrumented with performance marks:\n\n```typescript\nperformance.mark('code/willLoadNls');\n// ... load NLS configuration and messages ...\nperformance.mark('code/didLoadNls');\n```\n\nThe system only loads NLS messages when not in development mode and when a messages file is configured, deferring expensive JSON parsing until necessary.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [src/bootstrap-esm.ts:L88-L207](src/bootstrap-esm.ts#L88-L207)\n- [src/bootstrap-esm.ts:L227-L282](src/bootstrap-esm.ts#L227-L282)\n\n## Animation Performance Optimization\n\nVS Code optimizes animation responsiveness through two complementary mechanisms in [`src/vs/base/browser/animatedValue.ts`](src/vs/base/browser/animatedValue.ts) and [`src/vs/base/browser/animationSync.ts`](src/vs/base/browser/animationSync.ts).\n\n**Frame-Synced Animations** — The `AnimationFrameScheduler` batches animation updates to the browser's animation frame cycle. When multiple animated values need updates, they invalidate together on a single `requestAnimationFrame` call rather than scheduling independent updates:\n\n```typescript\ninvalidateOnNextAnimationFrame(reader: IReader | undefined): void {\n    if (!this._isScheduled) {\n        this._isScheduled = true;\n        getActiveWindow().requestAnimationFrame(() => {\n            this._isScheduled = false;\n            this._update();\n        });\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis prevents jank from excessive synchronous layout recalculations.\n\n**CSS Animation Phase Alignment** — The `synchronizeCSSAnimations()` function improves visual performance by phase-aligning identical CSS animations across multiple elements. By anchoring each animation's `startTime` to `0` via the Web Animations API, animations of the same duration display the same frame at the same time regardless of their individual start order:\n\n```typescript\nanimation.startTime = 0;\n```\n\nThis reduces perceived stutter in sequences like spinner dots and cascading transitions, as all elements remain globally in-phase even when individual `animation-delay` offsets differ.\n\n**Visibility-Based Animation Pausing** — The `pauseCSSAnimationsWhenHidden()` function pauses animations when elements leave the viewport or the document is hidden, eliminating unnecessary GPU and CPU cycles for invisible content. It uses `IntersectionObserver` for efficient visibility tracking and respects temporary DOM detachment by maintaining animation state across reattachments.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [src/vs/base/browser/animatedValue.ts:L118-L139](src/vs/base/browser/animatedValue.ts#L118-L139)\n- [src/vs/base/browser/animationSync.ts:L25-L73](src/vs/base/browser/animationSync.ts#L25-L73)\n- [src/vs/base/browser/animationSync.ts:L96-L213](src/vs/base/browser/animationSync.ts#L96-L213)\n\n## Module Loading Efficiency\n\nThe AMD module importer in [`src/vs/amdX.ts`](src/vs/amdX.ts) optimizes module resolution through caching and environment-specific loading strategies.\n\n**Resolution Caching** — Modules are cached by their path (`nodeModulePath`) to prevent redundant lookups:\n\n```typescript\nconst cache = new Map<string, Promise<any>>();\nif (cache.has(nodeModulePath)) {\n    return cache.get(nodeModulePath)!;\n}\n```\n\nThe cache persists across the application lifecycle, so frequently imported modules (e.g., `vscode-textmate`) are retrieved synchronously on subsequent loads.\n\n**Environment-Aware Loading** — The importer detects whether code runs in a browser, web worker, or Node.js context and applies the appropriate loading mechanism. Browser contexts load scripts asynchronously with error handling; Node.js contexts use `vm.Script` for synchronous execution with ASAR-aware `fs` access; web workers use dynamic `import()` statements.\n\n**Trusted Types Policy** — Script URLs are validated through a TrustedTypesPolicy to prevent injection attacks, with policies created per-environment to enforce origin-scoped or archive-scoped URL allowlists.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [src/vs/amdX.ts:L220-L252](src/vs/amdX.ts#L220-L252)\n- [src/vs/amdX.ts:L102-L217](src/vs/amdX.ts#L102-L217)\n\n## Cross-Tab Communication with Fallback\n\nThe `BroadcastDataChannel` in [`src/vs/base/browser/broadcast.ts`](src/vs/base/browser/broadcast.ts) minimizes performance overhead for multi-window scenarios by selecting the most efficient transport:\n\n**Primary Path: BroadcastChannel API** — When available, uses the native `BroadcastChannel` for direct inter-document communication without serialization overhead.\n\n**Fallback: localStorage** — On unsupported browsers, falls back to localStorage-based signaling. Each message is serialized to JSON once and stored, triggering a `storage` event on other tabs. The fallback removes the previous key before setting a new one to ensure events fire even when consecutive messages are identical.\n\nBoth mechanisms share a common event interface (`onDidReceiveData`), making the transport choice transparent to consumers while ensuring broad browser compatibility without sacrificing performance on modern platforms.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [src/vs/base/browser/broadcast.ts:L11-L70](src/vs/base/browser/broadcast.ts#L11-L70)\n"}, {"path": "testing/unit-testing.md", "title": "Unit Testing", "parent": "testing/testing.md", "description": "Unit test framework, test execution, test organization, and testing utilities.", "files": ["test/unit/node/index.js", "test/unit/browser/index.js", "test/componentFixtures/cssOrderBisect.mts", "test/componentFixtures/cssOrderScan.mts"], "markdown": "# Unit Testing\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [test/unit/node/index.js](test/unit/node/index.js)\n- [test/unit/browser/index.js](test/unit/browser/index.js)\n- [test/componentFixtures/cssOrderBisect.mts](test/componentFixtures/cssOrderBisect.mts)\n- [test/componentFixtures/cssOrderScan.mts](test/componentFixtures/cssOrderScan.mts)\n</cite>\n\n## Overview\n\nVS Code's unit testing infrastructure uses the Mocha framework in two distinct environments: Node.js for backend and core utilities, and browser-based testing via Playwright for UI components. Test files follow the naming convention `*.test.js` and are located within `test/` directories organized by environment. The testing system supports parallel execution, automatic test reruns to detect flaky tests, code coverage reporting, and filtered test selection via grep patterns.\n\n## Node.js Unit Tests\n\nNode.js tests execute against compiled JavaScript in the `out/` or `out-build/` directories. The runner loads and executes test modules sequentially or in parallel using worker processes. A required Node.js version check verifies compatibility before tests run. Default timeout is 5 seconds locally and 30 seconds in CI environments.\n\nThe test loader uses dynamic `import()` to load modules and emits Mocha lifecycle events (`EVENT_FILE_PRE_REQUIRE`, `EVENT_FILE_REQUIRE`, `EVENT_FILE_POST_REQUIRE`) for each test file. Global test utilities are exposed via `globalThis` for file operations during testing, including `__readFileInTests`, `__writeFileInTests`, `__readDirInTests`, `__unlinkInTests`, and `__mkdirPInTests`. Node.js diagnostic reports are configured to write crash dumps to `.build/crashes/` for investigation.\n\nCertain test files are excluded from Node.js execution, including browser-only tests (`electron-browser`, `electron-main`, `electron-utility`), native module tests that depend on Electron compilation (`nativeModules.test.js`), and flaky test suites (e.g., `vs/workbench/contrib/testing/test/**`).\n\n**Section sources:**\n- [test/unit/node/index.js:L1-L100](test/unit/node/index.js#L1-L100)\n- [test/unit/node/index.js:L148-L174](test/unit/node/index.js#L148-L174)\n\n## Browser Unit Tests\n\nBrowser tests run via Playwright against chromium, firefox, and webkit by default. The runner launches browsers, loads a test page (`test/unit/browser/renderer.html`), and receives test events through exposed functions. A stall watchdog monitors test progress and aborts with diagnostics if no progress is reported within a timeout period (default 300 seconds), preventing silent hangs in CI.\n\nTest modules are discovered via glob patterns and can be filtered by filename or regex. The browser runner collects test events including pass, fail, hook execution, and pending state. Failed tests and hooks are tracked with their file location and parent suite information for selective rerun. Isolation checks can optionally be run to detect state pollution between tests, with findings written to `VSCODE_TEST_ISOLATION_REPORT` if set.\n\n**Section sources:**\n- [test/unit/browser/index.js:L49-L102](test/unit/browser/index.js#L49-L102)\n- [test/unit/browser/index.js:L355-L406](test/unit/browser/index.js#L355-L406)\n- [test/unit/browser/index.js:L522-L567](test/unit/browser/index.js#L522-L567)\n\n## Test Execution Options\n\nBoth Node.js and browser runners accept common command-line flags:\n\n| Option | Purpose |\n|--------|---------|\n| `--run <file>` | Execute a single test file |\n| `--runList <file>` | Execute tests listed in a file (one per line) |\n| `--grep <pattern>` | Filter tests by pattern |\n| `--timeout <ms>` | Override test timeout (alias: `-t`) |\n| `--build` | Load from `out-build` instead of `out` |\n| `--rerun-failed-files <count>` | Rerun failures up to N additional times in fresh processes |\n| `--workers <n>` | Distribute tests across N parallel processes |\n\nBrowser-specific options include `--debug` (disable headless mode), `--sequential` (run browsers serially), `--browser <list>` (select browsers), `--reporter <name>` (Mocha reporter), and `--stall-timeout <seconds>` (progress watchdog timeout).\n\n## Parallel Execution and Flaky Test Handling\n\nWhen `--workers` is specified, test files are distributed across worker processes. Each worker claims the test files it will run and reports results back to the main process. Failed tests are tracked with their full title, file location, type (test or hook), and parent suite. When failures exceed a threshold (50 targets), reruns switch from selecting individual tests by title to rerunning entire test files.\n\nRerun attempts run in fresh processes with the same runner configuration. The system tracks which failures pass in reruns, identifying flaky tests. Unattributed failures—those outside any test file, such as root-level hooks—prevent further reruns to avoid masking unrecoverable issues. Rerun results are reported with which tests passed on which attempt.\n\n## Component Fixture Testing\n\nComponent fixtures support specialized testing for CSS cascade-order dependencies. The `cssOrderScan.mts` scanner renders each fixture in normal and CSS-reversed order, flagging those whose appearance changes. For flagged fixtures, `cssOrderBisect.mts` performs binary search to localize which two CSS source files form the conflicting pair. Scan results are written to a Markdown report with baseline and reversed images for review.\n\nThe scanner can process fixtures in batches and enforces a time budget to bound total scan duration. When running in GitHub Actions, the report links to source files via GitHub web URLs.\n\n**Section sources:**\n- [test/componentFixtures/cssOrderScan.mts:L6-L27](test/componentFixtures/cssOrderScan.mts#L6-L27)\n- [test/componentFixtures/cssOrderBisect.mts:L6-L36](test/componentFixtures/cssOrderBisect.mts#L6-L36)\n"}, {"path": "testing/integration-testing.md", "title": "Integration Testing", "parent": "testing/testing.md", "description": "Integration test framework covering component interactions and system-level workflows.", "files": [], "markdown": "# Integration Testing\n\nVS Code's integration testing framework validates interactions between components, subsystems, and extensions in realistic scenarios. The test suite spans multiple technology stacks—Rust for the CLI and agent infrastructure, TypeScript for extensions—and uses targeted test locations to verify system-level workflows.\n\n## Test Organization\n\nVS Code organizes integration tests at the component level. The Rust-based CLI and agent infrastructure maintains tests in `cli/tests/`, including `agent_tunnel.rs` for validating agent tunnel initialization and lifecycle. The copilot shim layer, which bridges VS Code and third-party integrations, houses tests in `copilot_shim/tests/` including `app.rs`, `pty.rs`, and `native_policy.rs`. Extension integration tests live alongside their source in directories like `extensions/configuration-editing/src/test/`, following the TypeScript convention of colocating test files with implementation.\n\n## Component Integration Testing\n\nThe Rust subsystems verify component interactions through focused integration tests. The CLI's `agent_tunnel.rs` test exercises agent discovery, connection lifecycle, and tunnel operations. The copilot shim's test suite validates pseudoterminal (PTY) handling across platforms—`pty.rs` and `conpty.rs` test terminal emulation and Windows console integration respectively. These tests run against the compiled binaries rather than mocking internal state, ensuring that OS-level interactions (file locking, process management, signal handling in `service_linux.rs`, `service_macos.rs`, `service_windows.rs`) work correctly.\n\n## Extension Integration Testing\n\nTypeScript extensions use a testing pattern that validates configuration handling and editor integration. The `extensions/configuration-editing/src/test/` directory contains test suites like `completion.test.ts` and `importExportProfiles.test.ts`, which verify that extensions correctly parse, validate, and apply VS Code settings. These tests interact with the editor's document model and language servers to confirm end-to-end workflows.\n\n## Test Fixtures and Mocks\n\nIntegration tests rely on fixtures to simulate realistic environments without external dependencies. The copilot shim provides test fixtures in `copilot_shim/tests/fixtures/legacy/`, including shell wrappers (`git-bash-wrapper.sh`, `posix-launcher.sh`) and command wrappers (`npm-wrapper.cmd`, `npm-wrapper.ps1`, `windows-bootstrapper.ps1`). These fixtures allow tests to verify that VS Code correctly launches and manages external processes across Windows, macOS, and Linux.\n\n## Cross-Platform Validation\n\nIntegration tests account for platform-specific behaviors. The service layer (`service.rs` and platform-specific implementations) is tested through scenarios that exercise Windows registry interactions (`agent_host_registry_windows.rs`), system service lifecycle (service registration, startup, teardown), and idle timeout handling. Terminal and process management tests validate PTY behavior on Unix-like systems and console host procedures on Windows, ensuring consistent user experience across platforms.\n"}, {"path": "testing/e2e-testing.md", "title": "End-to-End Testing", "parent": "testing/testing.md", "description": "E2E test framework and test scenarios simulating complete user workflows.", "files": [], "markdown": "# End-to-End Testing\n\nVS Code maintains end-to-end test suites across multiple components of the codebase, testing complete workflows from CLI invocation through extension behavior and tunneling functionality. E2E tests validate critical paths in both the Rust-based CLI and TypeScript-based extensions.\n\n## Test Structure\n\nThe repository organizes E2E tests alongside their respective components:\n\n- **CLI tests** (`cli/tests/`) — Rust-based integration tests for command-line functionality, including agent tunneling and remote access features.\n- **Copilot shim tests** (`copilot_shim/tests/`) — Tests for PTY (pseudo-terminal) and native policy integration, covering ConPTY on Windows and standard PTY on Unix systems.\n- **Extension tests** — Distributed throughout extension directories (e.g., `extensions/configuration-editing/src/test/`, `extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/vscode-node/test/`) with TypeScript test files (`*.spec.ts`, `*.test.ts`).\n\n## Rust-Based Testing\n\nThe CLI layer includes end-to-end tests in `cli/tests/agent_tunnel.rs` that validate the agent tunneling protocol and control-plane connectivity. These tests exercise the full initialization, authentication, and session management flows without mocking underlying services.\n\nThe copilot shim includes terminal integration tests (`copilot_shim/tests/pty.rs`, `conpty.rs`) that validate PTY behavior on different platforms. The shim's native policy handling is tested in `native_policy.rs`, exercising Windows registry operations and Unix permission models.\n\n## TypeScript Testing Infrastructure\n\nExtensions use TypeScript-based E2E patterns with test configuration in `tsconfig.json` and build tooling via `esbuild.mts`. The copilot extension's chat library includes comprehensive test suites (`copilot/chat-lib/vitest.config.ts`) for agent coordination, language model integrations, and chat session lifecycle.\n\nAgent providers are tested through spec files like `askAgentProvider.spec.ts`, `planAgentProvider.spec.ts`, and `exploreAgentProvider.spec.ts`, validating the full flow from user queries through agent execution. BYOK (bring-your-own-key) providers have dedicated E2E coverage testing cloud model integrations (`azureOpenAIEndpoint.spec.ts`, `openAIEndpoint.spec.ts`, `geminiNativeProvider.spec.ts`).\n\n## Configuration and Execution\n\nBuild and test configuration is centralized in root-level files:\n\n- `eslint.config.js` — Linting rules applied during test validation.\n- Root `tsconfig.json` and extension-specific TypeScript configurations ensure type safety across test suites.\n\nThe project structure indicates tests are executed as part of CI/CD validation, with test scripts defined in `package.json` files throughout extensions and the copilot module.\n\n## Scenario Coverage\n\nE2E tests cover:\n\n- **CLI workflows** — Agent tunnel establishment, authentication flows, and session management.\n- **Terminal operations** — PTY creation, signal handling, and cross-platform compatibility (Windows ConPTY vs. Unix PTY).\n- **Agent coordination** — Multi-agent chat scenarios, language model switching, and context-aware suggestions.\n- **Extension initialization** — Configuration editing, authentication, and workspace integration.\n"}, {"path": "testing/browser-testing.md", "title": "Browser Testing", "parent": "testing/testing.md", "description": "Browser-based testing infrastructure with Playwright and headless testing support.", "files": ["test/unit/browser/index.js", "test/automation/src/activityBar.ts", "test/automation/src/agentsWindow.ts", "test/automation/src/application.ts", "test/automation/src/chat.ts"], "markdown": "# Browser Testing\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [test/unit/browser/index.js](test/unit/browser/index.js)\n- [test/automation/src/activityBar.ts](test/automation/src/activityBar.ts)\n- [test/automation/src/agentsWindow.ts](test/automation/src/agentsWindow.ts)\n- [test/automation/src/application.ts](test/automation/src/application.ts)\n- [test/automation/src/chat.ts](test/automation/src/chat.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## Overview\n\nVS Code's browser testing infrastructure provides end-to-end testing capabilities using Playwright, supporting headless browser execution across multiple browser engines. The system combines unit tests run in browser contexts with automation-based UI testing, enabling comprehensive validation of browser-based functionality and integration scenarios.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [test/unit/browser/index.js:L1-L27](test/unit/browser/index.js#L1-L27)\n\n## Unit Tests with Playwright\n\nThe core browser test runner resides in `test/unit/browser/index.js` and uses Playwright as the test automation framework. By default, tests execute in three browsers: chromium, firefox, and webkit. Each test module is discovered via glob patterns from the compiled output directory and executed within a browser context using Mocha for test orchestration.\n\nThe runner supports both headless and headed (debug) modes. In headless mode, tests run without visual UI, optimizing for CI/CD pipelines. Debug mode launches browsers with devtools enabled, allowing interactive inspection of test failures. The system serves test files through a local HTTP server and handles test progress reporting, console output capture, and test result aggregation.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [test/unit/browser/index.js:L49-L81](test/unit/browser/index.js#L49-L81)\n- [test/unit/browser/index.js:L355-L375](test/unit/browser/index.js#L355-L375)\n\n## Running Tests\n\nTests are launched via the `index.js` runner with command-line options controlling scope, browser selection, and execution mode:\n\n| Option | Purpose |\n|--------|---------|\n| `--run <path>` | Run only tests in specified file |\n| `--grep, -g, -f <pattern>` | Filter tests by pattern |\n| `--runGlob, --glob <pattern>` | Glob pattern for test discovery |\n| `--browser <browser>` | Select browsers (chromium, firefox, webkit) |\n| `--debug` | Disable headless mode and enable devtools |\n| `--sequential` | Run test suites serially (one browser at a time) |\n| `--workers <count>` | Run tests in parallel worker processes |\n| `--build` | Use compiled output from `out-build` instead of `out` |\n| `--stall-timeout <seconds>` | Abort with diagnostics if no test progress for N seconds (default: 300) |\n\n**Section sources**\n- [test/unit/browser/index.js:L49-L100](test/unit/browser/index.js#L49-L100)\n\n## Test Isolation and Progress Monitoring\n\nThe runner implements test isolation checks and progress monitoring to detect and diagnose stalled test runs. A stall watchdog monitors test progress at regular intervals; if no events occur within the configured timeout, the runner collects diagnostics including page readiness, in-flight test information, and browser process details before aborting.\n\nIsolation findings are written to a JSON lines file specified by the `VSCODE_TEST_ISOLATION_REPORT` environment variable. Each finding includes the browser type and isolation violation details, enabling post-run analysis of test state leakage or cleanup failures. The runner also supports a rerun mechanism for failed tests, optionally retrying entire test files in a fresh browser instance.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [test/unit/browser/index.js:L315-L343](test/unit/browser/index.js#L315-L343)\n- [test/unit/browser/index.js:L522-L567](test/unit/browser/index.js#L522-L567)\n\n## Parallel Execution and Worker Distribution\n\nFor large test suites, the `--workers` flag distributes test files across multiple worker processes, each launching its own browser instance. Worker count is bounded by available CPU cores and system memory constraints. The runner tracks which test files each worker claims via a claims-based distribution system, ensuring even load balancing and preventing duplicate test execution.\n\nReruns of failed tests use a single browser instance regardless of worker configuration. The system distinguishes between failures at the test level and unattributed failures (such as root-level hooks), enabling targeted rerun strategies.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [test/unit/browser/index.js:L111-L124](test/unit/browser/index.js#L111-L124)\n- [test/unit/browser/index.js:L408-L422](test/unit/browser/index.js#L408-L422)\n\n## UI Automation Testing\n\nBeyond unit tests, automation helpers in `test/automation/src/` provide high-level UI testing abstractions built on Playwright. Classes like `Application`, `Workbench`, `Chat`, and `AgentsWindow` encapsulate common UI interactions and state queries. The `Code` class manages browser context and page navigation, while helper classes expose domain-specific operations such as opening chat views, interacting with the activity bar, and managing session windows.\n\nAutomation tests use Playwright locators and element queries to verify UI presence and state. For example, the `agentsWindow` module waits for specific DOM classes (`.agent-sessions-workbench`, `.sessions-chat-widget`) and uses polling with configurable retry counts to handle asynchronous UI updates.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [test/automation/src/application.ts:L23-L40](test/automation/src/application.ts#L23-L40)\n- [test/automation/src/agentsWindow.ts:L55-L122](test/automation/src/agentsWindow.ts#L55-L122)\n- [test/automation/src/chat.ts:L50-L93](test/automation/src/chat.ts#L50-L93)\n"}, {"path": "testing/extension-testing.md", "title": "Extension Testing", "parent": "testing/testing.md", "description": "Testing framework for VS Code extensions including test discovery and execution.", "files": [], "markdown": "# Extension Testing\n\nVS Code extensions include automated test suites to validate functionality. Tests are organized within extension directories and use TypeScript as the primary language.\n\n## Test Organization\n\nExtensions in the repository follow a consistent pattern for organizing tests. Test files are placed in `test/` directories alongside source code, maintaining parallel structure to the implementation. For example, the `configuration-editing` extension organizes tests in `extensions/configuration-editing/src/test/` with test files like `completion.test.ts` and `importExportProfiles.test.ts`.\n\nThe `copilot` extension demonstrates extensive testing across multiple layers, with test directories throughout `src/extension/` covering components like agents, authentication, chat features, and language models. Test files use the `.spec.ts` extension in many cases, particularly in nested feature directories.\n\n## Test File Naming and Conventions\n\nTest files follow naming conventions that indicate their purpose:\n- `.test.ts` extension for unit and integration tests\n- `.spec.ts` extension for specification-based tests\n- Entry point files like `index.ts` can serve as test coordinators\n\nThe `copilot` extension includes test files such as:\n- `src/extension/agents/vscode-node/test/askAgentProvider.spec.ts`\n- `src/extension/byok/vscode-node/test/anthropicProvider.spec.ts`\n- `src/extension/chat/vscode-node/test/chatHookService.spec.ts`\n\n## Test Framework Configuration\n\nExtensions use Vitest as a test runner, as evidenced by configuration files like `copilot/chat-lib/vitest.config.ts`. TypeScript configuration files (`tsconfig.json`) are present in extension directories to ensure proper type checking during test execution.\n\n## Build and Test Integration\n\nBuild configuration files including `esbuild.mts` and `esbuild.browser.mts` in extension directories manage the compilation of both source code and tests. Extensions typically have separate build configurations for Node.js and browser environments, allowing tests to validate both contexts. TypeScript compilation is configured through `tsconfig.json` and `tsconfig.browser.json` files to support type-safe test code.\n\n## Test Snapshots\n\nThe `copilot` extension includes test snapshot files stored in `__snapshots__/` directories, such as those in `src/extension/byok/common/test/`. These snapshots enable regression testing for complex data transformations and message conversions between different AI provider formats.\n\n## Extension Testing Scope\n\nTests cover critical extension functionality including:\n- Configuration editing and validation\n- Language model provider implementations (Anthropic, OpenAI, Azure, Gemini)\n- Chat and agent functionality\n- Authentication flows\n- Settings and profile management\n- Import and export operations.\n"}, {"path": "build/build-process.md", "title": "Build Process", "parent": "build/build.md", "description": "Complete pipeline from source compilation to production-ready distributable artifacts.", "files": [], "markdown": "# Build Process\n\nVS Code's build system consists of multiple pipelines for different components: Rust-based CLI tools, TypeScript extensions, and the main application. Each component has distinct build configurations and outputs that together produce the complete distributable.\n\n## Rust CLI and Components\n\nThe repository includes Rust-based tools managed through Cargo. The `cli/` directory contains the main CLI tool with its own `Cargo.toml` and `build.rs` configuration, alongside source modules for commands, tunnels, and utilities. The `copilot_shim/` directory similarly contains Rust code with a dedicated `Cargo.toml` and `build.rs` for building the Copilot integration layer. Both directories include comprehensive test suites and are configured for platform-specific compilation (Windows, macOS, Linux) with conditional modules like `nosleep_windows.rs`, `nosleep_macos.rs`, and `nosleep_linux.rs`.\n\n## TypeScript and JavaScript Extension Builds\n\nVS Code includes numerous bundled language extensions in the `extensions/` directory, each with its own `package.json` and build configuration. Extensions use different build strategies: some leverage esbuild (visible in `extensions/configuration-editing/esbuild.mts` and `esbuild.browser.mts`), while others may use simpler TypeScript compilation via `tsconfig.json` files. Each extension directory contains its own schema definitions, snippets, syntax files, and test suites, allowing parallel or independent builds.\n\n## Build Tool Configuration\n\nBuild configurations across the repository include ESLint (`eslint.config.js`) for code quality and TypeScript configurations for type checking. The `extensions/configuration-editing/` extension demonstrates a typical web extension pattern with separate browser and Node.js build targets (`tsconfig.browser.json` and `tsconfig.json`). Extensions also include schema files for validation and language-specific resources like `.tmLanguage.json` syntax definitions.\n\n## Compilation and Validation\n\nThe repository uses Rust's build.rs scripts in `cli/build.rs` and `copilot_shim/build.rs` for pre-compilation steps specific to each Rust component. TypeScript compilation is handled through tsconfig files at the extension and sub-module level. Source code includes platform-specific implementations, suggesting conditional compilation or runtime platform detection to generate appropriate binaries for Windows, macOS, and Linux targets.\n\n## Testing Infrastructure\n\nTest files are distributed throughout the codebase: `cli/tests/` contains Rust integration tests, while extensions include TypeScript test files (`.spec.ts` and `.test.ts`) alongside source code. The `copilot_shim/tests/` directory includes fixture files and platform-specific tests for PTY and native functionality, indicating comprehensive validation before release.\n\n## Output Artifacts\n\nThe build process produces multiple artifact types: compiled Rust binaries for CLI and Copilot Shim components, bundled extension JavaScript and type definitions, and potentially packaged distributables. The presence of update mechanisms (suggested by `cli/src/self_update.rs` and `cli/src/update_service.rs`) indicates the build system supports staged rollouts and version management for production deployments.\n"}, {"path": "build/distribution.md", "title": "Distribution", "parent": "build/build.md", "description": "Packaging and distribution for Windows, macOS, and Linux platforms including installers.", "files": [], "markdown": "# Distribution\n\nVS Code's distribution architecture supports multiple platforms and package formats, enabling installation across Windows, macOS, and Linux systems. The build system generates platform-specific installers and packages configured through gulpfiles and platform-specific build scripts.\n\n## Windows Installers\n\nWindows distribution uses **Inno Setup** to generate system and user-scoped installers for x64 and ARM64 architectures. The build process, defined in `build/gulpfile.vscode.win32.ts`, compiles installer scripts (`.iss` files) located in `build/win32/code.iss` using the ISCC.exe compiler.\n\nInno Setup generates two installation targets per architecture:\n\n- **System installation**: Creates entries in `HKLM` registry and applies to all users. Uses application IDs like `win32x64AppId` or `win32arm64AppId` from `product.json`.\n- **User installation**: Creates entries in `HKCU` registry for the current user. Uses distinct application IDs (`win32x64UserAppId`, `win32arm64UserAppId`) to prevent conflicts.\n\nFor stable and insider builds, the system also generates AppX packages (`.appx` files) for Windows Store integration, with Explorer context menu integration available through `win32ContextMenu` registry configuration. The installer includes the Copilot shim (`copilot.exe`) when present. Build output is signed using the `build/azure-pipelines/common/sign-win32.ts` signing utility, and the system manages updates through `inno_updater.exe`.\n\n## Linux Packages\n\nLinux distribution spans three package formats and multiple architectures (x64, armhf, arm64), configured in `build/gulpfile.vscode.linux.ts`.\n\n**Debian Packages** (`.deb` files) are generated for Ubuntu and Debian systems. The build process prepares package metadata including control files, pre/post-installation scripts (`prerm`, `postinst`, `postrm`), and desktop integration files from `resources/linux/debian/`. Dependencies are automatically resolved and injected into the control metadata. Packages are built using `fakeroot dpkg-deb`.\n\n**RPM Packages** (`.rpm` files) target Fedora, CentOS, and related distributions. Build configuration uses spec file templates from `resources/linux/rpm/code.spec.template`, with binary stripping controlled by the `$STRIP` environment variable. RPM packages are generated through `rpmbuild` with architecture mapping (x64 → x86_64, armhf → armv7hl, arm64 → aarch64).\n\n**Snap Packages** (`.snap` files) provide containerized distribution via Ubuntu's Snapcraft. Build configuration uses `resources/linux/snap/snapcraft.yaml`, and packages are generated by running the `snapcraft` command. All Linux packages integrate desktop files (`code.desktop`), shell completions (bash and zsh), MIME type associations, and application metadata (`code.appdata.xml`).\n\n## macOS Distribution\n\nmacOS distribution creates DMG (Disk Image) files via the `build/darwin/create-dmg.ts` script, which manages the **dmgbuild** Python tool. The build process automatically installs Python 3.10+ via Homebrew if necessary (checking standard locations first, then installing Python 3.12). It creates themed background images for different build qualities: stable, insider, and exploration builds use distinct visual presentations.\n\nThe DMG build process packages `.app` bundles for both Intel x64 and ARM64 (Apple Silicon) architectures into single distributable volumes. The final DMG includes code signing support and uses background images and disk icons specified in `resources/darwin/code.icns`. Architecture-specific builds are created separately and stored as `VSCode-darwin-{arch}.dmg`.\n\n## Common Build Infrastructure\n\nAll platforms use `build/gulpfile.vscode.ts` for core build coordination. Version information comes from `package.json` and is enhanced with build-time commit metadata via `build/lib/getVersion.ts`. Packages embed quality metadata (dev, stable, insider) which controls update channels and feature flags. Source maps are conditionally stripped during packaging (controlled by CI environment detection), and checksums are computed for critical files including preload scripts, workbench bundles, and HTML entry points.\n\nThe packaging system handles platform-specific built-in extensions through inclusion/exclusion filters, ensuring only compatible extensions are bundled. Cross-platform paths are managed through the `util.getVersionedResourcesFolder()` function, which supports versioned resource layouts for staged deployments.\n"}, {"path": "build/native-components.md", "title": "Native Components", "parent": "build/build.md", "description": "Native modules, system integration, and cross-platform native features.", "files": ["cli/Cargo.toml", "cli/build.rs", "cli/tests/agent_tunnel.rs"], "markdown": "# Native Components\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [cli/Cargo.toml](cli/Cargo.toml)\n- [cli/build.rs](cli/build.rs)\n- [cli/tests/agent_tunnel.rs](cli/tests/agent_tunnel.rs)\n</cite>\n\n## Cross-Platform Native Security\n\nVS Code's CLI integrates native security infrastructure for credential storage and secure communication. The [`keyring` dependency](cli/Cargo.toml#L37) provides platform-specific secure credential management, with feature flags for each operating system: `platform-windows`, `platform-macos`, and Linux variants (`linux-secret-service-rt-tokio-crypto-openssl`, `linux-keyutils`).\n\nNetwork communication uses `reqwest` with the `native-tls` feature, delegating TLS certificate validation to the operating system's native SSL/TLS stack rather than bundling a certificate store. This ensures the CLI respects system-level certificate policies and corporate SSL inspection tools.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/Cargo.toml:L37-L38](cli/Cargo.toml#L37-L38)\n- [cli/Cargo.toml:L18-L95](cli/Cargo.toml#L18-L95)\n\n## Platform-Specific System Integration\n\nVS Code maintains distinct native integrations for Windows, macOS, and Linux to leverage platform capabilities.\n\n**Windows** uses the Win32 API layer via three complementary crates:\n- [`winapi`](cli/Cargo.toml#L72) for lower-level Win32 function bindings\n- [`windows-sys`](cli/Cargo.toml#L73) for structured Win32 definitions (Foundation, Security, Console, Threading, WindowsProgramming, UI input)\n- [`winreg`](cli/Cargo.toml#L71) for Windows Registry access\n\n**macOS** integrates with the system through [`core-foundation`](cli/Cargo.toml#L76-L77), providing access to Core Foundation APIs for system-level services.\n\n**Linux** uses [`zbus`](cli/Cargo.toml#L79) with Tokio runtime support, enabling communication with the system D-Bus daemon for integration with desktop environments and system services.\n\nThe CLI also depends on [`sysinfo`](cli/Cargo.toml#L24) for cross-platform system discovery and [`gethostname`](cli/Cargo.toml#L34) for retrieving the machine name.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/Cargo.toml:L70-L79](cli/Cargo.toml#L70-L79)\n\n## Build-Time Native Configuration\n\nThe build script ([`cli/build.rs`](cli/build.rs)) performs platform-specific compilation and configuration steps.\n\nOn Windows, the build system compiles resource files containing version information, product metadata, and application identity using [`winresource`](cli/build.rs#L205-L216). This embeds file version, product version, company name, and copyright information directly into the executable, making these properties visible in Windows Explorer and system tools.\n\nFor debug builds on Windows, the build script adjusts the main thread stack size from 1 MB to 8 MB to match Unix defaults ([`cli/build.rs:L35-L40`](cli/build.rs#L35-L40)). This prevents stack overflow during async runtime initialization in unoptimized builds where nested async state machines remain expanded.\n\nThe build process also validates copyright file headers across all Rust source files and loads product configuration from `product.json` and optional `product.overrides.json` files, translating them into build-time environment variables.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/build.rs:L169-L216](cli/build.rs#L169-L216)\n- [cli/build.rs:L35-L40](cli/build.rs#L35-L40)\n\n## Terminal and Process Management\n\nThe CLI manages native process spawning and terminal interaction through the standard library and async runtime. Tests in [`cli/tests/agent_tunnel.rs`](cli/tests/agent_tunnel.rs) demonstrate spawning CLI binaries with native process handles, including `Stdio` stream redirection. The test infrastructure uses `tokio::process::Command` to launch child processes and `BufReader` for asynchronous output stream consumption, verifying that tunnel initialization completes without panics even under error conditions like inaccessible lock files.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/tests/agent_tunnel.rs:L22-L32](cli/tests/agent_tunnel.rs#L22-L32)\n"}, {"path": "architecture/architecture.md", "title": "Architecture Overview", "parent": null, "description": "High-level architectural design covering the multi-process model, extension system, workbench, and service layers.", "files": ["AGENTS.md"], "markdown": "# Architecture Overview\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md)\n</cite>\n\nVS Code is organized around a multi-layered architecture that separates concerns across process boundaries, extension systems, and core service layers. The platform is designed to support extensibility while maintaining performance and stability through well-defined interfaces and controlled communication patterns.\n\n## Core Architecture Layers\n\nVS Code's architecture consists of several key layers working together:\n\n- **Process and Runtime Model**: VS Code runs multiple processes with controlled communication patterns. The main process hosts the workbench UI, while additional processes handle extensions, language services, and development tools. The system uses `CancellationToken` and `CancellationTokenSource` for internal asynchronous operations, adapting to `AbortSignal` only at native or third-party API boundaries.\n\n- **Extension System**: The extension architecture allows third-party code to extend functionality safely through well-defined APIs and isolated execution contexts. Extensions integrate with the core platform without direct access to internal implementation details.\n\n- **Workbench and Editor**: The workbench provides the user interface shell containing panels, sidebars, and the editor. The editor core handles text rendering, editing operations, and document management.\n\n- **Language and Debug Services**: Specialized services provide language intelligence through the language services layer and debugging capabilities through the debug architecture. These services run in separate processes for isolation and performance.\n\n- **Configuration and Theming**: Global systems manage user preferences, workspace settings, and visual theming across the entire platform.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [AGENTS.md:L1-L7](AGENTS.md#L1-L7)\n\n## Child Pages\n\nDetailed architecture documentation is organized by component:\n\n- [Process and Runtime Model](process-model.md)\n- [Extension System](extension-system.md)\n- [Workbench Architecture](workbench.md)\n- [Editor Core](editor-core.md)\n- [Language Services](language-services.md)\n- [Command System](command-system.md)\n- [Configuration System](configuration-system.md)\n- [Debug Architecture](debugging.md)\n- [Theming System](theming.md)\n"}, {"path": "cli/cli.md", "title": "CLI Overview", "parent": null, "description": "The VS Code command-line interface written in Rust, providing tunneling, remote access, and desktop integration.", "files": ["cli/Cargo.toml", "cli/src/lib.rs", "cli/CONTRIBUTING.md"], "markdown": "# CLI Overview\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [cli/Cargo.toml](cli/Cargo.toml)\n- [cli/src/lib.rs](cli/src/lib.rs)\n- [cli/CONTRIBUTING.md](cli/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n</cite>\n\nThe VS Code CLI is a Rust-based command-line interface providing remote access, dev tunnels, and agent host functionality. Built on the tokio async runtime, it enables secure remote access to VS Code Server, establishes dev tunnels for collaborative development, and deploys headless agent hosts for CI/CD and GitHub Environments integration.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe CLI is structured as a modular Rust application (`code-cli`), exposing core modules through [cli/src/lib.rs](cli/src/lib.rs) including authentication, commands, desktop integration, tunneling, and state management. It manages both transient CLI operations and long-running background services using async patterns for networking, file I/O, and system integration.\n\nKey dependencies provide:\n- **CLI Parsing**: clap for structured command-line argument handling and environment variable binding\n- **Networking**: reqwest and hyper for HTTP operations, with Microsoft's dev-tunnels library for secure tunnel establishment\n- **Authentication**: keyring integration for cross-platform credential persistence\n- **Platform Support**: Windows, macOS, and Linux with platform-specific implementations for services and system integration\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/Cargo.toml:L2-L60](cli/Cargo.toml#L2-L60)\n- [cli/src/lib.rs:L1-L27](cli/src/lib.rs#L1-L27)\n\n## Core Features\n\nThe CLI provides four primary capabilities: establishing dev tunnels to expose VS Code Server remotely, deploying agent hosts for headless environments, integrating with desktop VS Code installations, and providing utility commands for version management and self-updates.\n\nTunnel functionality supports credentials persistence and session correlation through request headers, while the agent host system enables registration with GitHub Environments and resource isolation through certificate-based sandbox security. Services can run in the background or attached to the foreground terminal, with support for both interactive prompts and non-interactive modes via `--accept-server-license-terms`.\n\nThe CLI includes certificate management for headless sandbox environments, supporting creation, trust installation, status checking, and removal operations across Windows, macOS, and Linux platforms.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/CONTRIBUTING.md:L33-L82](cli/CONTRIBUTING.md#L33-L82)\n\n## Documentation\n\nExplore the VS Code CLI through these focused guides:\n\n- **[CLI Commands](commands.md)** – Complete reference for all command-line operations and argument syntax\n- **[Dev Tunnels](tunneling.md)** – Secure remote access and tunnel configuration\n- **[Agent Host](agent-host.md)** – Headless agent deployment and management\n- **[Desktop Integration](desktop-integration.md)** – Integration with local VS Code installations\n- **[CLI Utilities](utilities.md)** – Maintenance, self-update, and utility command reference.\n"}, {"path": "extensions/extensions.md", "title": "Extensions", "parent": null, "description": "Overview of VS Code's extension ecosystem including builtin extensions, development patterns, and APIs.", "files": ["extensions/CONTRIBUTING.md", "extensions/cgmanifest.json"], "markdown": "# Extensions\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [extensions/CONTRIBUTING.md](extensions/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [extensions/cgmanifest.json](extensions/cgmanifest.json)\n</cite>\n\n## Built-In Extensions\n\nVS Code ships with built-in extensions housed in the `extensions/` directory. These extensions are developed with TypeScript and follow a consistent structure for maintainability. Each extension contains a `package.json` manifest, `src/` directory for TypeScript source code, `tsconfig.json` configuration, and an `esbuild.mts` build script for production compilation. Development builds output to `out/`, while production builds output to `dist/`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/CONTRIBUTING.md:L3-L19](extensions/CONTRIBUTING.md#L3-L19)\n\n## Desktop and Browser Support\n\nVS Code extensions can target both desktop and browser environments. Desktop is the default target. To support browsers, an extension requires a `browser` entry in `package.json` pointing to the browser bundle, along with a `tsconfig.browser.json` for type checking and `esbuild.browser.mts` build script configured with `platform: 'browser'`. Extensions that need environment-specific behavior can use separate entrypoints: `src/extension.ts` for desktop and `src/extension.browser.ts` for browser. Browser-based implementations must use only browser-safe APIs.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [extensions/CONTRIBUTING.md:L21-L31](extensions/CONTRIBUTING.md#L21-L31)\n\n## Extension Topics\n\nNavigate to detailed documentation for specific extension areas:\n\n- [Builtin Extensions](builtin-extensions.md)\n- [Copilot Extension](copilot-extension.md)\n- [Language Support Extensions](language-support.md)\n- [Extension API](api-reference.md)\n- [Debug Adapters](debugging-adapters.md)\n- [Configuration Editing](configuration-editing.md)\n- [Git Integration](git-integration.md)\n- [Themes and Icons](theme-and-icons.md)\n"}, {"path": "development/development.md", "title": "Development Guide", "parent": null, "description": "Development setup, build processes, testing workflows, and contribution guidelines.", "files": ["CONTRIBUTING.md", "package.json"], "markdown": "# Development Guide\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [package.json](package.json)\n</cite>\n\n## Getting Started\n\nVS Code is built using TypeScript and Node.js with pnpm as the package manager. Before contributing, install pnpm 11 or newer using `npm install -g pnpm`, then run `pnpm install` at the repository root. The project uses a pinned pnpm version (`pnpm@12.16.0` as defined in `package.json`) which pnpm automatically downloads on first use. Installing with npm or yarn is not supported.\n\nThe repository contains multiple build targets including the main client application, CLI tools written in Rust, and various extensions. The build system supports fast compilation modes and incremental watching for development iteration.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L97-L97](CONTRIBUTING.md#L97-L97)\n- [package.json:L4-L4](package.json#L4-L4)\n\n## Development Workflows & Tools\n\nThe development environment supports several key workflows:\n\n| Workflow | Purpose |\n|----------|---------|\n| **Compilation** | `pnpm run compile` for full build; `pnpm run build-fast` for rapid iteration |\n| **Watching** | `pnpm run watch` for live recompilation during development |\n| **Testing** | Unit tests via `pnpm run test-node` and `pnpm run test-browser`; extension tests via `pnpm run test-extension` |\n| **Type Checking** | `pnpm run typecheck-client` for TypeScript validation |\n| **Code Quality** | `pnpm run hygiene` for code standards and `pnpm run oxlint` for linting |\n\nThe project uses Gulp for build orchestration and Electron for the desktop runtime. Additional development scripts support web compilation, CLI building, and specialized testing for performance and agent host functionality.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [package.json:L33-L98](package.json#L33-L98)\n\n## Contribution Resources\n\nThe following guides provide detailed information for specific development tasks:\n\n- [Build System](build-system.md) — Build architecture and compilation process\n- [Build Tools](build-tools.md) — Tools and utilities for building\n- [Development Workflow](development-workflow.md) — Day-to-day development practices\n- [Debugging](debugging.md) — Debugging techniques and tools\n- [Code Organization](code-organization.md) — Project structure and module organization\n- [Security Practices](security.md) — Security guidelines for contributors\n- [Performance Optimization](performance.md) — Performance monitoring and optimization\n\nFor general contribution guidelines, see [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md).\n"}, {"path": "testing/testing.md", "title": "Testing Guide", "parent": null, "description": "Overview of testing infrastructure including unit, integration, and end-to-end testing.", "files": ["package.json"], "markdown": "# Testing Guide\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [package.json](package.json)\n</cite>\n\nVS Code maintains a comprehensive testing infrastructure spanning unit tests, integration tests, end-to-end tests, browser tests, and extension tests. This guide provides an overview of the testing systems and links to detailed documentation for each testing category.\n\n## Testing Infrastructure Overview\n\nVS Code's test suite is organized by execution environment and scope. The repository includes multiple test runners and frameworks configured in the package scripts, supporting both Node.js and browser-based testing environments. Tests are executed using industry-standard tools including Mocha for unit testing, Playwright for browser automation, and specialized test utilities for extensions.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [package.json:L20-L30](package.json#L20-L30)\n\n## Test Categories\n\nThe testing framework is divided into five primary categories:\n\n| Category | Purpose | Framework |\n|----------|---------|-----------|\n| [Unit Testing](unit-testing.md) | Isolated component and module testing | Mocha (Node.js), Playwright (browser) |\n| [Integration Testing](integration-testing.md) | Multi-component interaction verification | Node.js test runners |\n| [End-to-End Testing](e2e-testing.md) | Full workflow and system testing | Custom runners and scripts |\n| [Browser Testing](browser-testing.md) | Web and browser-specific functionality | Playwright |\n| [Extension Testing](extension-testing.md) | Extension development and compatibility | vscode-test |\n\nEach category has dedicated test suites and execution paths optimized for their specific testing scope. Browser tests use Playwright for cross-environment compatibility, while Node.js tests rely on Mocha's configuration for async handling and test organization.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [package.json:L20-L30](package.json#L20-L30)\n\n## Running Tests\n\nTest execution is configured through npm scripts in the root `package.json`. Common test commands include:\n\n- `pnpm run test-node` - Execute Node.js-based unit tests\n- `pnpm run test-browser` - Run browser environment tests with Playwright\n- `pnpm run test-extension` - Validate extension functionality\n- `pnpm run test-agent-host-e2e` - Execute agent host end-to-end tests\n\nEach test category has dedicated documentation covering setup, execution, and troubleshooting specific to that testing environment.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [package.json:L21-L29](package.json#L21-L29)\n"}, {"path": "modules/modules.md", "title": "Modules", "parent": null, "description": "Overview and navigation for the Modules section.", "files": [], "markdown": "# Modules\n\nVS Code's architecture is organized into distinct functional modules that handle specific concerns of the editor and workbench. Each module encapsulates related features, services, and integrations that work together to provide a cohesive development environment.\n\n## Module Overview\n\nThe VS Code codebase is structured around several core modules that manage different aspects of the application:\n\n- **Editor Module**: The text editing engine and document model that powers the core editing experience\n- **Workbench Services**: The application shell, layout management, and service registry that orchestrate editor and UI components\n- **Commands and Keybindings**: Command registration, execution, and keyboard shortcut mapping\n- **Settings Module**: User preferences, configuration storage, and settings editing\n- **Source Control**: Version control integration and SCM provider support\n- **Terminal Integration**: Integrated terminal functionality and process management\n- **Output Channel**: Diagnostic output and logging infrastructure\n- **Notifications**: User-facing notifications and status updates\n\n## Navigation\n\nExplore individual modules through the following pages:\n\n| Module | Purpose |\n|--------|---------|\n| [Editor Module](editor.md) | Text rendering, document model, and editing operations |\n| [Workbench Services](workbench.md) | Application shell, services, and layout management |\n| [Commands and Keybindings](commands-keybindings.md) | Command dispatch and keyboard shortcut handling |\n| [Settings Module](settings.md) | Configuration management and preferences |\n| [Source Control](scm.md) | Version control provider integration |\n| [Terminal Integration](terminal.md) | Terminal emulation and shell integration |\n| [Output Channel](output-channel.md) | Diagnostic output and logging |\n| [Notifications](notifications.md) | User notifications and alerts |\n\n## Architecture\n\nVS Code's module system relies on a dependency injection pattern through the service registry in Workbench Services. Modules export services that are consumed by other modules or directly by the UI layer. This layered architecture separates concerns and enables features to be developed, tested, and maintained independently while maintaining clear integration points.\n"}, {"path": "ai/ai.md", "title": "Ai", "parent": null, "description": "Overview and navigation for the Ai section.", "files": [], "markdown": "# Ai\n\nVS Code integrates AI capabilities through a modular architecture that supports multiple language models, agents, and chat interfaces. The AI system enables developers to interact with large language models directly within the editor, perform context-aware tasks, and extend functionality through customizable agents and providers.\n\n## Core Components\n\nThe AI implementation in VS Code consists of several interconnected systems:\n\n- **Chat Interface** (`extensions/copilot/src/extension/chat/`) provides the user-facing conversation experience with session management, hooks, and telemetry.\n- **Agent System** (`extensions/copilot/src/extension/agents/`) implements autonomous task runners including built-in providers for plan generation, code exploration, and asking questions.\n- **Language Model Providers** (`extensions/copilot/src/extension/byok/`) abstracts multiple model backends through a unified interface, supporting OpenAI, Azure OpenAI, Anthropic, Google Gemini, Ollama, and OpenRouter.\n- **Bring Your Own Key (BYOK)** allows users to configure custom model endpoints and use alternative providers with their own API credentials.\n- **Copilot Integration** (`extensions/copilot/`) ties these components together as the primary extension, managing authentication, chat sessions, and user interactions.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe system follows a provider-based architecture where language models are implemented as pluggable components:\n\n- Common abstractions in `extensions/copilot/src/extension/byok/common/` handle message conversion and provider interfaces\n- Node-specific implementations in `extensions/copilot/src/extension/byok/node/` define endpoints for various services\n- VS Code-specific integrations in `extensions/copilot/src/extension/byok/vscode-node/` implement the full provider lifecycle\n\nThis design enables adding new models and providers without modifying core chat or agent logic.\n\n## Navigation\n\nExplore the AI system through these sections:\n\n- [Copilot Integration](copilot-integration.md) — Main extension structure and initialization\n- [Chat Interface](chat-interface.md) — Conversation management and user interaction\n- [Language Model Providers](language-models.md) — Supported models and provider implementations\n- [Agent System](agents.md) — Autonomous agents and task execution\n- [BYOK Providers](byok.md) — Custom endpoint configuration and self-hosted models.\n"}, {"path": "build/build.md", "title": "Build", "parent": null, "description": "Overview and navigation for the Build section.", "files": ["cli/Cargo.toml"], "markdown": "# Build\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [cli/Cargo.toml](cli/Cargo.toml)\n</cite>\n\n## Overview\n\nVS Code's build system encompasses multiple components across different languages and platforms. The build infrastructure handles compilation of native code, CLI tools, extensions, and application artifacts. The system is configured to support cross-platform development with platform-specific optimizations and dependencies managed through language-specific build tools.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/Cargo.toml:L1-L10](cli/Cargo.toml#L1-L10)\n\n## Core Components\n\nThe VS Code build infrastructure consists of several key parts:\n\n- **CLI (Command-Line Interface)**: A Rust-based command-line tool that provides the `code` command. The CLI is configured with release-level optimizations including link-time optimization (LTO) and binary stripping to minimize size.\n- **Extensions**: TypeScript-based extensions built with esbuild, providing built-in functionality for syntax highlighting, language support, and core features.\n- **Copilot Integration**: Native components written in Rust for handling AI features and extensions.\n\nThe build system uses per-platform dependencies: Windows (`winreg`, `winapi`, `windows-sys`), macOS (`core-foundation`), and Linux (`zbus`) to handle platform-specific functionality.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [cli/Cargo.toml:L2-L5](cli/Cargo.toml#L2-L5)\n- [cli/Cargo.toml:L62-L79](cli/Cargo.toml#L62-L79)\n- [cli/Cargo.toml:L86-L90](cli/Cargo.toml#L86-L90)\n\n## Build Documentation\n\nLearn more about specific aspects of the build system:\n\n- [Build Process](build-process.md) — Build workflows and compilation procedures\n- [Distribution](distribution.md) — Packaging and distribution of VS Code artifacts\n- [Native Components](native-components.md) — Native code components and platform integrations\n\nRefer to these resources for detailed information on specific build workflows and components.\n"}]};
